E["npc:baine-bloodhoof"] = {t="npc",n="Baine Bloodhoof",z="mulgore",s="Baine Bloodhoof is the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof and the acting protector of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. While his father governs the tribes from the towering heights of Thunder Bluff, Baine directly oversees local defense, investigating encroaching industrial exploitation and defending against feral predators. He serves as an essential bridge between youthful tauren braves and the ancestral wisdom of his clan.",h="Baine Bloodhoof is the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof and the acting protector of Bloodhoof Village...",sec={{t="Son of the High Chieftain",b="Born to Cairne and the late Tamaala, Baine spent his youth traveling alongside his nomadic people before they claimed the rich grasslands of Mulgore. His early life was marked by the ever-present threat of the centaur clans. Shortly after the Third War, a centaur warband under Khiragg captured young Baine, plunging Cairne into a grief-stricken stupor that nearly shattered the newly founded settlements. It was only through the courage of the champion Rexxar that Baine was liberated from captivity. That rescue restored Cairne's spirit, allowing the tauren to unite with Thrall's Horde and secure their foothold on the continent.",sp=0},{t="Guardian of Bloodhoof Village",b="Rather than lingering in the relative comfort of the capital mesas, Baine chooses to reside among his kin in Bloodhoof Village. As Cairne advances in age, Baine carries an increasing burden of stewardship, representing the next generation of tauren leadership. He directs braves and visiting allies to combat the unruly Palemane gnolls who pester the borders, and keeps a watchful eye on suspicious outside influences threatening Mulgore's natural balance. Whenever Cairne departs the capital on state affairs, Baine steps forward as the acting leader for the united tribes.",sp=0},{t="Defending the Earth Mother",b="Beyond territorial border clashes, Baine faces modern encroachers unconcerned with spiritual balance. To the east, the goblin Venture Company cuts deep into the sacred landscape, while dwarven prospectors from Bael Modan dig into ancestral soil at the Bael'dun Digsite. Baine advocates for a firm yet measured response: attempting to warn the miners and destroy their destructive equipment rather than rushing into senseless bloodshed. He warns that the dwarves' violent digging is hurting the spirits of the earth, though the miners refuse to listen.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Baine Bloodhoof?",a="Baine is the only son of Cairne Bloodhoof, the High Chieftain of the tauren tribes. He oversees defense and tribal affairs in Bloodhoof Village, stepping in to direct local braves and protect Mulgore while his father rules from Thunder Bluff.",al={"baine lore","who is baine","cairnes son"}},{q="Where can Baine be found right now?",a="Baine stands in Bloodhoof Village, situated in central Mulgore just south of Thunder Bluff. He can be found near the town center organizing tasks for newly arrived adventurers.",al={"baine location","where is baine","baine bloodhoof spot"}},{q="How was Baine rescued from the centaur?",a="Following the Third War, a centaur leader named Khiragg captured Baine, plunging Cairne into debilitating despair. The beastmaster Rexxar sought out Bovan Windtotem's outcasts, tracked the centaur to their encampment, slew the captors, and safely returned Baine to his father.",al={"baine kidnapped","rexxar saves baine","baine captured"}},{q="What problems does Baine manage in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Baine coordinates efforts against the aggressive Palemane gnolls harassing local herds and keeps a close watch on foreign invaders, such as the goblin Venture Company and Alliance dwarven excavators disrupting the earth.",al={"baine quests","what does baine do","bloodhoof village troubles"}},{q="What is Baine's stance on the Bael'dun dwarven digsite?",a="Baine believes the violent blasting techniques of the Prospector Khazgorm and his miners harm the spirits of the earth. He seeks to sabotage their industrial tools and scare them off before their reckless digging brings elemental retribution upon the region.",al={"baine dwarves","bael dun mining","dwarf digsite mulgore"}},{q="Will Baine succeed Cairne as High Chieftain?",a="As Cairne's beloved son and right-hand advisor, Baine already acts as temporary leader whenever Cairne is away from Thunder Bluff. He is widely considered the natural heir to lead the united tauren tribes in the future.",al={"does baine become leader","future high chieftain","cairne heir"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Baine represents both the honorable lineage of High Chieftain Cairne and the living hope of the young tribes gathered across the plains of Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="Baine embodies the calm wisdom and quiet martial strength that anchors the tauren within Thrall's Horde, offering guidance to those passing through central Mulgore.",["class:Shaman"]="Baine respects the spirits above all else, sending travelers to silence dwarven machines and reckless miners before the fury of the earth elementals is unleashed."},kw={"baine","bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","cairne","centaur","khiragg","mulgore","palemane","rexxar","thunder bluff"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","subzone:bael-dun-digsite","topic:centaur","topic:tauren","topic:horde"},src={"Baine Bloodhoof"}}
E["npc:cairne-bloodhoof"] = {t="npc",n="Cairne Bloodhoof",z="mulgore",s="Cairne Bloodhoof is the venerable High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes and ruler of Thunder Bluff. A towering warrior of immense wisdom, he bound the fate of the tauren to Thrall's Horde after the orcs aided his people in escaping extinction at the hands of the centaur.",h="Cairne Bloodhoof is the venerable High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes and ruler of Thunder Bluff.",sec={{t="A Nation in Mulgore",b="For generations, the tauren lived as wandering nomads across the Barrens, continually hunted and driven toward extinction by savage centaur marauders. When Thrall and his orcs arrived on the shores of Kalimdor, Cairne recognized a rare nobility in them. Thrall offered military protection, escorting Cairne's desperate caravan across the plains to the safety of Mulgore in exchange for guidance to the Oracle at Stonetalon Peak. Settling upon the towering mesas of Thunder Bluff, Cairne finally established a permanent, unified home for all tauren tribes.",sp=0},{t="Pledge to the Horde",b="Indebted to Thrall, Cairne followed the orcs to Stonetalon Peak and fought side by side with the Horde and Jaina Proudmoore's humans against the Burning Legion during the Third War. Later, when Admiral Daelin Proudmoore invaded Durotar, Cairne personally took up his ancestral runespear once more to fight alongside Rexxar after his captured son, Baine, was safely recovered. Today, Cairne stands as one of the Horde's most respected elders and Thrall's closest confidant, offering calm council against reckless warmongering.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Cairne Bloodhoof?",a="Cairne Bloodhoof is the High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes, chieftain of the Bloodhoof tribe, and leader of Thunder Bluff. He is an ancient warrior whose leadership and strength led the tauren out of nomadic exile and into a secure homeland.",al={"who is cairne","tauren leader","high chieftain"}},{q="Why are the tauren allied with the orcs?",a="During the Third War, marauding centaur tribes were hunting the tauren to extinction. Thrall and his newly arrived orcs fought off the marauders and escorted Cairne's great caravan to Mulgore. In gratitude, Cairne pledged his people's loyalty and strength to Thrall's Horde.",al={"why tauren join horde","cairne thrall friendship","tauren orc alliance"}},{q="Where does Cairne reside?",a="He presides atop Thunder Bluff, the capital city built on the high mesas of Mulgore, ruling alongside the leaders of the various reunited tauren tribes.",al={"where is cairne","cairne location","find cairne bloodhoof"}},{q="Who is Baine Bloodhoof to Cairne?",a="Baine is Cairne's beloved son and heir. When Baine was abducted by centaur raiders, Cairne fell into deep grief, refusing to fight until Rexxar and his companions tracked Baine down and returned him safely home.",al={"cairne son","is baine cairne's son","baine bloodhoof relation"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Cairne is the savior of your people, having united the scattered tribes, repelled the centaur, and led the construction of Thunder Bluff.",["faction:Horde"]="Alongside Thrall, Cairne is a founding father of the modern Horde on Kalimdor, serving as a pillar of honor, wisdom, and military power."},kw={"baine","bloodhoof","cairne","centaur","high chieftain","mulgore","thrall","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:thrall","topic:tauren","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore","npc:baine-bloodhoof","topic:centaur","topic:horde","subzone:stonetalon-peak"},src={"Cairne Bloodhoof"}}
E["npc:chief-hawkwind"] = {t="npc",n="Chief Hawkwind",z="mulgore",s="Chief Hawkwind is the tribal leader of Camp Narache, the proving grounds nestled in the Red Cloud Mesa of Mulgore. Responsible for guiding the youth of the Shu'halo, he mentors new adventurers as they embark on their traditional rites of passage. Under his watchful gaze, young braves learn humility, respect for the land, and the strength needed to protect their people.",h="Chief Hawkwind is the tribal leader of Camp Narache, the proving grounds nestled in the Red Cloud Mesa of...",sec={{t="Chieftain of the Mesa",b="Standing at the heart of Camp Narache, Chief Hawkwind carries the solemn responsibility of preparing the next generation of tauren for the wider world. Red Cloud Mesa serves as a sheltered haven where fledgling hunters, shamans, druids, and warriors undertake the ancient Rites of the Earthmother. Hawkwind oversees these customs, ensuring that every youth learns duty and endurance before venturing beyond the safety of the mesa.",sp=0},{t="Guiding the Rites",b="Hawkwind's lessons begin with humility rather than martial glory. He sends initiates on simple chores, such as fetching clean spring water, to instill reverence for the natural world and their elders. Only after demonstrating a humble heart are braves guided toward tests of martial prowess, culminating in trials that test their resolve against the aggressive Bristleback quilboar encroaching upon the ancestral plains.",sp=0},{t="Defending Camp Narache",b="Beyond his role as a mentor, Chief Hawkwind must reckon with rising dangers right at his camp's doorstep. The Bristleback tribe has grown increasingly bold, pressing hard against the perimeter of Red Cloud Mesa. Hawkwind coordinates the defense of the settlement, tasking newly proven braves with blunting the quilboar assaults to ensure the sacred training grounds remain secure.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Chief Hawkwind?",a="Chief Hawkwind is the chieftain of Camp Narache in the Red Cloud Mesa. He oversees tribal matters in the area and mentors young tauren as they prepare to take their first steps into the wider world of Mulgore and Kalimdor.",al={"hawkwind","chief hawkwind lore","who is the chief at camp narache"}},{q="What are the Rites of the Earthmother?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother are a traditional series of sacred trials undertaken by young tauren coming of age. Guided by elders like Chief Hawkwind, these rites test humility, strength, courage, and spiritual connection to the land and the ancestors.",al={"tauren rites","rites of earthmother","what are the rites"}},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind have you fetch water first?",a="Hawkwind assigns 'A Humble Task' to remind young braves that service and respect for the community are as vital as martial strength. Gathering fresh water for tribal elders teaches patience and humility before initiates are sent into battle.",al={"a humble task","why fetch water","hawkwind water quest"}},{q="What threat is Camp Narache facing?",a="Camp Narache is under constant pressure from hostile Bristleback quilboar who inhabit the surrounding hills of Red Cloud Mesa. Chief Hawkwind must organize the defense of the mesa and send initiates to thin the quillboar ranks.",al={"narache threats","who is attacking camp narache","quilboar camp narache"}},{q="Where does Chief Hawkwind send adventurers next?",a="Once a young brave completes the preliminary rites on the mesa, Chief Hawkwind directs them down into the sprawling valleys of Mulgore, sending them toward Bloodhoof Village to continue their trials under the guidance of Cairne Bloodhoof's elders.",al={"where do i go after hawkwind","after camp narache","leaving red cloud mesa"}},{q="Is Chief Hawkwind related to Greatmother Hawkwind?",a="Yes, Greatmother Hawkwind is his mother. The Hawkwind family has long held deep roots and leadership roles among the tauren dwelling on Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"greatmother hawkwind relation","hawkwind family","who is greatmother hawkwind"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As the first chieftain to guide you on your journey, Chief Hawkwind instills the foundational values of the Shu'halo: reverence for the Earthmother, loyalty to the tribe, and humility in strength.",["faction:Horde"]="Hawkwind's teachings ensure that newly raised tauren braves step forward not just as capable warriors and spellcasters, but as steadfast and honorable allies to the young Horde."},kw={"a humble task","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rite of strength","rites of the earthmother","tauren chieftain"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","quest:763","quest:752","quest:757","quest:781","npc:greatmother-hawkwind"},src={"Chief Hawkwind"}}
E["npc:doomsayer-wiserunner"] = {t="npc",n="Doomsayer Wiserunner",z="mulgore",s="In the present era, this respected tauren elder is known as Seer Wiserunner, serving his people faithfully in the rolling hills of Mulgore. Stationed near the ancestral grounds, he aids young tauren braves as they undertake the traditional rites of passage that lead toward spiritual adulthood.",h="In the present era, this respected tauren elder is known as Seer Wiserunner, serving his people faithfully in...",sec={{t="A Guide on the Sacred Path",b="Seer Wiserunner stands among the gentle bluffs of Mulgore as an elder and mystic of the shu'halo. When young hunters and shamans complete their initial trials of strength and vision, they are sent to seek his counsel during their ancestral rites. He interprets visions of the Earth Mother and guides the youth on how to honor their forebears, offering the steady wisdom that binds the scattered nomadic tribes into a unified community.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is this NPC associated with the name 'Doomsayer Wiserunner'?",a="He is not known by that title in our present time. In 25 ADP, he is simply Seer Wiserunner, an honored tauren elder in good standing with the tribes of Mulgore. Whispers or rumors linking him to apocalyptic doomsaying do not reflect his current role guiding young initiates through sacred rites.",al={"doomsayer wiserunner name","why doomsayer","seer vs doomsayer"}},{q="What role does he play in Mulgore?",a="Seer Wiserunner serves as a spiritual mentor for young tauren. After braves embark on their early vision quests, he receives them to complete the traditional Rite of Wisdom, testing their readiness to serve both tribe and Earth Mother.",al={"what does wiserunner do","seer wiserunner purpose","wiserunner role"}},{q="Where can Seer Wiserunner be found?",a="He can be found watching over the hills of central Mulgore, situated to the north of the sacred ancestral grounds where tauren youth travel between their various spiritual trials.",al={"where is wiserunner","seer wiserunner location","wiserunner coords"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young shu'halo venturing beyond Red Cloud Mesa, Seer Wiserunner is an essential mentor on the ancestral journey toward spiritual maturity.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman will recognize Wiserunner as a keeper of ancient traditions who understands the messages woven through the earth and sky."},kw={"camp narache","doomsayer wiserunner","mulgore","rite of vision","rite of wisdom","seer wiserunner","tauren elder"},rel={"quest:773","quest:767","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Doomsayer Wiserunner"}}
E["npc:elder-bloodhoof"] = {t="npc",n="Elder Bloodhoof",z="mulgore",s="Elder Bloodhoof is an ancestral spirit found resting just north of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. Appearing prominently to travelers during the Lunar Festival, he stands as a revered guardian spirit of the Bloodhoof tribe.",h="Elder Bloodhoof is an ancestral spirit found resting just north of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Spirit of the Ancestors",b="Elder Bloodhoof represents the deep spiritual bond the tauren share with their lineage. Manifesting near Bloodhoof Village, this ghostly elder watches over his people and rewards respectful travelers who seek wisdom during the Lunar Festival. His presence reinforces the enduring guidance of deceased chieftains and tribal forebears, whose spirits remain intimately tied to the plains of Mulgore.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Elder Bloodhoof?",a="Elder Bloodhoof is a ghostly tauren spirit who appears just north of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. He represents the honored ancestors of the Bloodhoof tribe, offering blessings and ancient wisdom to travelers who visit him during seasonal traditions like the Lunar Festival.",al={"elder bloodhoof lore","who is the ghost outside bloodhoof village","elder bloodhoof identity"}},{q="When can you see Elder Bloodhoof?",a="Elder Bloodhoof primarily manifests during the annual Lunar Festival, where he acts as an elder spirit offering festival blessings.",al={"where is elder bloodhoof","elder bloodhoof missing","how to find elder bloodhoof"}},{q="What is his connection to Bloodhoof Village?",a="As an honored ancestor bearing the clan's name, Elder Bloodhoof watches over the ancestral settlement of Bloodhoof Village. Tauren tradition places deep significance on ancestral spirits lingering close to where their descendants live and gather.",al={"why is he near bloodhoof village","elder bloodhoof relation to cairne"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren pilgrims, paying respects to Elder Bloodhoof is a direct communion with tribal lineage, honoring the sacrifice of those who walked Mulgore long before the founding of Thunder Bluff.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman revere Elder Bloodhoof as a manifestation of ancestral reverence, demonstrating how ancestral spirits guide and protect their descendants in the physical realm."},kw={"ancestor","bloodhoof village","elder bloodhoof","lunar festival","mulgore","red rocks","tauren spirit"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:8673","zone:mulgore","subzone:red-rocks","topic:tauren"},src={"Elder Bloodhoof"}}
E["npc:gart-mistrunner"] = {t="npc",n="Gart Mistrunner",z="mulgore",s="Gart Mistrunner is the resident druid trainer stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa. He welcomes novice tauren called by the Earth Mother and guides them through their first rites of nature magic.",h="Gart Mistrunner is the resident druid trainer stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="Mentor of the Red Cloud Mesa",b="Standing watch over Camp Narache, Gart Mistrunner represents the ancient druidic path rekindled among the Shu'halo. New tauren druids are directed straight to Gart upon taking their first steps on the Red Cloud Mesa, often carrying a verdant note commending them into his care. Through his patient guidance, young practitioners learn to commune with the natural world, drawing upon the restorative powers of nature and the lunar radiance of Mu'sha to heal their allies and strike down threats to the land.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Gart Mistrunner?",a="Gart Mistrunner is a tauren druid trainer located at Camp Narache in the Red Cloud Mesa of Mulgore. He instructs novice tauren druids in their initial spells and rites.",al={"who is gart","gart mistrunner lore","gart mistrunner"}},{q="What spells does he teach newly awakened druids?",a="Gart introduces beginners to essential nature abilities, teaching early healing spells such as Healing Touch and offensive nature magic like Moonfire.",al={"what does gart teach","gart mistrunner training","druid spells camp narache"}},{q="What is his connection to the Mistrunner clan?",a="Mistrunner is simply Gart's family name. No lore describes a Mistrunner tribe or says more about his kin; he is known only as the druid trainer of Camp Narache.",al={"mistrunner clan","who are mistrunners"}}},ang={["class:Druid"]="For tauren druids, Gart Mistrunner is the very first master to shape their communion with the wilds and the gifts of the Earth Mother.",["race:Tauren"]="Gart represents the resurgence of the Shu'halo's ancient druidic traditions, passing ancestral wisdom to the youth of Camp Narache."},kw={"camp narache","druid trainer","gart mistrunner","mistrunner","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren druid"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:druid","topic:tauren","quest:3094"},src={"Gart Mistrunner"}}
E["npc:greatmother-hawkwind"] = {t="npc",n="Greatmother Hawkwind",z="mulgore",s="Greatmother Hawkwind is a revered tauren elder resting by the water well on Red Cloud Mesa. As the mother of Camp Narache's leader, Chief Hawkwind, she embodies the enduring spirit and communal responsibility of the tribe despite her advanced age.",h="Greatmother Hawkwind is a revered tauren elder resting by the water well on Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="Elder of the Mesa",b="Greatmother Hawkwind spends her days beside the fresh water well at Camp Narache, serving as the spiritual matriarch for young tauren beginning their rites of passage. Though years have slowed her stride, she refuses to let age sideline her duty to the village. She believes that every member of the tribe, young or old, has an active role to play in sustaining the community, often asking fledgling youths to lend a hand with simple domestic chores like fetching fresh water.",sp=0},{t="A Mother's Guidance",b="As the mother of Chief Hawkwind, the chieftain of Camp Narache, Greatmother Hawkwind provides steady counsel and quiet dignity amidst the threats facing Red Cloud Mesa. While her son organizes hunters and warriors against encroaching Bristleback quilboar and local predators, she tends to the moral heart of the settlement, reminding the newly awakened that strength begins with humility and service to one's kin.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Greatmother Hawkwind?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is an elderly tauren matriarch residing in Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. She is the mother of the camp's chieftain, Chief Hawkwind, and acts as an elder guide to young adventurers.",al={"greatmother hawkwind lore","who is greatmother hawkwind","camp narache elder"}},{q="Where can she be found?",a="She sits near the central water well in Camp Narache, located within Red Cloud Mesa in southwestern Mulgore.",al={"greatmother hawkwind location","where is greatmother hawkwind"}},{q="What tasks does she ask of young tauren?",a="She charges young tauren with 'A Humble Task,' asking them to gather water from the village well and deliver it to others in the camp. The chore teaches that no task is beneath a true servant of the tribe.",al={"greatmother hawkwind quest","a humble task"}},{q="What is her relation to Chief Hawkwind?",a="She is the mother of Chief Hawkwind, who oversees the defense and training of young braves at Camp Narache.",al={"chief hawkwind mother","is she related to chief hawkwind"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren starting their journey on Red Cloud Mesa, Greatmother Hawkwind embodies the core values of the Earth Mother: respect for elders, community solidarity, and humbleness in service."},kw={"a humble task","camp narache","chief hawkwind","greatmother hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren elder","unaya"},rel={"npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","quest:752","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore"},src={"Greatmother Hawkwind"}}
E["npc:grull-hawkwind"] = {t="npc",n="Grull Hawkwind",z="mulgore",s="Grull Hawkwind is a young tauren brave stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. As the son of Chief Hawkwind, he helps guide novice tauren taking their very first steps into the world. He directs young hunters and warriors through their earliest trials to test their resolve and honor their ancestors.",h="Grull Hawkwind is a young tauren brave stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Chieftain's Son",b="Standing proudly beside the braves and elders of Camp Narache, Grull Hawkwind bears both the proud lineage of Chief Hawkwind and the responsibility of preparing the tribe's youth. He serves as an initial mentor for new arrivals on the Red Cloud Mesa, assessing their readiness to serve the Shu'halo. By assigning trials that test stamina, tracking, and survival against local beasts such as plainstriders and mountain cougars, Grull ensures that young tauren understand that balance and respect for nature are foundational to life in Mulgore.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Grull Hawkwind?",a="Grull Hawkwind is a tauren quest giver residing in Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa. He is the son of Chief Hawkwind, the leader of the settlement, and acts as one of the primary mentors for young tauren beginning their journey.",al={"grull hawkwind role","who is grull","grull hawkwind lore"}},{q="What role does Grull Hawkwind play for young adventurers?",a="Grull assesses the basic training of novice tauren, receiving introductory notes from tribal elders and tasking youth with their opening trials of hunting and survival in the surrounding plains.",al={"grull quests","what does grull do","grull hawkwind purpose"}},{q="Is Grull related to the camp's leadership?",a="Yes, Grull is the son of Chief Hawkwind, the chieftain of Camp Narache. He upholds his father's traditions and helps prepare the camp's defenders.",al={"chief hawkwind son","grull hawkwind family","is grull related to the chief"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren stepping out onto Red Cloud Mesa, Grull Hawkwind is often the very first brave to test their mettle and introduce them to the traditions of the Shu'halo.",["class:Hunter"]="Grull provides novice hunters with fundamental early tasks, testing their patience and skill against the fauna of Mulgore."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","grull hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rite of strength","tauren"},rel={"npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","quest:757","quest:747","quest:780"},src={"Grull Hawkwind"}}
E["npc:harb-clawhoof"] = {t="npc",n="Harb Clawhoof",z="mulgore",s="Harb Clawhoof is a tauren mount breeder stationed just outside Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. He tends to the tribe's sturdy kodos, offering them to worthy tauren riders and trusted champions of Thunder Bluff.",h="Harb Clawhoof is a tauren mount breeder stationed just outside Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Breeder of the Great Kodo",b="Situated near the pens of Bloodhoof Village, Harb Clawhoof spends his days tending the massive kodo beasts that serve as the backbone of tauren travel and labor. Built to carry immense weight across vast distances, these sturdy animals are essential to life on the plains. Harb ensures each beast is well-cared for, disciplined, and matched only with riders who command the respect of the tauren people.",sp=0},{t="Steeds of the Plains",b="While tauren naturally earn the trust required to ride Harb's kodos, outsiders face stricter scrutiny. Due to their immense size and temperament, kodos are not easily guided by those unfamiliar with their nature. Non-tauren allies who wish to ride away atop one of Harb's prized mounts must first prove their loyalty and earn exalted status with Thunder Bluff.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find Harb Clawhoof?",a="Harb Clawhoof is stationed alongside the kodo corral on the outskirts of Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore, just south of Thunder Bluff.",al={"harb clawhoof location","where is harb clawhoof","kodo vendor location"}},{q="What does Harb Clawhoof sell?",a="Harb sells various types of kodo mounts, including standard brown, gray, and white kodos, as well as heavily armored great kodos for seasoned riders.",al={"what does harb sell","harb mounts","kodo vendor wares"}},{q="Can non-tauren buy kodos from Harb?",a="Yes, but only if they have earned exalted standing with Thunder Bluff. Tauren can purchase them directly, but other Horde races must prove themselves to the tribe before Harb will entrust them with a kodo.",al={"can orcs buy kodos","can trolls ride kodos","kodo reputation requirement"}},{q="Why are kodos so important to the tauren?",a="Kodos are powerful, resilient creatures perfectly suited for the nomadic lifestyle and open plains of central Kalimdor. They serve as reliable beasts of burden, war mounts, and trade transport across the rugged landscape.",al={"tauren kodo lore","why kodos"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Harb Clawhoof provides the quintessential mount of your heritage, providing both practical transit across Mulgore and a symbol of your nomadic traditions.",["faction:Horde"]="For orcs, trolls, and Forsaken, earning a kodo from Harb is the ultimate mark of honor and friendship recognized by the elders of Thunder Bluff."},kw={"bloodhoof village","harb clawhoof","kodo trainer","kodo vendor","mount vendor","mulgore kodo","thunder bluff mounts"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren","topic:horde","quest:7662","quest:7663"},src={"Harb Clawhoof"}}
E["npc:harken-windtotem"] = {t="npc",n="Harken Windtotem",z="mulgore",s="Harken Windtotem is a tauren huntsman residing in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. Dedicated to the traditions of his people, he tasks young hunters and travelers with proving their tracking and foraging skills across the golden plains.",h="Harken Windtotem is a tauren huntsman residing in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Huntsman of Bloodhoof",b="Situated near the heart of Bloodhoof Village, Harken Windtotem serves as an instructor and guide for those learning the hunting ways of the tauren. Life on the plains of Mulgore demands respect for the natural world alongside the practical skill to sustain one's tribe. Harken evaluates young adventurers by directing them to hunt the native swoops that circle the mesas, collecting their quills to gauge a hunter's patience, aim, and ability to harvest without waste.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Harken Windtotem?",a="Harken is a tauren hunter living in Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore. He guides travelers and fledgling adventurers in learning the fundamental tracking and hunting traditions of the tauren.",al={"harken windtotem lore","who is harken"}},{q="Where can Harken Windtotem be found?",a="Harken is stationed in Bloodhoof Village, a settlement situated south of Thunder Bluff along the shores of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore.",al={"harken location","where is harken windtotem"}},{q="What tasks does Harken give to adventurers?",a="He offers the task Swoop Hunting, asking hunters to venture into the grasslands of Mulgore to bring down wild swoops and gather their sturdy flight quills.",al={"harken windtotem quests","swoop hunting quest"}},{q="Is Harken related to Bovan Windtotem?",a="They share the Windtotem clan name, suggesting a tribal kinship or family connection, though Harken remains focused on local village hunts in Mulgore.",al={"is harken related to bovan","bovan windtotem relation"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Harken Windtotem embodies the ancestral tauren philosophy of hunting: proving discipline and self-sufficiency on the plains while honoring the beasts of the Earth Mother.",["class:Hunter"]="For wilderness trackers passing through Bloodhoof Village, Harken's task of bringing down airborne swoops provides an essential early test of marksmanship and quarry collection."},kw={"bloodhoof village","harken windtotem","mulgore","swoop hunting","tauren","windtotem"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:761","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Harken Windtotem"}}
E["npc:holt-thunderhorn"] = {t="npc",n="Holt Thunderhorn",z="mulgore",s="Holt Thunderhorn is a tauren hunter trainer stationed upon Hunter Rise in the elevated city of Thunder Bluff. He instructs novice and seasoned track-masters in their craft, offering basic martial guidance and pointing maturing marksmen toward specialized trials across Mulgore and beyond.",h="Holt Thunderhorn is a tauren hunter trainer stationed upon Hunter Rise in the elevated city of Thunder Bluff.",sec={{t="Master of the Rise",b="Perched on Hunter Rise above the sweeping plains of Mulgore, Holt Thunderhorn serves the Horde as a seasoned instructor in ranged combat, tracking, and survival. Like many elders of his craft, he guides young tauren as they make the transition from roaming hunters into disciplined defenders of their new capital. When aspirants reach an age to bind and train beasts of their own, Holt directs them to seasoned masters like Ormak Grimshot to undertake the necessary trials.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can Holt Thunderhorn be found?",a="Holt Thunderhorn stands on Hunter Rise, the southeastern elevated plateau within the tauren capital of Thunder Bluff in Mulgore.",al={"holt thunderhorn location","where is holt","hunter trainer thunder bluff"}},{q="What does Holt Thunderhorn teach?",a="He is a hunter trainer who instructs players in essential marksmanship, wilderness survival, and beast-taming techniques, as well as directing novices to specialized beast mastery trials.",al={"what does holt teach","holt thunderhorn trainer","what abilities holt"}},{q="What is Holt's connection to Ormak Grimshot?",a="When growing hunters are ready to expand their knowledge of beasts and animal companion care, Holt sends them to confer with fellow veteran hunter Ormak Grimshot on Hunter Rise.",al={"ormak and holt","why send to ormak","ormak grimshot holt"}},{q="What role do hunters hold in Thunder Bluff?",a="Hunters form the traditional backbone of tauren society, providing sustenance, patrolling territorial borders against centaur raiding parties, and honoring the Earth Mother through balanced, reverent tracking.",al={"hunter rise purpose","tauren hunter lore"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="Holt Thunderhorn serves as a central mentor in Thunder Bluff, offering weapon techniques, tracking training, and referrals for taming beasts.",["race:Tauren"]="Stationed on Hunter Rise, Holt embodies the traditional tauren hunting culture that fed and protected the nomadic tribes long before they established a permanent home atop the bluffs."},kw={"holt thunderhorn","hunter rise","hunter trainer","mulgore","ormak grimshot","thunder bluff"},rel={"subzone:hunter-rise","zone:thunderbluff","npc:ormak-grimshot","quest:6081","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Holt Thunderhorn"}}
E["npc:lanka-farshot"] = {t="npc",n="Lanka Farshot",z="mulgore",s="Lanka Farshot is a tauren hunter trainer stationed at Camp Narache within the Red Cloud Mesa. He guides novice tauren hunters as they take their first steps in tracking, marksmanship, and surviving the plains of Mulgore.",h="Lanka Farshot is a tauren hunter trainer stationed at Camp Narache within the Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="Mentor of the Red Cloud Mesa",b="Standing watch over Camp Narache, Lanka Farshot greets new tauren called to the path of the hunter. Fresh recruits arrive carrying an etched note directing them to seek his counsel. Under his steady eye, apprentices learn the fundamentals of ranged combat, tracking prey, and respecting the wild beasts that roam the grassy plains of Mulgore. His teachings ensure that every young hunter serves as a capable scout and provider for the tribe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Lanka Farshot?",a="Lanka Farshot is the tauren hunter trainer stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. He provides initial training and guidance to young hunters beginning their journey.",al={"who is lanka","lanka farshot npc","camp narache hunter"}},{q="What quests does Lanka Farshot offer?",a="Lanka receives young tauren hunters sent to him via an etched note upon their arrival in Camp Narache and directs them onto the hunter's path as they learn the basics of their craft.",al={"lanka farshot quests","hunter quests camp narache"}},{q="Is Lanka Farshot related to Mixie Farshot?",a="Despite sharing the same surname, there is no evidence that Lanka Farshot is related to the goblin Mixie Farshot.",al={"is lanka related to mixie","mixie farshot relation"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="For tauren hunters beginning their training in Mulgore, Lanka Farshot is the very first mentor they encounter, teaching fundamental marksmanship and wild survival.",["race:Tauren"]="Lanka embodies the traditional tauren hunting philosophy, viewing marksmanship and tracking as vital duties for protecting the tribe and living in balance with the plains."},kw={"camp narache","hunter trainer","lanka farshot","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren hunter"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","quest:3092","quest:6065","topic:tauren","topic:horde"},src={"Lanka Farshot"}}
E["npc:lorekeeper-raintotem"] = {t="npc",n="Lorekeeper Raintotem",z="mulgore",s="Lorekeeper Raintotem is a revered tauren elder stationed in northern Mulgore just south of Red Rocks. He serves as an overseer for sacred rites of passage and guardian of the ancestors' resting grounds.",h="Lorekeeper Raintotem is a revered tauren elder stationed in northern Mulgore just south of Red Rocks.",sec={{t="Watcher of Red Rocks",b="Positioned near the sacred bluffs of Red Rocks in Mulgore, Lorekeeper Raintotem watches over ancestral heritage and spiritual rites. As young tauren complete their traditional rites of passage, Raintotem oversees the Rite of Wisdom, testing their understanding and spiritual devotion before guiding them toward greater trials across the plains.",sp=0},{t="Defender of Ancestral Grounds",b="Beyond testing fledgling heroes, Lorekeeper Raintotem watches over the hallowed burial sites at Red Rocks. When Bristleback quilboar interlopers disturb the sanctified resting places of deceased tauren chieftains and shamans, Raintotem enlists travelers to restore dignity and quiet the disturbed dead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find Lorekeeper Raintotem?",a="Lorekeeper Raintotem is stationed in northern Mulgore, located just south of Red Rocks along the path leading up toward the ancestral cliffs.",al={"raintotem location","where is lorekeeper raintotem","find raintotem mulgore"}},{q="What is the Rite of Wisdom?",a="The Rite of Wisdom is an integral rite of passage for young tauren adventurers in Mulgore. Supervised by Lorekeeper Raintotem, it tests an initiate's spiritual grounding and readiness to serve their people beyond Bloodhoof Village.",al={"rite of wisdom quest","what is rite of wisdom"}},{q="Why does Raintotem need help at Red Rocks?",a="Red Rocks serves as a sacred tauren burial ground, but Bristleback quilboar interlopers are ravaging the grave site. Raintotem tasks capable adventurers with putting the dishonored ancestral grounds back in order.",al={"red rocks problem","why sacred burial quest"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren heroes, presenting yourself to Lorekeeper Raintotem is an essential rite of passage that bridges youthful training and adult duties to the tribe.",["class:Shaman"]="Raintotem's vigilance over restless dead at Red Rocks directly concerns shamanic duty, ensuring ancestral spirits receive proper peace and reverence."},kw={"lorekeeper raintotem","mulgore","raintotem","red rocks","rite of wisdom","sacred burial"},rel={"subzone:red-rocks","quest:773","quest:833","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Lorekeeper Raintotem"}}
E["npc:minor-manifestation-of-earth"] = {t="npc",n="Minor Manifestation of Earth",z="mulgore",s="A Minor Manifestation of Earth is a localized elemental spirit bound to sacred stones in Mulgore and Durotar. Invisible to ordinary sight, it reveals itself only to fledgling shamans who drink the sacred Earth Sapta, providing the rough quartz required to carve their very first earth totem.",h="A Minor Manifestation of Earth is a localized elemental spirit bound to sacred stones in Mulgore and Durotar.",sec={{t="Voice of the Stone",b="The spirits of the earth dwell everywhere beneath the dirt and stone, but communicating with them requires deliberate ritual. At sacred landmarks like Kodo Rock in Mulgore and Spirit Rock in Durotar, Minor Manifestations of Earth linger in the unseen realm, surrounded by even smaller elemental presences. Only initiates who have drunk the Earth Sapta—a ritual draught brewed by shamanic mentors—can lift the veil between the physical world and the elements. Once acknowledged, the spirit imparts the earth's blessings along with a piece of rough quartz, granting the novice the spiritual focal point needed to fashion an Earth Totem.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why can't I see the Minor Manifestation of Earth?",a="The manifestation exists on the elemental plane and remains invisible to the naked eye. You must drink an Earth Sapta, provided by your shaman trainer, to enter the altered state of perception required to perceive and speak with elemental spirits.",al={"cannot see manifestation","where is the earth spirit","earth manifestation invisible"}},{q="Where can these earth spirits be found?",a="They appear at Kodo Rock in the rolling plains of Mulgore and at Spirit Rock in the arid canyons of Durotar. Both sites serve as traditional proving grounds for young tauren, orc, and troll shamans.",al={"minor manifestation location","where to find manifestation of earth"}},{q="What is the spirit's purpose during the rite?",a="The manifestation tests and formally acknowledges the initiate's connection to the earth element. Upon speaking with the shaman, it bestows a piece of rough quartz, which is taken back to a shaman trainer to construct the Earth Totem.",al={"what does manifestation do","manifestation quest purpose"}},{q="What are the smaller creatures surrounding it?",a="Surrounding the central spirit are lesser manifestations of earth. They are minor echos of the element drawn to the sacred grounds, existing purely as part of the natural communion, and cannot be directly interacted with.",al={"lesser manifestations","small earth elementals around it"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Every young Horde shaman must commune with a Minor Manifestation of Earth under the influence of the Earth Sapta to earn their very first totem.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren youth travel east across Mulgore to Kodo Rock to seek communion with this manifestation as part of their ancestral spiritual trials."},kw={"call of earth","earth sapta","earth totem","kodo rock","minor manifestation of earth","rough quartz","spirit rock"},rel={"quest:1520","subzone:kodo-rock","subzone:spirit-rock","zone:mulgore","zone:durotar"},src={"Minor Manifestation of Earth"}}
E["npc:morin-cloudstalker"] = {t="npc",n="Morin Cloudstalker",z="mulgore",s="Morin Cloudstalker is a tauren scout keeping watch along the southern road connecting Bloodhoof Village to the Great Gate in Mulgore. Standing vigil over the plains, he monitors local trade routes and investigates the encroaching threats endangering tauren travelers.",h="Morin Cloudstalker is a tauren scout keeping watch along the southern road connecting Bloodhoof Village to...",sec={{t="Watcher on the Southern Trail",b="Positioned beside the dirt path winding south toward the Great Gate, Morin Cloudstalker acts as both a scout and a guardian for travelers through southern Mulgore. The grassy plains here are usually peaceful, but the opening of trade routes and shifting territories have drawn opportunists and bandits dangerously close to the heart of the tauren homeland.",sp=0},{t="Investigating the Caravan",b="Morin is among the first to notice when a supply caravan goes missing along the southern roadway. Upon finding the wreckage of the ravaged wagon, he coordinates investigations to track down the raiders and determine whether local quillboar or other marauders are responsible for pillaging essential goods.",sp=0},{t="Defending Against Venture Co.",b="Beyond common brigands, Morin is deeply concerned by the industrial presence of the Venture Company establishing operations within Mulgore. He directs passing Horde adventurers to strike at their logging and mining outposts, targeting key leaders like Supervisor Fizsprocket to curtail the environmental devastation threatening the sacred plains.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where can I find Morin Cloudstalker?",a="Morin Cloudstalker stands by the dirt trail south of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, along the route leading toward the Great Gate.",al={"morin location","where is morin","morin cloudstalker map"}},{q="What is Morin's role in Mulgore?",a="He is a watchful tauren scout responsible for monitoring travel through southern Mulgore and organizing defense against bandits and corporate intruders.",al={"who is morin cloudstalker","morin role"}},{q="Why is Morin investigating the ruined caravan?",a="A supply wagon was ambushed along the trail, and Morin tasks adventurers with surveying the scene, uncovering clues, and dealing with the raiders who attacked it.",al={"ravaged caravan quest","caravan attack mulgore"}},{q="Why does Morin want the Venture Company stopped?",a="The Venture Company is stripping Mulgore's natural resources with machinery and deforestation. Morin seeks to disrupt their destructive enterprise by eliminating their overseers, including Supervisor Fizsprocket.",al={"venture co mulgore","why kill fizsprocket"}},{q="Who does Morin report to?",a="Morin operates on behalf of the elders and chieftains of Bloodhoof Village and the broader tauren leadership across Mulgore.",al={"morin allegiance","who sent morin"}},{q="Does Morin offer quests to Alliance players?",a="No. Morin is an NPC loyal strictly to the Horde and will only interact with Horde adventurers.",al={"can alliance talk to morin","morin faction"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren journeying outward from Bloodhoof Village, Morin represents the vigilant defense of your homeland against both opportunistic bandits and corporate defilers.",["faction:Horde"]="Morin offers Horde champions an early opportunity to protect the supplies and sacred lands of their new tauren allies."},kw={"bloodhoof village","great gate","morin cloudstalker","mulgore","ravaged caravan","supervisor fizsprocket","venture co"},rel={"quest:749","quest:764","quest:765","subzone:bloodhoof-village","subzone:great-gate","subzone:ravaged-caravan","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Morin Cloudstalker"}}
E["npc:mull-thunderhorn"] = {t="npc",n="Mull Thunderhorn",z="mulgore",s="Mull Thunderhorn is a tauren elder residing in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. Deeply devoted to the Earth Mother and the balance of the land, he watches over the sacred water wells scattered across the plains. As outside exploiters threaten the region's lifegiving waters, Mull guides young tauren through the rituals required to cleanse the ancestral springs.",h="Mull Thunderhorn is a tauren elder residing in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Guardian of the Sacred Waters",b="Standing watch in Bloodhoof Village, Mull Thunderhorn carries the weight of Mulgore's spiritual well-being. The nomadic tribes have gathered under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof to establish a permanent home, yet their lands are already under siege by greed and reckless expansion. Mull serves as a spiritual anchor for the young braves stepping out of Camp Narache, calling upon them to safeguard the vital resources of their ancestors.",sp=0},{t="Tainted Wells and the Venture Company",b="The primary crisis facing Mull is the sudden sickness spreading through Mulgore's water sources: the Winterhoof, Thunderhorn, and Wildmane water wells. This foul affliction is traced to the goblins of the Venture Company, an unscrupulous commercial outfit carving operations into the surrounding hills and dumping poisons into the local water table. Mull directs young tauren to prepare specific purification totems and perform cleansing rites at each ancestral well before the corruption spreads irrevocably into the land and its wildlife.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Mull Thunderhorn?",a="Mull Thunderhorn is a tauren quest giver situated in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. He acts as a guardian of the sacred water wells in the area, offering rites and rituals to keep the earth and its waters pure from external contamination.",al={"mull thunderhorn lore","who is mull","bloodhoof village mull"}},{q="Why is Mull Thunderhorn worried about the water wells?",a="The ancestral water wells—such as the Winterhoof, Thunderhorn, and Wildmane wells—are life-giving sources for the tribes and wildlife of Mulgore. Intrusive operations nearby have tainted these sacred springs with noxious runoff and pollution, threatening the natural balance of the plains.",al={"why are wells poisoned","water wells mulgore","winterhoof well poisoned"}},{q="Who is polluting the water wells in Mulgore?",a="The pollution is caused by the Venture Company, a reckless goblin enterprise establishing industrial mining and logging footholds across the edges of Mulgore. Their operations show no regard for the sacred landscape, spilling industrial toxins into the local water table.",al={"who poisoned the water","venture co in mulgore","who tainted the wells"}},{q="Why do Mull's purification quests only involve tauren?",a="The water wells hold deep ancestral and spiritual significance tied to the founding lineages of the tauren tribes—including the Thunderhorn, Winterhoof, and Wildmane clans. Cleansing them is viewed as a sacred rite of passage and cultural duty for maturing tauren.",al={"tauren only quests","why only tauren","mull thunderhorn requirements"}},{q="How are the water wells purified?",a="Mull instructs travelers to assemble specially consecrated totems imbued with local natural reagents, such as claws and hides. Once consecrated, these totems are brought directly to the fouled wells to channel spiritual purification and wash away the unnatural toxins.",al={"how to cleanse wells","cleansing totems","cleaning the water"}},{q="What is the Rite of Vision?",a="The Rite of Vision is a foundational spiritual journey undertaken by young tauren to commune with their ancestors and animal spirits. Mull Thunderhorn directs initiates through key steps of this visionary trial after they demonstrate their respect and duty toward the earth.",al={"rite of vision quest","what is rite of vision"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, Mull Thunderhorn represents tribal memory and ancestral duty, guiding young braves in protecting the sacred plains against greedy foreign invaders.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman will recognize Mull's reliance on elemental harmony, as his cleansing totems utilize ancestral spirits to mend waters torn by mechanical greed.",["class:Druid"]="Druids share Mull's urgency, witnessing firsthand how the corrupted water wells poison the balance of Mulgore's wildlife.",["faction:Horde"]="Mull's struggle underscores the Horde's friction with unaligned, exploitative organizations like the Venture Company that strip Kalimdor's riches without consent."},kw={"bloodhoof village","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","poison water","thunderhorn totem","venture company","water wells","wildmane cleansing","winterhoof cleansing"},rel={"zone:mulgore","subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:748","quest:754","quest:756","quest:758","topic:venture-company","topic:tauren"},src={"Mull Thunderhorn"}}
E["npc:ruul-eagletalon"] = {t="npc",n="Ruul Eagletalon",z="mulgore",s="Ruul Eagletalon is a tauren resident of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. Stationed near the settlement's perimeter, he keeps a watchful eye on the local wildlife, particularly the aggressive Windfury harpies encroaching upon tauren lands.",h="Ruul Eagletalon is a tauren resident of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Watcher of Bloodhoof",b="Ruul Eagletalon stands guard in Bloodhoof Village, tending to the security of the settlement as young braves arrive from Camp Narache. While Mulgore is a peaceful homeland compared to the arid wastes of the Barrens, territorial predators and hostile creatures continually press against the pastures. Ruul concerns himself with threats roaming the nearby plains, notably directing capable travelers to confront the savage Windfury harpies who prey on unwary nomads and game.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Ruul Eagletalon?",a="Ruul Eagletalon is a tauren stationed in Bloodhoof Village who helps protect the settlement by directing adventurers against local threats across Mulgore.",al={"who is ruul","ruul eagletalon npc","bloodhoof ruul"}},{q="What threat does Ruul Eagletalon ask travelers to deal with?",a="Ruul warns of the hostile Windfury harpies nesting within Mulgore and tasks capable braves with culling their numbers before they bring harm to the village.",al={"ruul quest","dangers of the windfury","harpies mulgore quest"}},{q="Where can Ruul Eagletalon be found?",a="He can be found standing along the grounds of Bloodhoof Village, situated by the shores of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore.",al={"ruul eagletalon location","find ruul","where is ruul"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Ruul represents the practical vigilance required of the Shu'halo, ensuring that the newfound sanctuary of Mulgore remains safe from territorial predators and winged raiders."},kw={"bloodhoof","eagletalon","harpies","mulgore","ruul","windfury"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:743","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Ruul Eagletalon"}}
E["npc:seer-graytongue"] = {t="npc",n="Seer Graytongue",z="mulgore",s="Seer Graytongue is a venerable tauren elder and spiritual guide residing on Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. Stationed near Camp Narache, he tests young braves undertaking their earliest tribal trials. Through rituals such as the Rite of Strength, he ensures that the youth honor the Earthmother before stepping into the wider world.",h="Seer Graytongue is a venerable tauren elder and spiritual guide residing on Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Guide on the Mesa",b="Seer Graytongue makes his home atop Red Cloud Mesa, serving as an early instructor for aspiring tauren braves and spellweavers. As young adventurers set forth from Camp Narache, Graytongue oversees critical steps of their sacred passage, including the Rites of the Earthmother. By testing their resolve against the harsh plains and dangerous beasts of Mulgore, he instills the virtues of honor, patience, and reverence that every true tauren must carry into adulthood.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Seer Graytongue?",a="Seer Graytongue is an elder tauren seer living in a lodge atop Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. He directs young heroes through traditional ceremonial duties to prove their worth to their tribe.",al={"graytongue","seer graytongue role","who is the seer on red cloud mesa"}},{q="What trials does Seer Graytongue oversee?",a="He is tasked with guiding youths through components of the Rites of the Earthmother, including the Rite of Strength. These rituals test a brave's fortitude and respect for the natural balance of the plains.",al={"graytongue quests","what does graytongue want","rites of the earthmother graytongue"}},{q="Where can I find Seer Graytongue?",a="He resides in a modest leather lodge resting along the perimeter of Red Cloud Mesa, just outside the central activity of Camp Narache in Mulgore.",al={"seer graytongue location","where is graytongue","graytongue hut"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren youths, visiting Seer Graytongue marks a sacred rite of passage that connects personal strength with the eternal blessings of the Earthmother.",["class:Shaman"]="Spiritual initiates look to elders like Graytongue to learn how sacred ritual and trial bind the physical might of a warrior to ancestral guidance."},kw={"camp narache","graytongue","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rite of strength","rites of the earthmother","seer graytongue"},rel={"quest:763","quest:757","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","topic:quilboar"},src={"Seer Graytongue"}}
E["npc:seer-ravenfeather"] = {t="npc",n="Seer Ravenfeather",z="mulgore",s="Seer Ravenfeather is a tauren shaman instructor stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa. She guides fledging spiritualists through their early trials, helping them attune to the voices of the spirits and the land of Mulgore.",h="Seer Ravenfeather is a tauren shaman instructor stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="A Guide on the Mesa",b="Standing within the quiet shelters of Camp Narache, Seer Ravenfeather serves as a patient teacher for young tauren called to walk the shamanic path. The Red Cloud Mesa is a sacred proving ground, and Ravenfeather ensures that novice practitioners understand their duties before venturing into the wider expanses of Mulgore. She instructs them in the foundational rites needed to commune with elemental forces and honor the Earth Mother.",sp=0},{t="The Call of the Elements",b="Ravenfeather's primary charge is overseeing the initial spiritual steps of nascent shamans, directing them in the traditional rite known as the Call of Earth. Through her guidance, initiates prepare ceremonial saptas and seek communion with the earthen spirits nestled within the mesa's stony outcrops, forging a bond that anchors their elemental magic.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Seer Ravenfeather?",a="Seer Ravenfeather is a spiritual mentor residing in Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa. She trains young tauren shamans taking their first steps in the world.",al={"seer ravenfeather lore","who is ravenfeather","camp narache seer"}},{q="What role does she play in shaman training?",a="She guides novice shamans through their earliest rituals, most notably the quest chain to commune with the spirits of the land and answer the Call of Earth.",al={"shaman quests ravenfeather","call of earth trainer","what does ravenfeather teach"}},{q="Where can I find Seer Ravenfeather?",a="She can be found among the tents and lodges of Camp Narache, located on the secluded Red Cloud Mesa in southwestern Mulgore.",al={"ravenfeather location","where is ravenfeather","camp narache location"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="For young tauren shamans, Seer Ravenfeather is the very first guide on the long journey of learning to commune with the elements and bind the powers of earth.",["race:Tauren"]="As a seer of Camp Narache, she represents the deep spiritual tradition that anchors tauren society as they settle into their ancestral home."},kw={"call of earth","camp narache","earth sapta","mulgore","ravenfeather","red cloud mesa","seer ravenfeather","shaman"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","quest:1520","topic:tauren"},src={"Seer Ravenfeather"}}
E["npc:skorn-whitecloud"] = {t="npc",n="Skorn Whitecloud",z="mulgore",s="Skorn Whitecloud is a tauren hunter and weapon merchant residing at Camp Sungraze in the rolling plains of Mulgore. He guides young hunters in understanding their relationship with the land's predators and aids travelers who cross paths with Mulgore's lurking dangers.",h="Skorn Whitecloud is a tauren hunter and weapon merchant residing at Camp Sungraze in the rolling plains of...",sec={{t="A Guide on the Golden Plains",b="Positioned at Camp Sungraze in southern Mulgore, Skorn Whitecloud serves as both an armorer and an instructor in the ways of the hunt. While he provides basic weapons and hunting implements to passing travelers, his primary role is instilling respect for the wild creatures of the plains. He tests aspiring hunters by directing them to track Mulgore's ferocious flatland cougars, ensuring that those who take up the bow or spear understand the balance between predator and prey.",sp=0},{t="Vigilance Against the Unnatural",b="Beyond ordinary hunting duties, Skorn remains watchful for strange disturbances across the savannah. When unnatural threats surface—such as signs of demonic corruption left upon the wildlife and beasts of the plains—Skorn helps piece together what transpired, ensuring the tribes of Mulgore remain alert to malevolent forces creeping into their ancestral home.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where can Skorn Whitecloud be found?",a="Skorn Whitecloud is located at Camp Sungraze, a small tauren encampment situated in the southern grasslands of Mulgore.",al={"skorn whitecloud location","where is skorn","skorn whitecloud map"}},{q="What role does Skorn play for young hunters?",a="Skorn teaches the core principles of hunting through the rite known as The Hunter's Way. He tasks aspiring hunters with proving their tracking and combat skills against the plains' stealthy flatland cougars.",al={"what does skorn do","skorn hunter quest","the hunters way"}},{q="What goods does Skorn sell?",a="Skorn serves as a weaponsmith and general arms merchant, offering basic melee armaments, staves, and thrown weapons to adventurers passing through Camp Sungraze.",al={"skorn vendor","what does skorn sell","skorn weapons"}},{q="Is Skorn Whitecloud related to any other tauren in Mulgore?",a="He shares a surname with Provisioner Whitecloud, a tauren merchant residing at Bloodhoof Village, suggesting ties to the same extended tribal family.",al={"whitecloud family","skorn relatives","provisioner whitecloud"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="For young tauren hunters, Skorn Whitecloud offers early guidance in stalking swift predators like the flatland cougar, reinforcing the spiritual harmony of the hunt.",["race:Tauren"]="Skorn exemplifies the Whitecloud family's dedication to providing shelter, weaponry, and practical wisdom across the scattered settlements of Mulgore."},kw={"camp sungraze","flatland cougar","mulgore","skorn","tauren","the hunter's way","whitecloud"},rel={"zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","quest:861","quest:770"},src={"Skorn Whitecloud"}}
E["npc:vorn-skyseer"] = {t="npc",n="Vorn Skyseer",z="mulgore",s="Vorn Skyseer is a peaceful tauren resident of Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore. Resting beside the village lodgings, he greets young adventurers arriving from the Red Cloud Mesa and introduces them to local life.",h="Vorn Skyseer is a peaceful tauren resident of Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Friendly Face in Bloodhoof",b="Situated near the heart of Bloodhoof Village on the shores of Lake Stonebull, Vorn Skyseer serves as an informal welcoming presence for travelers descending from Camp Narache. As young tauren complete their rites of passage and venture into the wider valleys of Mulgore, Vorn offers kind words and hospitality, helping new arrivals settle into the bustling settlement.",sp=0},{t="A Detached Observer",b="Despite the constant friction between the Horde and Alliance on other fronts, Vorn maintains a quiet, almost neutral demeanor. Even during border incursions into the settlement, he remains aloof from skirmishes, refusing to take up arms or summon the village braves, content instead to watch over the tranquil plains of his homeland.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Vorn Skyseer?",a="Vorn Skyseer is an elder resident of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. He primarily serves to welcome young tauren braves who have freshly arrived from their introductory rites at Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"vorn skyseer lore","who is vorn","vorn mulgore"}},{q="Where can I find Vorn Skyseer?",a="He can be found resting outdoors among the tents and longhouses of Bloodhoof Village, situated in the central plains of Mulgore just north of Lake Stonebull.",al={"vorn skyseer location","where is vorn","vorn bloodhoof"}},{q="What quests does Vorn offer?",a="He is involved in welcoming young Horde adventurers to the settlement, helping them acclimate to the village after departing their tribal training grounds.",al={"vorn skyseer quests","vorn welcome quest"}},{q="Why doesn't Vorn Skyseer attack Alliance players?",a="Unlike the Bloodhoof village braves and guards, Vorn is an unattackable civilian NPC who remains passive and does not sound the alarm or engage in combat when hostile intruders enter town.",al={"is vorn hostile","can alliance kill vorn","why is vorn peaceful"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren venturing down from the Red Cloud Mesa, Vorn Skyseer is often one of the first familiar, reassuring faces to greet them as they step into the wider life of Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="Vorn exemplifies the calm and steady nature of the plains tribes who have gathered under Cairne Bloodhoof's leadership to build a permanent home."},kw={"bloodhoof village","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren","vorn skyseer","welcome"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","quest:5805","topic:tauren","topic:alliance"},src={"Vorn Skyseer"}}
E["npc:yaw-sharpmane"] = {t="npc",n="Yaw Sharpmane",z="mulgore",s="Yaw Sharpmane is an experienced tauren hunter trainer stationed in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. He guides young hunters venturing out from Camp Narache, helping them master the wild beasts of their ancestral plains.",h="Yaw Sharpmane is an experienced tauren hunter trainer stationed in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Guide on the Plains",b="Positioned amidst the tents and fires of Bloodhoof Village, Yaw Sharpmane serves the Shu'halo as an instructor in the ways of marksmanship, tracking, and survival. Young hunters who have proven themselves in Red Cloud Mesa are directed to Yaw to hone their craft as they step onto the broader plains of Mulgore.",sp=0},{t="Mastering the Bond",b="Yaw's most essential role is guiding novice hunters through the ancient rite of taming wild beasts. He tasks initiates with bonding with the native fauna of Mulgore, testing their patience and respect for the natural balance before instructing them on how to properly care for and train a permanent companion.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Yaw Sharpmane is the resident hunter trainer in Bloodhoof Village. He instructs young tauren hunters in marksmanship, wilderness survival, and the art of taming animal companions.",al={"yaw sharpmane","who is yaw","bloodhoof hunter trainer"}},{q="Where can I find Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Yaw is located in Bloodhoof Village, situated in central Mulgore along the shores of Stonebull Lake.",al={"yaw sharpmane location","where is yaw","yaw coords"}},{q="What does Yaw Sharpmane teach hunters?",a="He offers standard hunter training, selling rank-ups for basic abilities and directing promising hunters through the rites required to tame and command beasts.",al={"what does yaw teach","yaw hunter skills","yaw abilities"}},{q="What quests does Yaw offer?",a="Yaw provides the tauren version of the beast-taming questline, starting with 'The Hunter's Path' and progressing through 'Taming the Beast' and 'Training the Beast.'",al={"yaw quests","yaw sharpmane quests"}},{q="Why do hunters need to speak to him around level 10?",a="Upon reaching their tenth season, hunters are sent to Yaw to learn the sacred methods of capturing, feeding, and commanding permanent animal companions from the wilds of Mulgore.",al={"yaw level 10","tame beast bloodhoof"}},{q="Does Yaw Sharpmane interact with non-hunters?",a="He will speak politely with travelers of other callings, but his professional knowledge and specific quest trials are reserved strictly for hunters.",al={"yaw non hunters","can other classes talk to yaw"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="For tauren hunters leaving the safety of Red Cloud Mesa, Yaw Sharpmane is an essential mentor who guides you through bonding with your very first wild companion.",["race:Tauren"]="Yaw represents the revered Shu'halo tradition of living in harmony with nature and honoring the spirits of the wild beasts of Mulgore."},kw={"beasts","bloodhoof","hunter","mulgore","sharpmane","taming","tauren","trainer","yaw"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","quest:6061","quest:6081","quest:6065","topic:tauren"},src={"Yaw Sharpmane"}}
E["npc:zarlman-two-moons"] = {t="npc",n="Zarlman Two-Moons",z="mulgore",s="Zarlman Two-Moons is a revered tauren mystic residing in Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore. While Baine Bloodhoof oversees the village's daily earthly affairs, Zarlman guides its spiritual life and communions with the spirits. He plays a vital role in initiating young tauren into adulthood through the sacred Rite of Vision.",h="Zarlman Two-Moons is a revered tauren mystic residing in Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore.",sec={{t="Keeper of the Unseen",b="Within Bloodhoof Village, leadership is divided between the physical and the mystical. Baine Bloodhoof commands the settlement's defense and temporal needs, but it is Zarlman Two-Moons who maintains the harmony of the spirit world. In a culture deeply rooted in reverence for the Earth Mother and ancestral wisdom, Zarlman serves as a conduit between the everyday struggles of the plains and the timeless voices of the past.",sp=0},{t="The Rite of Vision",b="Zarlman's most crucial duty to his people is administering the Rite of Vision, a traditional trial every maturing tauren must undertake. By guiding initiates to prepare sacred draughts and listen closely to ancestral portents, he helps young hunters, shamans, and warriors discover their destined path and spirit guides. Under his instruction, initiates learn that physical strength on the plains means little without spiritual clarity.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is a prominent tauren mystic stationed in Bloodhoof Village. While Baine Bloodhoof handles temporal leadership and the town's practical defense, Zarlman watches over the settlement's spiritual well-being and guides young adventurers through ancient tribal traditions.",al={"who is zarlman","zarlman two moons lore","who is the shaman in bloodhoof"}},{q="What is Zarlman's role in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Zarlman oversees spiritual affairs, ancestral communion, and cultural rites for the residents of Bloodhoof Village. He complements Baine Bloodhoof's physical leadership by ensuring the tribe remains in balance with nature spirits and their ancestors.",al={"zarlman job","zarlman purpose","bloodhoof village mystic"}},{q="What is the Rite of Vision?",a="The Rite of Vision is a traditional rite of passage for young tauren passing through Mulgore. Guided by Zarlman, initiates drink a ritual concoction that opens their spiritual sight, allowing them to follow visions and receive guidance from nature spirits.",al={"rite of vision quest","what is the vision rite","tauren vision quest"}},{q="Where can Zarlman Two-Moons be found?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is located in Bloodhoof Village, situated in the center of Mulgore just south of Stonebull Lake.",al={"where is zarlman","zarlman location","how to find zarlman"}},{q="How does Zarlman relate to Baine Bloodhoof?",a="Zarlman works alongside Baine Bloodhoof as a spiritual counterpart. While Baine leads the village in physical and martial matters, Zarlman provides mystical wisdom and watches over the tribe's spiritual balance.",al={"baine and zarlman","zarlman relation to baine"}},{q="Why is the Rite of Vision important to young tauren?",a="The Rite of Vision is essential for every tauren coming of age. It marks the transition from unproven youth to an acknowledged member of the tribe, revealing their inner spirit guide and cementing their bond with the Earth Mother.",al={"why do rite of vision","rite of vision importance"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren adventurers, Zarlman is an essential guide who helps you complete the sacred rites of your youth and awaken your spiritual sight.",["class:Shaman"]="Zarlman exemplifies the classical tauren tradition of shamanism, balancing communion with the spirits alongside practical tribal leadership.",["faction:Horde"]="Stationed in Mulgore's central hub, Zarlman introduces newly arrived Horde allies to the deep spiritual customs that shape tauren culture."},kw={"bloodhoof","mulgore","rite","shaman","spirits","tauren","two-moons","village","vision","zarlman"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","npc:baine-bloodhoof","quest:767","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Zarlman Two-Moons"}}
E["quest:1520"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="mulgore",s="This sacred rite marks a tauren shaman's spiritual attunement to the element of earth. By drinking an Earth Sapta at a holy rock east of Camp Narache, initiates peer beyond physical senses to commune directly with a Minor Manifestation of Earth.",h="This sacred rite marks a tauren shaman's spiritual attunement to the element of earth.",sec={{t="The Purpose of the Sapta",b="A sapta is a carefully concocted ceremonial potion crafted by shamanic elders to bind an initiate's mortal spirit directly to a primal element. Prepared with ancestral magic, only a select few among the tauren preserve the secrets of its brewing. When imbibed at a designated sacred site, the draught expands the imbiber's mind, piercing the veil between the physical realm and the spiritual plane so the elements can be witnessed in their raw form.",sp=0},{t="Communion at Kodo Rock",b="East of Camp Narache stands Kodo Rock, a looming stone pillar that serves as a holy sanctuary. Consuming the sapta here reveals the Minor Manifestation of Earth awaiting the pilgrim. Unlike the volatile, fiery, or raging aspects of nature encountered later along the shaman's path, the spirit of earth asks only that the initiate approach in quiet humility, granting the mountain's enduring strength and the plains' calm patience to all who honor the land.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is an Earth Sapta?",a="An Earth Sapta is an alchemical and spiritual draught brewed by shamanic elders. Its magic opens the drinker's perception, allowing an initiate to see and communicate directly with elemental earth spirits.",al={"what is sapta","earth sapta meaning","what does the drink do"}},{q="Where is Kodo Rock located?",a="Kodo Rock is an imposing natural stone monolith situated directly east across the Red Cloud Mesa from Camp Narache.",al={"find kodo rock","kodo rock location","where is the shrine"}},{q="Who crafted the Earth Sapta for this rite?",a="The potion was prepared and granted by Seer Ravenfeather, an elder shaman residing at Camp Narache who oversees the training of young spiritual guides.",al={"who gives the sapta","seer ravenfeather sapta"}},{q="Why must the sapta be consumed at a holy site?",a="A sapta is inherently sacred and dangerous if misused. Tauren tradition demands it only be imbibed within consecrated shrines where elemental spirits naturally dwell and can receive the petitioner safely.",al={"why drink at kodo rock","can i drink sapta anywhere"}},{q="What happens after drinking the potion at Kodo Rock?",a="The potion lifts the mundane veil, revealing a Minor Manifestation of Earth. Speaking with the entity completes the bond between the shaman and the element of earth.",al={"what appears","minor manifestation of earth spawn"}},{q="What lesson does the earth spirit impart to initiates?",a="The spirit teaches that earth demands humble patience rather than combat. While elements like fire or air can be chaotic and violent, earth offers strength, endurance, and quiet foundation to the shaman's soul.",al={"earth spirit lesson","meaning of earth rite"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This rite forms the spiritual core of a tauren shaman's bond with the land, serving as the required bridge to earn communion with the earth and receive an Earth Totem.",["race:Tauren"]="Deep reverence for the Earth Mother makes the sapta ritual one of the most culturally revered coming-of-age tests for tauren spiritualists on the Red Cloud Mesa."},kw={"call of earth","camp narache","earth sapta","kodo rock","manifestation","mulgore","sapta","seer ravenfeather"},rel={"npc:seer-ravenfeather","npc:minor-manifestation-of-earth","subzone:kodo-rock","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","quest:99141"},m={start="Seer Ravenfeather",["end"]="Minor Manifestation of Earth",level="4",faction="Horde",id=1520},src={"Call of Earth (Mulgore 2)"}}
E["quest:1521"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="mulgore",s="This rite concludes a young tauren shaman's initial communion with the spirits of the land in Mulgore. After drinking the Sapta of Earth and speaking directly with a spirit manifestation, the initiate receives a fragment of raw stone to bring home. Returning to Camp Narache marks the fledgling mystic's formal bond with the first of the four elements.",h="This rite concludes a young tauren shaman's initial communion with the spirits of the land in Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Gift from the Mountain",b="Upon communing with the Minor Manifestation of Earth atop the crags near Red Cloud Mesa, the spirit yields a simple Rough Quartz. Though plain in appearance, the stone represents an elemental pact and the endurance of stone itself. The spirit reminds the young shaman that patience and strength are the core virtues demanded by the earth, promising that mutual respect will grant harmony with the land.",sp=0},{t="The First Totem",b="Returning to Camp Narache, Seer Ravenfeather uses the gifted quartz to fashion the shaman's very first Earth Totem. Far more than a decorative symbol of status, the totem serves as a permanent physical and spiritual anchor. Through this carved focus, the initiate channels the grounding, protective magic of the earth itself, taking the first true step along the fourfold elemental path.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Rough Quartz?",a="The quartz is a token given directly by the Minor Manifestation of Earth as proof of communion. Seer Ravenfeather uses it as the spiritual heart of your newly carved Earth Totem.",al={"why do i need rough quartz","what is the quartz for","rough quartz use"}},{q="Why is an Earth Totem so important to a shaman?",a="Totems act as conduits through which a shaman channels elemental power. Without the Earth Totem as a focus, a shaman cannot petition the spirits of the land to manifest their spells and protective wards.",al={"why do i need earth totem","what does earth totem do","earth totem lore"}},{q="Who is Seer Ravenfeather?",a="Seer Ravenfeather is the spiritual guide and shaman trainer stationed at Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. She oversees the elemental trials of young tauren, guiding them through their introductory visions and crafting their sacred implements.",al={"who is ravenfeather","seer ravenfeather lore","camp narache seer"}},{q="What are the four paths Seer Ravenfeather mentions?",a="She refers to the four primal elements that a shaman must master: Earth, Fire, Water, and Air. Earth is the foundation, representing endurance and steadfastness, and serves as the initiate's initial trial.",al={"four paths","four elements shaman","what are the paths"}},{q="Where does this communion take place?",a="The Minor Manifestation of Earth resides in the rugged cliffs bordering Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore, reachable only after drinking the specialized Earth Sapta.",al={"where do i find the earth spirit","manifestation location","where is the spirit"}},{q="Do other Horde shamans undertake a similar rite?",a="Yes. Orcs and Darkspear trolls in Durotar undertake their own version of the Call of Earth in the Valley of Trials, seeking communion with local elemental manifestations to carve their first totems.",al={"do orcs do call of earth","troll call of earth","do all shamans get earth totem"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This rite marks the acquisition of your very first totem. Carrying it grounds your spiritual identity and unlocks your ability to cast essential earth-aligned wards.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren culture regards the Earth Mother with profound reverence. Undertaking this trial binds an initiate directly to the living rock and soil of their ancestral homeland.",["faction:Horde"]="Shamans serve as vital spiritual anchors and advisors within the new Horde, bridging the mortal races with the volatile elemental forces of Kalimdor."},kw={"call of earth","camp narache","earth totem","minor manifestation of earth","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rough quartz","seer ravenfeather","tauren shaman"},rel={"npc:seer-ravenfeather","npc:minor-manifestation-of-earth","quest:1520","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Minor Manifestation of Earth",["end"]="Seer Ravenfeather",level="4",faction="Horde",id=1521},src={"Call of Earth (Mulgore 3)"}}
E["quest:3092"] = {t="quest",n="Etched Note",z="mulgore",s="The Etched Note is a formal summons directed to promising young tauren hunters in Camp Narache. Delivered on behalf of the trainer Lanka Farshot, the message calls the novice forward to begin mastering the practical and spiritual arts of the wild.",h="The Etched Note is a formal summons directed to promising young tauren hunters in Camp Narache.",sec={{t="A Summons to the Hunt",b="While training amidst the bluffs of Red Cloud Mesa, young tauren showing an aptitude for marksmanship and tracking are passed an etched message. Handed down through Rohaku Stonehoof, the note carries word that Lanka Farshot has taken notice of the youth's bloodline and raw capability, urging them to formalize their study beneath her watchful eye.",sp=0},{t="Tradition of the Plains",b="To the Shu'halo, a hunter is far more than a simple scout or warrior. Hunters sustain the tribes through their quarry, protect nomadic encampments, and live in close balance with the beasts of Kalimdor. By calling the novice into training, Lanka sets the groundwork for mastering weapons, understanding the creatures of Mulgore, and eventually undertaking the ancient art of taming wild companions.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Etched Note?",a="The Etched Note serves as a formal introduction connecting a novice tauren hunter with Lanka Farshot, the local class trainer stationed in Camp Narache.",al={"what does etched note do","etched note purpose","why did i get etched note"}},{q="Who delivers the note?",a="Rohaku Stonehoof passes the note along, explaining that a messenger from the hunter trainer sought out the young hunter moments prior.",al={"who gives etched note","etched note quest giver","rohaku note"}},{q="Where is Lanka Farshot located?",a="Lanka Farshot resides within Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa, standing near the hunter training grounds ready to instruct new recruits.",al={"where is lanka farshot","find lanka farshot","lanka location"}},{q="Why are hunters so revered among the tauren?",a="Hunters play a crucial role in tauren society by providing food, reading the untamed wilderness, and maintaining harmony with the spirits of the plains.",al={"tauren hunter lore","why are tauren hunters important"}},{q="What skills does this training introduce?",a="Lanka prepares the novice in foundational hunting techniques, including the use of ranged weaponry, understanding wild beasts, and ultimately the rituals required to tame animal companions.",al={"what does lanka teach","hunter basics tauren"}},{q="What is the next step after reading the note?",a="Reporting to Lanka Farshot completes the summons and begins The Hunter's Path, opening further instruction in tracking and survival.",al={"next quest after etched note","etched note follow up"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This summons marks your formal induction into the hunter tradition, preparing you to master marksman arts, wilderness survival, and beast companionship.",["race:Tauren"]="For the Shu'halo, hunting is a sacred duty honoring the Earth Mother through respect for the beasts that sustain the tribe across the plains of Mulgore."},kw={"camp narache","etched note","lanka farshot","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rohaku stonehoof","tauren hunter"},rel={"npc:lanka-farshot","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Rohaku Stonehoof",["end"]="Lanka Farshot",level="3",faction="Horde",id=3092},src={"Etched Note (quest)"}}
E["quest:3094"] = {t="quest",n="Verdant Note",z="mulgore",s="The Verdant Note is a formal summons delivered to promising young tauren in the Red Cloud Mesa. Entrusted by the elder Rohaku Stonehoof, the message bids budding protectors of the wilds to seek out the druidic mentor Gart Mistrunner at Camp Narache.",h="The Verdant Note is a formal summons delivered to promising young tauren in the Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="A Summons to the Path",b="Young tauren stepping into their responsibilities on the Red Cloud Mesa are quickly singled out for their natural gifts. Rohaku Stonehoof passes along this missive on behalf of a messenger sent by Gart Mistrunner. The note acts as an invitation into the ancient fold of tauren druidism, calling the initiate away from basic village errands to heed the whispering balance of the plains.",sp=0},{t="Voices of the Earthmother",b="Upon answering the summons, Gart Mistrunner warns the fledgling druid that ancestral and natural spirits have grown restless across Mulgore. Tauren druids are tasked to act as living avatars of nature, keeping harmony with the will of the Earthmother regardless of distant rivalries. While night elves also practice druidic arts, Gart cautions that their pride blinds them to the tauren's own deep-seated bond with the natural realm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Verdant Note?",a="It is an introductory summons delivered to young tauren druids in Camp Narache. It instructs the bearer to seek out the local trainer, Gart Mistrunner, to begin structured training in the druidic arts.",al={"verdant note","druid letter","mulgore druid quest note"}},{q="Who delivers the note to the player?",a="Rohaku Stonehoof hands the note to the young druid, mentioning that a messenger sent by Gart Mistrunner was recently searching for them.",al={"who gives verdant note","verdant note quest starter"}},{q="Where can Gart Mistrunner be found?",a="Gart Mistrunner resides in Camp Narache, stationed under the shelter of the camp's natural bluffs where he oversees training for novice tauren druids.",al={"gart mistrunner location","where is gart"}},{q="Why does Gart Mistrunner warn against the night elves?",a="Although both races revere nature and practice druidism through the Cenarion Circle, Gart observes that night elf arrogance often colors their perception of other cultures. He encourages his student to ignore such attitudes and remain focused solely on the Earthmother's wisdom.",al={"tauren and night elf druids","night elf pride druid"}},{q="Why are the spirits in Mulgore restless?",a="Gart Mistrunner senses disturbances stirring ancestral and nature spirits throughout the plains of Mulgore, marking an early sign that balancing the wild will be the new druid's lifelong responsibility.",al={"mulgore spirits restless","why are nature spirits angry"}},{q="What ability does Gart teach next after completing this quest?",a="After reporting to Gart Mistrunner, he directs the initiate toward learning Moonfire, beginning their mastery over primal celestial and natural magic.",al={"what comes after verdant note","druid quest follow up"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, druidism is not merely a school of magic, but a sacred covenant inherited from their mythic devotion to the Earthmother.",["class:Druid"]="This summons represents a druid's earliest induction into channeling the wild energies and restless spirits of Azeroth.",["faction:Horde"]="Tauren druids represent the primary bridge between the modern Horde and the natural mysteries of Kalimdor, providing spiritual balance to the faction's raw martial strength."},kw={"camp narache","druid","earthmother","gart mistrunner","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rohaku stonehoof","verdant note"},rel={"npc:gart-mistrunner","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:druid"},m={start="Rohaku Stonehoof",["end"]="Gart Mistrunner",level="3",faction="Horde",id=3094},src={"Verdant Note (quest)"}}
E["quest:3376"] = {t="quest",n="Break Sharptusk!",z="mulgore",s="Break Sharptusk! is a decisive rite of passage issued by Brave Windfeather on Red Cloud Mesa. To secure the tauren homeland against ongoing raids, young champions are sent east into Brambleblade Ravine to slay Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle. Eliminating the Bristleback chieftain strikes a severe blow against the quilboar encroaching upon Mulgore.",h="Break Sharptusk!",sec={{t="A Home Won in Blood",b="The grassy heights of Red Cloud Mesa have long served as a peaceful sanctuary where young tauren learn the traditions of the hunt and attunement with the Earth Mother. However, this peace is constantly contested. To the east lies Brambleblade Ravine, a thorn-choked gulch claimed by the hostile Bristleback quilboar tribe. The quilboar view the tauren as invaders stealing sacred ground, launching relentless skirmishes against the tribes attempting to establish a stable life in Mulgore.",sp=0},{t="The Threat of Thornmantle",b="The persistent attacks on the mesa are not aimless frenzy; they are coordinated under the brutal leadership of Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle. Residing in a fortified makeshift village deep within the ravine, Sharptusk directs his warriors and geomancers against the Shu'halo. Recognizing that the Bristleback will never cease their raids while their chief still draws breath, Brave Windfeather commands an aspiring hero to breach the ravine, strike down Thornmantle, and return with his severed head as proof.",sp=0},{t="The Lessons of the Hunt",b="For young tauren and their allies, hunting Sharptusk is more than a simple execution—it is an initiation into the harsh realities of survival. As Brave Windfeather teaches, the hunt against beasts teaches patience, but hunting an enemy cunning enough to hunt back tests courage, awareness, and martial discipline. Bringing down Sharptusk delivers justice for fallen kin and marks the hunter's readiness to venture out into the wider plains of Mulgore.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle?",a="Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle is the ruthless leader of the Bristleback quilboar inhabiting Brambleblade Ravine. Under his command, the tribe continuously stages raids and skirmishes against the tauren settlement on Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"who is sharptusk","sharptusk thornmantle lore","who is the quilboar chief"}},{q="Where can Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle be found?",a="Sharptusk resides deep within Brambleblade Ravine, situated on the eastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa. He oversees his tribe from their makeshift village nestled inside the thorn-covered canyon.",al={"where is sharptusk","sharptusk location","how to find sharptusk"}},{q="Who gives the quest Break Sharptusk!?",a="The quest is offered by Brave Windfeather, a seasoned tauren warrior who guards Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. He charges promising newcomers with proving their mettle by removing the Bristleback threat.",al={"quest giver break sharptusk","who starts break sharptusk","brave windfeather location"}},{q="Why do the Bristleback quilboar hate the tauren?",a="Quilboar believe that all land adorned with bloodstone and sacred thorn brambles belongs to them by divine right of their demigod creator, Agamaggan. They view the tauren founding settlements in Mulgore as encroaching trespassers on ancestral hunting grounds.",al={"why do quilboar attack","tauren quilboar conflict","why are quilboar hostile"}},{q="What proof does Brave Windfeather demand?",a="Brave Windfeather demands the severed head of Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle. Bringing the head proves beyond doubt that the leader has fallen and serves as a stern warning to any remaining Bristlebacks.",al={"what do i bring windfeather","sharptusk proof","quest objective sharptusk"}},{q="What is the cultural significance of this hunt for young tauren?",a="In tauren culture, true maturity comes from understanding the balance between hunter and hunted. Windfeather emphasizes that stalking a thinking, cunning adversary like Sharptusk teaches young warriors the gravity of mortal combat and the responsibility of defending the tribe.",al={"why kill sharptusk lore","tauren rite of passage sharptusk","meaning of the hunt"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Slaying Sharptusk Thornmantle is one of the final trials a young tauren undertakes before departing Camp Narache, proving they possess the resolve to defend the tribes from hostile neighbors.",["faction:Horde"]="By executing Chief Sharptusk, allies of the Horde demonstrate their commitment to stabilizing Mulgore and securing peaceful lands for their new tauren brothers.",["class:Hunter"]="For hunters, this quest is the ultimate early test: tracking a quarry that possesses its own cunning, weapons, and intent to hunt you in return."},kw={"brambleblade","bristleback","mulgore","narache","quilboar","red cloud mesa","sharptusk","thornmantle","windfeather"},rel={"subzone:brambleblade-ravine","topic:quilboar","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Brave Windfeather",level="5",faction="Horde",id=3376},src={"Break Sharptusk!"}}
E["quest:5844"] = {t="quest",n="Welcome!",z="mulgore",s="This errand serves as a festive greeting for adventurers arriving in Bloodhoof Village with a special gift voucher in hand. By presenting this token to Vorn Skyseer, travelers are granted a loyal companion creature to accompany them across Mulgore and beyond. It represents a warm, symbolic welcome to the land of the tauren.",h="This errand serves as a festive greeting for adventurers arriving in Bloodhoof Village with a special gift...",sec={{t="A Village Welcome",b="New wanderers stepping through Mulgore occasionally carry a distinct voucher tucked away in their belongings. Rather than an official decree from the high chieftain or an urgent call to arms against local predators, this token functions as an invitation. Those who present it to Vorn Skyseer in Bloodhoof Village are greeted as honored newcomers deserving of a friendly mascot for the long trails ahead.",sp=0},{t="The Skyseer's Favor",b="Vorn Skyseer, stationed in the heart of Bloodhoof Village, gladly accepts these rare tokens. Recognizing the bearer as an extraordinary newcomer embarking on their journey through Azeroth, the tauren offers them a small beast companion to walk alongside them through the grasslands and wherever else their path leads.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this quest?",a="The quest is a straightforward welcoming gesture. You turn in your starting gift voucher to Vorn Skyseer in Bloodhoof Village to claim a special vanity companion pet for your travels.",al={"what is welcome quest","why do welcome","what does this quest do"}},{q="Who is Vorn Skyseer?",a="Vorn Skyseer is a tauren resident situated in Bloodhoof Village within Mulgore. He acts as the local contact who accepts promotional gift vouchers and exchanges them for faithful companion animals.",al={"vorn skyseer identity","who is vorn","where is vorn"}},{q="Where does this quest begin and end?",a="The quest begins from a gift voucher item in your possession and finishes directly with Vorn Skyseer in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",al={"welcome turn in","where do i turn in welcome","bloodhoof voucher turn in"}},{q="Does this quest involve any combat or dangerous travel?",a="No. It simply involves speaking to Vorn Skyseer inside the safe perimeter of Bloodhoof Village to exchange your voucher.",al={"is welcome hard","do i have to fight for welcome","welcome danger"}},{q="Can members of the Alliance complete this version of the quest?",a="No. This specific voucher exchange takes place in Bloodhoof Village and is reserved for Horde travelers who find themselves in Mulgore.",al={"alliance welcome","can alliance do welcome","welcome faction"}},{q="Why is Vorn Skyseer handing out companions?",a="Vorn Skyseer views the token as a sign of a uniquely favored newcomer to the world of Azeroth, welcoming them to the tauren homelands with a loyal pet to ease the isolation of long travels.",al={"why do i get a pet","gift voucher pet lore","why companion"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren braves setting out from Red Cloud Mesa toward Bloodhoof Village, Vorn Skyseer's greeting provides an immediate reminder of their people's warm hospitality.",["faction:Horde"]="Bloodhoof Village stands as one of the central gathering grounds for Horde allies traversing central Kalimdor, making it an ideal post for extending a welcoming hand."},kw={"bloodhoof village","collector's edition","companion pet","mulgore","vorn skyseer","voucher","welcome"},rel={"npc:vorn-skyseer","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Bloodhoof Village Gift Voucher",["end"]="Vorn Skyseer",level="1",faction="Horde",id=5844},src={"Welcome! (Bloodhoof Village)"}}
E["quest:6061"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="mulgore",s="Taming the Beast is the foundational rite of passage for young tauren hunters in Bloodhoof Village. Guided by trainer Yaw Sharpmane, the hunter ventures into Mulgore to tame an adult plainstrider, taking the first step toward forging a spiritual bond with the creatures of the Earthmother.",h="Taming the Beast is the foundational rite of passage for young tauren hunters in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="A Sacred Bond with the Wild",b="For the tauren, hunting has never been purely about slaying beasts for meat and hides. Ancestral traditions hold that overcoming and communion with the wildlife of Mulgore allows a hunter to intake their spirits and draw closer to the Earthmother. Taming a companion beast elevates this connection, turning what could be an adversary into a lifelong partner aligned with the natural balance of the plains.",sp=0},{t="The First Test",b="Yaw Sharpmane begins an initiate's instruction not with ferocious apex predators, but with the swift and watchful plainstriders roaming Mulgore's grasslands. Armed with an enchanted taming rod, the hunter must face an adult plainstrider, calm its wild instincts, and earn its trust. This initial trial proves the hunter's resolve and prepares them for the more demanding beasts that follow.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this trial?",a="This quest is the first practical test for novice tauren hunters seeking to learn the art of animal companionship. By using a taming rod to pacify an adult plainstrider, the hunter learns the fundamentals of establishing a spiritual and working connection with beasts.",al={"why do i tame a plainstrider","purpose of quest","tauren pet quest purpose"}},{q="Who gives this quest and where are they located?",a="The quest is offered by Yaw Sharpmane, the hunter trainer situated in Bloodhoof Village within the central plains of Mulgore.",al={"who gives quest","quest giver location","yaw sharpmane location"}},{q="Do I keep the adult plainstrider permanently?",a="No. The beasts tamed during Yaw Sharpmane's initial three trials are temporary practice partners. Once you complete the entire training chain and master beast taming, you can venture out and tame any compatible companion permanently.",al={"is this pet permanent","do i keep the plainstrider","keep beast after quest"}},{q="Why do tauren view taming beasts as a spiritual act?",a="Tauren tradition teaches that interacting with and besting wild creatures allows hunters to intake their spirits and become one with the natural world. Taming a beast reflects mutual respect and unity with the creations of the Earthmother.",al={"tauren pet lore","earthmother taming lore","why tame beasts tauren"}},{q="Where can adult plainstriders be found?",a="Adult plainstriders roam widely throughout the rolling savannahs and valleys surrounding Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",al={"where are plainstriders","plainstrider location","find adult plainstrider"}},{q="What comes after this quest?",a="Returning the taming rod to Yaw Sharpmane leads into the next trial, where you are tasked with taming a stalker, followed by an aggressive swoop, before learning the art of permanent beast training.",al={"what is next","quest chain after this","next step taming"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This rite marks the transition from a fledgling tracker into a true beastmaster, teaching you how to commune with animals through the Earthmother's guidance.",["race:Tauren"]="Taming a beast is an ancestral practice central to the nomadic hunting heritage of Mulgore, strengthening the bond between hunter and nature."},kw={"bloodhoof village","earthmother","hunter quest","mulgore","plainstrider","taming","taming rod","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"npc:yaw-sharpmane","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","quest:6065","quest:6081","topic:tauren"},m={start="Yaw Sharpmane",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6061},src={"Taming the Beast (Tauren 1)"}}
E["quest:6065"] = {t="quest",n="The Hunter's Path",z="mulgore",s="The Hunter's Path is a vital milestone for young tauren hunters in Mulgore, directing them toward advanced training in beast companionship. Dispatched by their local mentors once their basic marksmanship and tracking are proven, novices are sent to Bloodhoof Village to seek the counsel of hunter trainer Yaw Sharpmane. This journey initiates the spiritual and practical trials required to tame and bond with wild beasts.",h="The Hunter's Path is a vital milestone for young tauren hunters in Mulgore, directing them toward advanced...",sec={{t="A Rite of Passage",b="To the nomadic Shu'halo, hunting is sacred rather than mere sport. A hunter must understand the balance of nature, honor the spirits of the plains, and show respect for the animals that sustain the tribes. When a young hunter demonstrates sufficient prowess in surviving the wild and wielding a bow or gun, their elders recognize that they are ready to step beyond solitary tracking. The directive to seek out seasoned mentors marks the threshold of true adulthood in tauren culture.",sp=0},{t="The Road to Bloodhoof Village",b="The path leads novitiates across the sweeping grasslands of Mulgore to Bloodhoof Village, a central gathering settlement nestled beside Stonebull Lake. There awaits Yaw Sharpmane, an experienced huntsman well-versed in the temperament of the local fauna. Speaking with Sharpmane is not a mere formality; it signals a hunter's readiness to undertake the demanding trials of beast taming, learning how to commune with predators rather than merely hunt them.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of The Hunter's Path?",a="The quest serves as the formal invitation for novice tauren hunters to begin their beast-taming trials. It directs the hunter to Bloodhoof Village to speak with Yaw Sharpmane, who oversees the rituals required to bond with an animal companion.",al={"why get this quest","hunters path purpose","hunter pet quest start"}},{q="Who sends you on this quest?",a="The quest is offered by tauren hunter trainers—such as those stationed in Camp Narache—once an aspiring hunter has gained enough experience and mastery over the basics of survival and marksmanship.",al={"who gives the hunters path","hunter trainer quest"}},{q="Who is Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Yaw Sharpmane is a respected tauren hunter trainer residing in Bloodhoof Village. He is entrusted with teaching young hunters the dangerous art of approaching, calming, and taming the wild beasts of Mulgore.",al={"who is sharpmane","yaw sharpmane role"}},{q="What happens after completing this quest?",a="Upon reporting to Yaw Sharpmane, he begins the series of 'Taming the Beast' trials. These tasks require the hunter to venture into the wild with a taming rod and pacify several native creatures before earning the right to train a permanent companion.",al={"what comes next","next quest after hunters path"}},{q="Why do tauren hunters bond with beasts instead of just hunting them?",a="In tauren tradition, beasts are kindred spirits of the Earth Mother. A bonded animal is not a servant, but a companion and partner on the hunt, embodying mutual respect and shared survival on the plains.",al={"tauren pet lore","why tame pets tauren"}},{q="Can hunters of other races take this specific quest?",a="This particular version of the quest is tailored specifically to tauren hunters training in Mulgore. Other Horde races, such as orcs and trolls, receive equivalent training directives suited to their home territories in Durotar.",al={"is this tauren only","can orcs do hunters path tauren"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This quest marks the moment an archer or rifleman transitions into a true beastmaster, taking the first step toward acquiring a loyal combat companion.",["race:Tauren"]="For the Shu'halo, learning to tame wild beasts is a spiritual extension of their reverence for nature and the Earth Mother's creation.",["faction:Horde"]="Horde hunters rely heavily on ferocious frontier beasts to act as front-line defenders and trackers in the harsh wilderness of Kalimdor."},kw={"beast","bloodhoof village","camp narache","hunter's path","mulgore","pet quest","taming","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"npc:yaw-sharpmane","quest:6061","quest:6081","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},m={["end"]="Yaw Sharpmane",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6065},src={"The Hunter's Path (Tauren)"}}
E["quest:6087"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="mulgore",s="This trial marks the second stage of a tauren hunter's initiation into the art of beast taming. Guided by hunter trainer Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof Village, the novice is charged with mastering the swift and fearless prairie stalker.",h="This trial marks the second stage of a tauren hunter's initiation into the art of beast taming.",sec={{t="The Stalker's Lessons",b="Having successfully mastered the defensive endurance of the adult plainstrider, Yaw Sharpmane challenges the young hunter to look beyond mere survival. The prairie stalker represents an entirely different hunting philosophy: relentless speed, pack coordination, and fearless aggression. To tame one requires approaching a cunning predator without breaking stride, mirroring the wolf's own decisive nature.",sp=0},{t="Muscle to Muscle",b="For the tauren, hunting with a beast is never about servitude or raw subjugation. Yaw Sharpmane stresses that the hunter and predator must fight as one cohesive unit, bound by mutual respect and shared rhythm. By bonding with the prairie stalker under the open skies of Mulgore, the hunter learns to complement a partner's assault and move in tandem during the heat of battle.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What animal does Yaw Sharpmane ask me to tame in this trial?",a="This second lesson requires you to tame a prairie stalker, one of the swift wolves that prowl the rolling plains of Mulgore.",al={"what beast do i tame","which pet is next","tame prairie stalker"}},{q="Why does a tauren hunter learn to tame wolves?",a="Yaw Sharpmane teaches that the prairie stalker embodies speed and fearlessness. By bonding with such a predator, the hunter internalizes those exact traits, learning how to coordinate attacks seamlessly alongside a swift hunting partner.",al={"why wolves","prairie stalker lore","why tame a wolf"}},{q="Where can prairie stalkers usually be found?",a="Prairie stalkers roam the grassy plains of central and northern Mulgore, frequently stalking the prairies surrounding Bloodhoof Village.",al={"prairie stalker location","where are prairie stalkers","find prairie stalker"}},{q="Do I get to keep the prairie stalker after the quest?",a="No. The taming rod creates only a temporary bond for training purposes. Once you complete the entire series of trials, you will gain the permanent ability to tame whatever wild companion you choose.",al={"can i keep the wolf","keep prairie stalker","is this my permanent pet"}},{q="Who is Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Yaw Sharpmane is the resident hunter trainer stationed in Bloodhoof Village. He guides young tauren through the spiritual and practical milestones required to master the wilds of Mulgore.",al={"who is yaw","sharpmane lore","yaw bloodhoof"}},{q="What comes after taming the prairie stalker?",a="Once Yaw Sharpmane confirms your mastery over the wolf, he will send you to complete the third and final trial of the chain, testing your resolve against the stealthy swoop.",al={"next quest","what is after prairie stalker","third taming quest"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This rite of passage bridges foundational survival techniques with offensive coordination, teaching you how to fight side-by-side with a fast-striking companion.",["race:Tauren"]="Unlike cultures that view pets purely as working animals, the Shu'halo view beast taming as forging a sacred kinship under the watchful grace of the Earthmother."},kw={"bloodhoof village","hunter trial","mulgore","prairie stalker","taming rod","taming the beast","tauren hunter","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"npc:yaw-sharpmane","subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:6061","quest:6081","quest:6065","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},m={start="Yaw Sharpmane",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6087},src={"Taming the Beast (Tauren 2)"}}
E["quest:6088"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="mulgore",s="This trial marks the final lesson in a young tauren hunter's training under Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof Village. By using a taming rod to bond with a wild swoop, the hunter proves mastery over creatures of the air and earns the sacred trust of the Earthmother. Completing this task grants the permanent knowledge required to tame and command a lifelong companion.",h="This trial marks the final lesson in a young tauren hunter's training under Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof...",sec={{t="Mastery of the Sky",b="Having already subdued ground-dwelling predators in the rolling plains of Mulgore, Yaw Sharpmane turns your focus toward the open heavens. The swoop is a fierce, sharp-beaked bird of prey known for its sweeping dives and intimidating demeanor. Bringing such a wild, airborne hunter under control tests your reflexes and your calm, demanding that you endure its talons while using the enchanted rod to align your spirit with its instincts.",sp=0},{t="The Earthmother's Gift",b="For the tauren, hunting and animal companionship are fundamentally spiritual pursuits rather than mere subjugation. By mastering creatures of both earth and sky, you demonstrate the balance and respect necessary to walk the plains without disrupting nature. Yaw Sharpmane recognizes this maturity, taking back his temporary taming rod and passing on the lasting techniques of calling, dismissing, and permanently taming a companion of your own choosing.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do I have to tame a swoop for the final trial?",a="Yaw Sharpmane wants to test your ability against creatures that inhabit the sky. Swoops possess sharp, swift attacks and an imposing presence, proving that a hunter can handle both aerial predators and ground beasts before earning true independence.",al={"why tame swoop","purpose of swoop taming","why a swoop"}},{q="Do I keep the swoop I tame during this quest?",a="No. The swoop is tamed temporarily using Yaw Sharpmane's taming rod as part of the ritual training. Once you complete the quest, you gain the actual ability to tame any wild beast you wish to keep permanently.",al={"do i keep the swoop","is the swoop my pet","can i keep this bird"}},{q="Who gives this quest and where are they located?",a="Yaw Sharpmane, the hunter trainer in Bloodhoof Village within central Mulgore, offers this quest as the third and final step of your initial beast taming trials.",al={"quest giver","who starts this","where is yaw sharpmane"}},{q="What happens after I return the taming rod?",a="Yaw Sharpmane teaches you the inherent abilities to tame, call, and dismiss beasts on your own. He will also direct you toward Thunder Bluff to learn how to properly train and care for your chosen companion.",al={"what do i learn next","rewards for finishing","what is after this quest"}},{q="Where can I find wild swoops in Mulgore?",a="Swoops roam the open grasslands and mesa edges throughout central Mulgore, often circling the plains just outside Bloodhoof Village.",al={"swoop locations","where are swoops","find a swoop"}},{q="Why is pet taming considered a rite of passage for tauren?",a="Tauren culture views beasts not as tools, but as fellow children of the Earthmother. Learning to bond with a companion requires patience, mutual respect, and spiritual balance rather than pure force.",al={"tauren hunter lore","hunter spiritual path","earthmother connection"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This quest completes the foundational training chain that unlocks permanent beast taming, marking the transition from an apprentice to a fully self-reliant scout of the wilds.",["race:Tauren"]="For the Shu'halo, bonding with a wild beast is an act of spiritual communion blessed by the Earthmother, carrying deep cultural significance across the plains of Mulgore."},kw={"bloodhoof village","earthmother","hunter pet","mulgore","swoop","taming rod","taming the beast","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"quest:6081","quest:6065","quest:6061","npc:yaw-sharpmane","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},m={start="Yaw Sharpmane",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6088},src={"Taming the Beast (Tauren 3)"}}
E["quest:6089"] = {t="quest",n="Training the Beast",z="mulgore",s="Training the Beast is the culminating rite of passage for young tauren hunters in Mulgore. After demonstrating the spiritual patience and resolve required to soothe the wild beasts of the plains, hunters are sent to Thunder Bluff to learn the lifelong discipline of bonding with, nourishing, and commanding a permanent animal companion.",h="Training the Beast is the culminating rite of passage for young tauren hunters in Mulgore.",sec={{t="The Path to the Great Bluff",b="Having successfully tested their spirit against the dangerous predators of Mulgore—plainstriders, prairie stalkers, and swoops—tauren hunters are judged ready for the true mantle of the hunter. Yaw Sharpmane, the mentor in Bloodhoof Village, entrusts the novice with a journey to Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff. There stands Holt Thunderhorn, a legendary figure among the shu'halo whose reputation for communing with the wilds is surpassed only by his reverence for the Earthmother.",sp=0},{t="A Sacred Bond",b="To the tauren, a beast is not a simple weapon or thrall; it is a partner granted through harmony with the natural world. Holt Thunderhorn imparts the vital knowledge required to sustain this partnership. A hunter must learn not only how to guide the creature's combat instincts, but how to nourish its body through regular feeding, mend its injuries, and call its spirit back should it fall defending its companion on the plains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who do I speak to for this quest?",a="You must speak with Holt Thunderhorn, a master hunter located atop Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff. Yaw Sharpmane directs you to him once your initial trials of taming wild beasts in Mulgore are complete.",al={"quest turn in","who is holt thunderhorn","where is holt"}},{q="What skills do tauren hunters learn upon completing this quest?",a="Holt Thunderhorn teaches you how to train your pet in new abilities, how to feed your companion to keep it loyal and content, and how to revive your pet should it perish in combat.",al={"what abilities do i get","quest reward skills","what does holt teach"}},{q="Where does this quest fit in the tauren hunter chain?",a="This is the final quest in the sequence. It follows The Hunter's Path and the three-part trial of Taming the Beast, where the hunter temporarily pacifies an adult plainstrider, a prairie stalker, and a swoop across the plains.",al={"hunter quest chain","pet quest mulgore","training the beast chain"}},{q="Why does Holt Thunderhorn hold such high standing among tauren hunters?",a="Holt has spent a lifetime testing himself against Azeroth's most ferocious beasts, commanding multiple powerful companions while maintaining a deep spiritual bond with the Earthmother. His wisdom makes him the principal teacher for young shu'halo hunters in Thunder Bluff.",al={"holt thunderhorn lore","who is holt"}},{q="Can hunters tame their permanent pet before turning in this quest?",a="You already have the power to tame wild beasts from the previous trial, but until you speak to Holt Thunderhorn, you lack the knowledge to feed, revive, or teach skills to your chosen companion.",al={"can i tame a pet now","when do i get my pet","do i need training the beast"}},{q="Why is pet care tied to the Earthmother in tauren culture?",a="Tauren see all living creatures as children of the Earthmother. A hunter does not dominate a beast through sheer force of will, but enters into a spiritual covenant where the hunter provides food and care in exchange for the animal's loyalty and protection.",al={"tauren pet lore","earthmother beasts"}}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This rite marks the transition from fighting alone to fighting beside a loyal companion, completing your fundamental training as a warden of the wilderness.",["race:Tauren"]="For the shu'halo, taking an animal companion into battle carries a sacred duty of stewardship and respect for the creations of the Earthmother."},kw={"beast training","holt thunderhorn","hunter rise","mulgore","tauren hunter","thunder bluff","training the beast","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"npc:holt-thunderhorn","npc:yaw-sharpmane","quest:6061","quest:6065","subzone:hunter-rise","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},m={start="Yaw Sharpmane",["end"]="Holt Thunderhorn",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6089},src={"Training the Beast (Tauren)"}}
E["quest:743"] = {t="quest",n="Dangers of the Windfury",z="mulgore",s="Dangers of the Windfury is a rite of martial readiness offered by Ruul Eagletalon in Bloodhoof Village. Young tauren and Horde adventurers are tasked with battling the savage Windfury harpies nesting in the southeastern cliffs of Mulgore. Hunting these feral predators serves as an essential test of skill and survival before journeying beyond the tribal homeland.",h="Dangers of the Windfury is a rite of martial readiness offered by Ruul Eagletalon in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="A Teacher's Assessment",b="In Bloodhoof Village, the veteran warrior Ruul Eagletalon observes all travelers who pass through the heart of Mulgore. For those coming of age or seeking to prove themselves ready for wider adventures, Ruul demands tangible proof of their combat instincts. Survival beyond the sheltered valleys of Mulgore requires poise against aggressive, cunning foes, and Ruul uses the land's fiercest native predators to measure a youth's mettle.",sp=0},{t="The Windfury Threat",b="The Windfury harpies roost along the mountainous southeastern rim of Mulgore, safely off the beaten trails but constantly posing a threat to wandering wildlife and isolated travelers. Vicious and relentlessly hungry for fresh flesh, these avian terrors rely on swift swoops and razor-sharp claws. Defeating them and claiming their talons demonstrates not only the power to defeat an entrenched threat, but the speed required to counter aerial raiders.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Ruul Eagletalon?",a="Ruul Eagletalon is an experienced tauren warrior and teacher residing in Bloodhoof Village. He mentors young adventurers, offering trials to ensure they possess the strength, reflexes, and fortitude needed before setting out into the wider, more perilous corners of Kalimdor.",al={"ruul eagletalon","who gives dangers of the windfury","ruul"}},{q="Where are the Windfury harpies found in Mulgore?",a="The Windfury harpies roost along the southeastern mountains of Mulgore. They make their crude nests high along the crags and foothill slopes, secluded away from the main road that cuts through the valley.",al={"windfury harpy location","where are windfury harpies","windfury nest location"}},{q="Why are the Windfury harpies considered natural enemies of the tauren?",a="Harpies are notoriously aggressive, territorial scavengers that attack anyone trespassing near their roosts. In Mulgore, the Windfury flock is known for its insatiable appetite for fresh meat and relentless aggression toward travelers and grazers alike, making them a perpetual menace to the plains.",al={"why do tauren fight harpies","harpy threat mulgore","windfury lore"}},{q="What proof does Ruul Eagletalon demand from the hunt?",a="Ruul asks adventurers to retrieve eight Windfury Talons directly from the slain harpies. Severing and returning these talons demonstrates mastery over the harpies' primary natural weapons.",al={"dangers of the windfury proof","what to collect for ruul","windfury quest objective"}},{q="Why must adventurers undergo this trial before leaving Mulgore?",a="Ruul views the fierce Windfury harpies as an ideal benchmark for martial preparedness. If an adventurer cannot handle the speed, ferocity, and claws of a harpy, they stand little chance against the deadlier dangers brewing beyond Mulgore's defensive bluffs.",al={"purpose of the trial","why do dangers of the windfury","ruul's test"}},{q="Where is Bloodhoof Village located?",a="Bloodhoof Village is situated in central Mulgore around the waters of Stonebull Lake, south of the Great Gate and Thunder Bluff. It serves as the primary hub of culture, trade, and training for tauren travelers moving across the plains.",al={"bloodhoof village location","where is bloodhoof","ruul location"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, clearing Windfury nests is both an act of defending the plains and a sacred passage into acknowledged adulthood under the watchful eyes of village elders.",["class:Warrior"]="Ruul Eagletalon's challenge is especially meaningful to aspiring warriors, who must learn to parry swift diving strikes and overpower vicious aerial ambushers with grounded discipline.",["faction:Horde"]="Allies of the tauren who undertake Ruul's trial demonstrate their dedication to safeguarding the Horde's heartland while earning the enduring respect of Bloodhoof Village."},kw={"bloodhoof village","dangers of the windfury","harpies","mulgore","ruul eagletalon","tauren trial","windfury","windfury talons"},rel={"npc:ruul-eagletalon","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Ruul Eagletalon",faction="Horde",id=743},src={"Dangers of the Windfury"}}
E["quest:745"] = {t="quest",n="Sharing the Land",z="mulgore",s="Assigned by Baine Bloodhoof in Bloodhoof Village, this task sends young Horde adventurers to curb the destructive poaching of the local Palemane gnolls. By culling their tanners, skinners, and poachers, you protect Mulgore's wildlife and learn the tauren philosophy of living in balance with the Earth Mother.",h="Assigned by Baine Bloodhoof in Bloodhoof Village, this task sends young Horde adventurers to curb the...",sec={{t="A Land Out of Balance",b="The tauren hold deep reverence for the natural world, taking from it only what is necessary to survive and honoring the Earth Mother in all things. The Palemane gnolls, having settled south of Bloodhoof Village and in a cavern to the west, harbor no such respect. Rather than hunting in moderation, they aggressively butcher wildlife across the plains, leaving stripped carcasses and upset ecosystems in their wake. For a people striving to build a peaceful homeland in Mulgore, this reckless slaughter cannot be tolerated.",sp=0},{t="When Words Fail",b="Baine Bloodhoof initially hoped to resolve the matter peacefully, attempting to open dialogue with the encroaching gnolls. The Palemanes rejected every overture, answering diplomacy with hostility and continued devastation. Seeing that negotiation is futile, Baine turns to force. Young braves are sent directly to their hunting camps to put down the poachers, tanners, and skinners before the grasslands suffer permanent damage.",sp=0},{t="The Circle of Life and Death",b="This mission serves as a rite of passage and a moral lesson for growing champions. Upon returning from the culling, Baine reminds the adventurer that taking life must never be done out of malice, but out of necessity to maintain harmony. Life and death exist as an interconnected circle where one cannot thrive without the other, a foundational truth every guardian of Mulgore must carry forward.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives this quest?",a="Baine Bloodhoof, son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, offers this task in Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore.",al={"quest giver","who starts sharing the land","baine quest"}},{q="Why are the tauren targeting the Palemane gnolls?",a="The Palemane gnolls hunt Mulgore's wildlife recklessly, depleting the herds without honoring the natural balance. They also refused all tauren attempts at peaceful communication.",al={"why kill gnolls","palemane problem","what did gnolls do"}},{q="Where are these gnolls located?",a="The Palemanes maintain camps to the south of Bloodhoof Village and dwell within the Palemane Rock cave system to the west.",al={"gnoll locations","palemane camp","where to find palemanes"}},{q="Did the tauren try to make peace first?",a="Yes. Baine notes that the tauren made several attempts to communicate and share the land, but the gnolls disrespected and rejected every peaceful overture.",al={"did tauren talk to gnolls","palemane diplomacy"}},{q="What lesson is Baine trying to teach here?",a="Baine wants young defenders to understand that death and life exist as an unbreakable circle. While taking a life is sometimes necessary to defend the balance, it must always be done with deep respect for the Earth Mother.",al={"baine lesson","meaning of sharing the land"}},{q="Who are the Palemane gnolls?",a="They are a primitive, hyena-like tribe native to Mulgore known for their pale fur, territorial aggression, and complete disregard for the harmony of the plains.",al={"who are palemanes","palemane tribe"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, answering Baine's call is an essential step in learning the sacred duty of defending Mulgore and honoring the Earth Mother's balance.",["class:Hunter"]="The Palemane skinners and poachers represent the antithesis of a true hunter, slaughtering wild creatures with cruelty and waste rather than respect.",["class:Druid"]="Druids recognize that unchecked poaching disrupts the spirit of the plains, requiring immediate intervention to heal the land's harmony.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting Baine Bloodhoof strengthens the bond between new Horde adventurers and the leadership of the fledgling tauren nation."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","gnolls","mulgore","palemane","poachers","sharing the land","skinners","tanners"},rel={"npc:baine-bloodhoof","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:gnoll"},m={start="Baine Bloodhoof",level="6",faction="Horde",id=745},src={"Sharing the Land (Classic)"}}
E["quest:746"] = {t="quest",n="Dwarven Digging",z="mulgore",s="Dwarven Digging is a quest in Mulgore given by Baine Bloodhoof in Bloodhoof Village. Concerned that dwarven excavations will enrage the native beasts and defile the sacred earth, Baine tasks young Horde defenders with sabotaging their efforts by confiscating and shattering their pickaxes.",h="Dwarven Digging is a quest in Mulgore given by Baine Bloodhoof in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="Trespassers in the Hills",b="Northwest of Bloodhoof Village, dwarven prospectors have begun staking out a dig site in the rolling hills of Mulgore. The Explorer's League and its excavation crews seek titan relics and earthen secrets across Kalimdor, but their operations rarely respect indigenous lands or customs. To the tauren, the earth is a living mother to be revered, not stripped and hollowed for stones and metal.",sp=0},{t="Defending the Sacred Earth",b="Baine Bloodhoof recognizes that the dwarven machinery and relentless blasting threaten the fragile harmony of the plains. Beyond the physical destruction, the commotion agitates the local wildlife, stirring the plains kodos into frenzy. By smashing the prospectors' tools at their own forges, the tauren hope to send a blunt message and stall the encroachment before lasting harm is done.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are dwarves digging in Mulgore?",a="Dwarven prospectors believe hidden secrets and artifacts lie deep within the earth of central Kalimdor. However, their disregard for tribal territory and violent mining methods have brought them into direct conflict with the tauren.",al={"dwarves in mulgore","why are prospectors here","dwarven excavation mulgore"}},{q="Who gives the quest Dwarven Digging?",a="The quest is given by Baine Bloodhoof, the young son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, located at Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore.",al={"quest giver dwarven digging","who starts dwarven digging"}},{q="Why does Baine want their tools smashed instead of just stolen?",a="Baine wants to render the equipment utterly useless to immediately halt the excavation. Destroying the tools at the forges sends a clear symbolic and practical message that the land will not yield its secrets through brute force and desecration.",al={"why break the picks","smashing dwarven tools"}},{q="How does the digging affect the wildlife of Mulgore?",a="The loud digging and deep scarring of the ground severely agitates the native creatures, particularly the massive kodos roaming the hills, turning peaceful herds irritable and aggressive.",al={"angry kodos","why are beasts mad"}},{q="Where can the picks be smashed?",a="The picks can be hammered into broken scrap at any functional forge. Players can use the forge located directly at the dwarven excavation camp itself, or carry them to the blacksmiths in Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is the forge","smash prospector picks forge"}},{q="Does this quest tie into the larger Explorer's League presence in Kalimdor?",a="Yes. Dwarf expeditions from Ironforge have established outposts across Kalimdor, including Mulgore, the Barrens, and Southern Kalimdor, frequently inciting skirmishes with Horde races over territorial sovereignty and sacred grounds.",al={"explorers league mulgore","ironforge in kalimdor"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Earth Mother's body is sacred, and seeing foreign intruders bore into Mulgore's rolling steppes is an affront that demands an immediate, firm response.",["faction:Horde"]="Alliance-aligned dwarves encroaching on Horde heartlands undermines the fragile peace established after the Third War, showing blatant disregard for tribal borders.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman understand that disturbing the bedrock through careless excavation upsets the elemental balance and provokes unrest among the land's spirits and creatures.",["profession:Mining"]="While miners revere the wealth hidden in stone, dwarven methods in Mulgore bypass respect and harmony, opting for reckless destruction."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","dwarf","forge","kodo","mulgore","picks","prospector"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","npc:baine-bloodhoof","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Baine Bloodhoof",level="8",faction="Horde",id=746},src={"Dwarven Digging"}}
E["quest:747"] = {t="quest",n="The Hunt Begins",z="mulgore",s="The Hunt Begins is a foundational rite of passage for young adventurers in Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. Tasked by Grull Hawkwind, initiates are sent to hunt local plainstriders to provide meat and feathers for the village. Fulfilling this task marks the first step toward earning the respect of Chief Hawkwind and eventually gaining entry into Thunder Bluff.",h="The Hunt Begins is a foundational rite of passage for young adventurers in Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="A Tribe's Sustenance",b="On the heights of Red Cloud Mesa, survival and tradition intertwine. The tauren hunt out of necessity to feed and clothe their people, while also viewing the stalk and chase as a vital test of discipline and sport. When the stores of Camp Narache run lean, the community looks to its rising youth to gather fresh plainstrider meat for cooking and sturdy feathers for tribal garments.",sp=0},{t="Earning Recognition",b="No aspiring hero simply walks off Red Cloud Mesa into the wider reaches of Mulgore without proving their worth. Grull Hawkwind serves as a primary judge of character for the newcomers, insisting that one must prove their capability before stepping before his father, Chief Hawkwind. Bringing in a clean hunt demonstrates not only self-reliance, but devotion to supporting the tribe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives this quest?",a="Grull Hawkwind offers this task in Camp Narache. He is the son of the village leader, Chief Hawkwind, and acts as an early mentor to young hunters and adventurers on Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"quest giver","who starts the hunt begins","grull hawkwind"}},{q="Why are plainstriders hunted instead of other beasts?",a="Plainstriders are plentiful across the mesa and offer both nutritious meat to replenish dwindling food stores and durable feathers used in traditional tauren clothing and adornments.",al={"why plainstriders","plainstrider meat and feathers","hunting plainstriders"}},{q="Why do I have to prove myself before seeing Chief Hawkwind?",a="Tauren society places high value on practical contribution and hunting prowess. Grull Hawkwind requires initiates to demonstrate basic survival skills and loyalty to the village before they are granted audience and deeper responsibilities by the chief.",al={"why prove to chief hawkwind","chief hawkwind approval","why hunt first"}},{q="What is the broader goal of these trials on Red Cloud Mesa?",a="The mesa acts as a proving ground. Once a young tauren proves their capability to Chief Hawkwind and the elders of Camp Narache, they are deemed ready to descend into the wider plains of Mulgore and enter the capital city of Thunder Bluff.",al={"mesa trials purpose","why am i on red cloud mesa","leaving red cloud mesa"}},{q="What comes after this hunt?",a="Successfully bringing back the meat and feathers earns Grull's approval, leading directly into 'The Hunt Continues', where initiates track larger, more elusive game across the mesa.",al={"next quest","followup","what is after the hunt begins"}},{q="Do tauren only hunt for survival?",a="While sustenance and resources like leather, bone, and feathers are essential, tauren also practice hunting as sport and spiritual communion, sharpening instincts that keep their tribes alert and strong.",al={"tauren hunting philosophy","hunt for sport","why do tauren hunt"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young shu'halo, taking down your first mesa plainstriders is an essential rite of passage, confirming your bond with the land and your duty to feed Camp Narache.",["class:Hunter"]="Stalking the swift plainstriders of Red Cloud Mesa serves as the earliest practical exercise in marksmanship, tracking, and honoring the beasts that sustain you."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","feathers","grull hawkwind","hunt begins","mulgore","plainstrider","red cloud mesa"},rel={"npc:grull-hawkwind","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren"},m={start="Grull Hawkwind",level="2",faction="Horde",id=747},src={"The Hunt Begins"}}
E["quest:748"] = {t="quest",n="Poison Water",z="mulgore",s="Poison Water begins the tauren effort to purge pollution from Mulgore's sacred wells. Entrusted by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village, young Horde defenders gather natural components from the plains to craft a restorative ritual totem.",h="Poison Water begins the tauren effort to purge pollution from Mulgore's sacred wells.",sec={{t="A Land Desecrated",b="The grassy plains of Mulgore are sacred ground to the tauren, but insidious forces have begun threatening their vital lifeblood. Mull Thunderhorn, an elder stationed in Bloodhoof Village, discovers that goblin interlopers and their lackeys have tainted the precious water wells that sustain the tribes and the local wildlife. The pollution strikes directly at the spiritual heart of the nomadic people who recently settled here.",sp=0},{t="Gathering Nature's Essence",b="To counteract industrial contaminants, Mull relies on traditional tauren shamanism rather than crude remedies. Cleansing the Winterhoof Water Well requires crafting a potent ritual totem imbued with the vitality of Mulgore's indigenous predators. He sends hunters to track prairie wolves for their paws and mature plainstriders for their talons across the southwestern plains, enlisting the strength of the land to heal its own wounds.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who poisoned the water wells in Mulgore?",a="Goblin encroachers and their hirelings are responsible for fouling the sacred watering holes across Mulgore. Their destructive industrial operations and disregard for natural balance threaten both the tauren tribes and local wildlife.",al={"who tainted the wells","who poisoned mulgore","who polluted the water"}},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need wolf paws and plainstrider talons?",a="Mull crafts traditional cleansing totems using parts harvested from Mulgore's native beasts. By harnessing the innate spirits and strength of the plains' predators, he creates a focus powerful enough to conduct a water-cleansing ritual.",al={"why wolf paws","why plainstrider talons","totem materials"}},{q="Which well does this quest prepare to cleanse?",a="This task gathers materials specifically to craft the cleansing totem for the Winterhoof Water Well, the first in a line of polluted wells that require ritual purification.",al={"what well is this for","which water well"}},{q="Where is Mull Thunderhorn located?",a="Mull Thunderhorn resides in Bloodhoof Village, the primary tauren settlement situated in central Mulgore around the waters of Stonebull Lake.",al={"mull thunderhorn location","where is mull","where to turn in poison water"}},{q="Where can the necessary wolves and plainstriders be found?",a="Prairie wolves and adult plainstriders wander the rolling plains southwest of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"where to find prairie wolves","plainstrider location","hunting grounds"}},{q="What happens after Mull receives the paws and talons?",a="Mull binds the harvested components into a Winterhoof Cleansing Totem and tasks you with carrying it directly to the Winterhoof Water Well to perform the purification rite.",al={"next quest after poison water","what do i do next"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The desecration of fresh water strikes at the core of the Earth Mother's gifts, making the cleansing ritual a fundamental duty for any tauren coming of age in Mulgore.",["class:Shaman"]="Restoring polluted waters through beast-empowered totems illustrates foundational tauren shamanism: working alongside the local wildlife to mend elemental disturbances.",["class:Druid"]="Goblins poisoning fresh springs represents a direct assault on the natural balance, demanding an immediate response to preserve Mulgore's fragile flora and fauna."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing totem","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","plainstrider","poison water","prairie wolf","winterhoof water well"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:winterhoof-water-well","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="5",faction="Horde",id=748},src={"Poison Water (Classic)"}}
E["quest:749"] = {t="quest",n="The Ravaged Caravan",z="mulgore",s="Morin Cloudstalker directs travelers to examine the wreckage of an ambushed Venture Company caravan near the northern shore of Stonebull Lake. Although the tauren torched the convoy in retribution for lost comrades, goblin salvagers returned to recover intact cargo. Investigating the surviving supply crates reveals the true industrial scale of the Venture Company's operations in Mulgore.",h="Morin Cloudstalker directs travelers to examine the wreckage of an ambushed Venture Company caravan near the...",sec={{t="Retribution by the Lake",b="The charred remnants along the northern bank of Stonebull Lake are the result of a swift strike by the tauren. Grieving the loss of two companions killed in an earlier skirmish, Morin Cloudstalker and his warriors ambushed an armed Venture Company supply convoy. Intending to obliterate the intruders' efforts, the tauren torched the wagons and abandoned the smoking ruin, presuming fire would consume whatever the goblins sought to haul.",sp=0},{t="Unfinished Business",b="The tauren soon noticed Venture Company salvagers scouring the blackened debris, suggesting their fire had failed to destroy the most crucial cargo. Rather than risk further lives blindly, Morin dispatches scouts to inspect the wreckage. Among the soot-stained wreckage rest intact crates marked with the insignia of the Venture Company Mining Division, packed and destined for central processing facilities.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why was the caravan destroyed?",a="Morin Cloudstalker and his tauren warriors ambushed and torched the caravan in retaliation after losing two of their scouts in a previous clash with the Venture Company.",al={"who destroyed the caravan","why is the caravan burned","caravan attack"}},{q="Where is the Ravaged Caravan located?",a="The burnt wagons sit along the northern shore of Stonebull Lake, east of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",al={"caravan location","where is the caravan","ravaged caravan spot"}},{q="Why are the goblins returning to the wreckage?",a="The Venture Company sent salvagers because fire failed to destroy all their cargo. They are attempting to retrieve durable supply crates stamped for shipment to their central processing operations.",al={"why are salvagers here","venture co salvagers","what do the goblins want"}},{q="What is inside the supply crates?",a="The crates bear the insignia of the Venture Company Mining Division and contain mineral resources labeled for refinement and processing at their primary industrial plants.",al={"crate contents","what is in the crate","sealed supply crate"}},{q="Who is Morin Cloudstalker?",a="Morin Cloudstalker is a tauren scout and tracker stationed east of Bloodhoof Village, tasked with monitoring hostile incursions across central Mulgore, particularly from the Venture Company.",al={"who is morin","morin cloudstalker role"}},{q="What does this caravan reveal about the Venture Company?",a="It shows that the Venture Company's presence in Mulgore is not a mere exploratory team, but an organized industrial extraction effort complete with armed supply lines and centralized refining networks.",al={"venture co mulgore presence","why are goblins in mulgore"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The desecration of the plains by Venture Company machines and timber-clearing strikes directly at your ancestral homeland, making the recovery and destruction of their supplies vital to the defense of Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="Unchecked corporate expansion into Mulgore threatens the stability of Thunder Bluff, requiring Horde scouts to disrupt Venture Company logistics.",["profession:Mining"]="The sturdy crates and logistics notes demonstrate the Venture Company's aggressive mineral surveying and extraction operations underway across Mulgore."},kw={"morin cloudstalker","mulgore","ravaged caravan","stonebull lake","supply crate","venture co","venture company"},rel={"npc:morin-cloudstalker","subzone:ravaged-caravan","subzone:stonebull-lake","quest:764","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Morin Cloudstalker",["end"]="Sealed Supply Crate",faction="Horde",id=749},src={"The Ravaged Caravan (Horde) (1)"}}
E["quest:751"] = {t="quest",n="The Ravaged Caravan",z="mulgore",s="Investigating a broken transport in Mulgore reveals that goblin industrialists have established an illegal foothold in tauren territory. By recovering documents from a sealed crate, scouts confirm the Venture Company is systematically stripping the land's mineral wealth. This discovery rallies the Bloodhoof Outrunners to mount an active defense of their ancestral plains.",h="Investigating a broken transport in Mulgore reveals that goblin industrialists have established an illegal...",sec={{t="Evidence of Exploitation",b="The wreckage of a ravaged caravan east of Bloodhoof Village tells an unsettling story. Within a sealed supply crate lies a dense cache of mined ore and minerals alongside a bound sheaf of Venture Company documents. This paperwork provides irrefutable proof that the greedy Venture Company has begun an unpermitted mining operation deep in the tranquil valleys of Mulgore.",sp=0},{t="The Outrunners' Vigil",b="Morin Cloudstalker and his fellow Outrunners have maintained a cautious watch over Mulgore's borders, mindful of outside factions looking to exploit their peaceful homeland. Delivering the retrieved documents confirms Morin's worst fears: smoke rising over the eastern foothills signals an escalating commercial invasion. The tauren are left with no choice but to push back against the defilers before their hills are gutted entirely.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is inside the sealed supply crate?",a="Prying open the crate reveals a packed assortment of mined ore, raw minerals, and a sheaf of Venture Company documents outlining their operations.",al={"supply crate loot","what is in the crate","crate contents"}},{q="Who is Morin Cloudstalker?",a="Morin Cloudstalker is a tauren scout and leader among the Bloodhoof Outrunners stationed near Bloodhoof Village, tasked with patrolling Mulgore and tracking foreign threats.",al={"who is morin","morin cloudstalker role","outrunner morin"}},{q="Why is the Venture Company in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company seeks to ruthlessly exploit Mulgore's rich natural resources and veins of mineral ore, carving into the hills without regard for the earth or its native people.",al={"why are goblins here","venture co goal mulgore","what do goblins want"}},{q="Where did the caravan come from?",a="The caravan belonged to the Venture Company, transporting mined ore and operational paperwork out from their foothill operations before it was destroyed along the trail.",al={"whose caravan was it","caravan origin","who attacked the caravan"}},{q="Where does this investigation lead next?",a="Armed with the documents, Morin Cloudstalker directs warriors to directly confront the Venture Company's leadership and dismantle their mining operation in the eastern foothills.",al={"what comes next","next quest after ravaged caravan","following up on the caravan"},sp=1},{q="Why does this threaten the tauren?",a="The tauren view the land as sacred and revere the Earth Mother. The Venture Company's reckless strip mining and industrial pollution actively defile Mulgore's pristine landscape.",al={"why do tauren care","venture co threat","threat to mulgore"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, seeing crude industrial machinery tear into the ancestral plains of Mulgore is an affront to the Earth Mother and an act of war against their newfound peace.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde relies on the loyalty and stability of Mulgore; leaving foreign merchant cartels to despoil its hills would threaten the safety of Thunder Bluff itself.",["class:Druid"]="Druids hold the balance of the land sacred, making the destructive, smoke-choked strip mining of the Venture Company a desecration that must be cleansed.",["profession:Mining"]="The high-grade ores packed within the crate prove the eastern hills of Mulgore conceal rich subterranean veins that outsiders are racing to exploit."},kw={"bloodhoof village","morin cloudstalker","mulgore","outrunners","ravaged caravan","supply crate","venture co","venture co documents"},rel={"npc:morin-cloudstalker","quest:764","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Sealed Supply Crate",["end"]="Morin Cloudstalker",faction="Horde",id=751},src={"The Ravaged Caravan (Horde) (2)"}}
E["quest:752"] = {t="quest",n="A Humble Task",z="mulgore",s="A Humble Task is an early rite of duty given by Chief Hawkwind in Camp Narache. As the Chieftain attends to pressing tribal concerns on the Red Cloud Mesa, he asks a young adventurer to venture southeast and check on his elderly mother at the local water well. The request emphasizes communal responsibility, elder reverence, and the daily harmony essential to the tauren way of life.",h="A Humble Task is an early rite of duty given by Chief Hawkwind in Camp Narache.",sec={{t="Communal Harmony and Duty",b="In the shadow of Camp Narache, every member of the tribe is expected to pull their weight and share in the harmony of life. For young tauren coming of age, tribal duty is not measured solely in battle prowess against quillboars or predators; it begins with basic social responsibility. Chief Hawkwind demonstrates this ethos directly: bound to his post by administrative and defensive leadership, he places his trust in novice adventurers to watch over the community's vulnerable elders.",sp=0},{t="The Well Southeast of Narache",b="Mulgore's Red Cloud Mesa relies on vital natural water wells scattered beyond the perimeter of the encampment. Early in the morning, Greatmother Hawkwind left the village unattended to retrieve fresh water from the southeastern spring. In a land stalked by territorial wildlife and encroaching Bristleback quilboars, an elder remaining absent for hours raises quiet concern, prompting Hawkwind to send aid to ensure she is unharmed.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers this quest and where do I find them?",a="Chief Hawkwind, the leader of Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore, offers the quest. He stands in the central pavilion of the camp overseeing local affairs.",al={"who gives a humble task","where to pick up quest","chief hawkwind location"}},{q="Where is Greatmother Hawkwind located?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is located just southeast of Camp Narache, tending to the water well on the lower elevation of the mesa.",al={"greatmother hawkwind location","where is the mother","water well location"}},{q="Why doesn't Chief Hawkwind check on his mother himself?",a="Chief Hawkwind must remain at Camp Narache to oversee pressing tribal matters and guide young braves. As a chieftain, he relies on the tribe's collective responsibility and trusts rising adventurers to look after village affairs in his stead.",al={"why cant chief go","why doesn't hawkwind check","chief hawkwind busy"}},{q="Is Greatmother Hawkwind in danger at the well?",a="While the surrounding plains host aggressive mountain cougars and striders, Greatmother Hawkwind is found safe and sound beside the well, pleasantly surprised that someone went out of their way to check on an elder.",al={"is she safe","is greatmother attacked","danger at water well"}},{q="What cultural significance does this quest carry for the tauren?",a="Tauren culture places immense value on family, community cohesion, and reverence for elders. The quest teaches young heroes that true strength is rooted in humble service and protecting one's kin rather than chasing personal glory.",al={"tauren culture humble task","why is this a humble task","tauren elders"}},{q="What happens after finding Greatmother Hawkwind?",a="Once you speak with Greatmother Hawkwind, she completes the task and asks for further assistance in carrying the filled water pitcher back to the camp.",al={"next quest","a humble task follow up","what to do after finding mother"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren braves, answering the Chieftain's call demonstrates the core tenets of the Earth Mother's teachings: respect for elders and unhesitating commitment to the tribe's shared well-being.",["faction:Horde"]="The quiet, pastoral nature of Narache's tasks highlights how the tauren's tranquil communal philosophy contrasts with the harsher, survivalist grit found across the rest of the fledgling Horde."},kw={"a humble task","camp narache","chief hawkwind","greatmother hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","water well"},rel={"npc:chief-hawkwind","npc:greatmother-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Chief Hawkwind",["end"]="Greatmother Hawkwind",level="2",faction="Horde",id=752},src={"A Humble Task"}}
E["quest:753"] = {t="quest",n="A Humble Task",z="mulgore",s="A Humble Task is an introductory rite of service in Red Cloud Mesa, where an elder tests a fledgling adventurer's humility and diligence. By carrying fresh well water across the plains to Camp Narache's leader, young tauren learn that serving their people starts with the simplest duties.",h="A Humble Task is an introductory rite of service in Red Cloud Mesa, where an elder tests a fledgling...",sec={{t="Vitality of the Youth",b="Greatmother Hawkwind has walked many trails in her long life, and while her spirit remains steady, her legs no longer carry her across the plains with the speed of her youth. Stationed near the water well on the southern edges of Red Cloud Mesa, she tends to her responsibilities with patient resolve. When aspiring tribal defenders cross her path, she redirects their youthful energy toward practical community needs, offering them a chance to prove their worth through service rather than violence.",sp=0},{t="Service to the Tribe",b="The task asks the initiate to haul a filled water pitcher back northwest to Camp Narache and present it directly to the settlement's leader, Chief Hawkwind, who happens to be the Greatmother's son. In tauren society, survival across the arid plains of Mulgore demands shared labor and reliable supplies of fresh water. Demonstrating respect for the community's elders by shouldering their burdens is considered the cornerstone of becoming a true protector of the tribe.",sp=0},{t="A Path Toward Thunder Bluff",b="Chief Hawkwind sees more than a mere chore completed when a young wanderer arrives bearing water from his mother. In tauren custom, an individual's readiness to champion their people across wider lands begins with humility and an unprompted willingness to help. By demonstrating this quiet discipline, the initiate catches the chief's eye and earns recommendation for the ancient Rites of the Earthmother, the true spiritual tests that open the gates to Thunder Bluff.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Greatmother Hawkwind?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is an elder of the Hawkwind family stationed at the well in Red Cloud Mesa. She is the mother of Camp Narache's leader, Chief Hawkwind, and guides young tauren in learning humility and tribal values.",al={"greatmother hawkwind","who gives a humble task","hawkwind elder"}},{q="Where is the water well located?",a="The water well sits southeast of Camp Narache on the grassy plains of Red Cloud Mesa. Pitchers of fresh well water rest beside it for travelers and camp caretakers to carry back.",al={"water well location","where is the well","find water pitcher"}},{q="Who is Chief Hawkwind?",a="Chief Hawkwind is the chieftain overseeing Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. He directs the training and spiritual rites of young tauren, preparing them to venture out and serve the wider tribe in Mulgore.",al={"chief hawkwind","who is the chief","camp narache leader"}},{q="Why is this chore treated as an important test?",a="Tauren culture places great value on humility, community support, and respect for elders. Accomplishing unglamorous, everyday duties demonstrates that an adventurer cares about sustaining their people, not just personal glory in battle.",al={"why carry water","why humble task","is this quest important"}},{q="What comes after completing A Humble Task?",a="Completing this task proves your discipline to Chief Hawkwind, who then directs you to undertake the Rites of the Earthmother—a sequence of traditional challenges required of all tauren coming of age.",al={"what comes next","next quest","after humble task"}},{q="Where does this path eventually lead?",a="Earning the respect of the elders in Camp Narache prepares you to leave the secluded mesa behind, journeying into the wider plains of Mulgore and ultimately toward the high mesas of the capital, Thunder Bluff.",al={"future destination","where are we going","thunder bluff goal"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Fetching water for your elders embodies the foundational tauren virtue of communal care, showing you are ready to walk the path of the Earthmother.",["faction:Horde"]="The Hawkwind family's lessons illustrate how the tauren maintain solidarity and survival in Mulgore, forming a peaceful yet unyielding cornerstone of Thrall's Horde."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","greatmother hawkwind","humble task","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rites of the earthmother","water pitcher"},rel={"quest:752","npc:greatmother-hawkwind","npc:chief-hawkwind","quest:763","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Greatmother Hawkwind",["end"]="Chief Hawkwind",level="3",faction="Horde",id=753},src={"A Humble Task (2)"}}
E["quest:754"] = {t="quest",n="Winterhoof Cleansing",z="mulgore",s="Winterhoof Cleansing is a rite entrusted to Horde adventurers by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village. After goblin operations fouled the local springs, a consecrated totem must be carried into the Venture Company's territory to purify the Winterhoof Water Well. Restoring this spring is a vital first step in healing the poisoned waters of central Mulgore.",h="Winterhoof Cleansing is a rite entrusted to Horde adventurers by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="A Land Despoiled",b="The grassy plains of Mulgore rely on a delicate network of ancestral water wells to sustain both wild game and the scattered tauren tribes. Recently, ruthless industrial mercenaries of the Venture Company moved into the rolling hills southeast of Bloodhoof Village. Their operations have contaminated the local aquifers, leaving the Winterhoof Water Well rank and toxic. Wild beasts shun the tainted water, and the nearby camps face an impending crisis if the corruption spreads further through the earth.",sp=0},{t="The Ritual of Purifying Waters",b="To counter the industrial filth, Mull Thunderhorn carves and enchants a ritual cleansing totem infused with the blessings of the Earth Mother. However, delivering this remedy requires braving armed Venture Company goons who have staked their claim over the spring. By placing the totem directly at the wellhead, an adventurer dispels the foul runoff, allowing clean, life-giving water to rise once more from the depths of the earth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Winterhoof Water Well located?",a="The Winterhoof Water Well is located in the hills to the southeast of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. It is heavily patrolled by goblin laborers and mercenaries working for the Venture Company.",al={"winterhoof well location","where is winterhoof well","how to find winterhoof well"}},{q="Why was the water well poisoned?",a="The Venture Company established mining and excavation operations nearby, allowing toxic industrial runoff and refuse to seep directly into the aquifer, rendering the natural spring undrinkable for local wildlife and the tauren.",al={"who poisoned the water","why is the well dirty","venture company well"}},{q="Who is Mull Thunderhorn?",a="Mull Thunderhorn is a spiritual elder stationed in Bloodhoof Village. Deeply tuned to the health of the earth, he crafts ceremonial totems to undo the environmental devastation wrought by outside invaders.",al={"who is mull","mull thunderhorn role"}},{q="What happens after the well is cleansed?",a="Once the Winterhoof Water Well runs clear and wildlife begins drinking from it safely again, Mull Thunderhorn directs your attention toward other compromised wells in Mulgore, starting with the Thunderhorn Totem.",al={"what comes next","after winterhoof cleansing","what does mull do next"},sp=1},{q="Who are the guards at the well?",a="The well is occupied by laborers and hirelings employed by the Venture Company, an unscrupulous goblin-led syndicate that strips lands of natural resources with no regard for the local ecosystem.",al={"who guards winterhoof well","what enemies are at the well"}},{q="How does the cleansing totem work?",a="Carved with sacred tauren rites and warding rituals, the totem draws upon natural spirits and the blessings of the Earth Mother to purge unnatural chemical pollutants from the spring's source.",al={"cleansing totem lore","how does mull totem work"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The wells of Mulgore are sacred life-givers gifted by the Earth Mother; defending them against goblin greed is both a civic duty and a holy obligation for every tauren.",["class:Shaman"]="Channeling spirits through totems to reverse unnatural industrial defilement represents the core purpose of a shaman's bond with the elemental waters.",["class:Druid"]="Seeing the immediate return of thirsty wildlife to the purified spring proves the vital necessity of healing nature wherever civilization threatens to despoil it.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing Mulgore's fresh water supply stabilizes the heartlands of the newly settled tauren, strengthening the southern pillar of the young Horde."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","poison","totem","venture company","water well","winterhoof"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:winterhoof-water-well","subzone:bloodhoof-village","topic:venture-company","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="6",faction="Horde",id=754},src={"Winterhoof Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["quest:756"] = {t="quest",n="Thunderhorn Totem",z="mulgore",s="Thunderhorn Totem is a task given by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village to craft a sacred cleansing totem. To combat goblin-borne pollution spreading through Mulgore's water wells, young Horde champions must gather natural talons from plains predators to bind the land's primal strength into ritual wood.",h="Thunderhorn Totem is a task given by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village to craft a sacred cleansing totem.",sec={{t="Hunting the Plains Predators",b="As the Venture Company's industrial exploitation poisons Mulgore's precious water sources, Mull Thunderhorn works urgently to craft remedies. Creating the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem requires the natural essences of the plains' most focused hunters: prairie stalkers and flatland cougars. By taking the claws of these predators roaming east and west of Bloodhoof Village, the ritual channels the raw, uncorrupted predatory vigor of the wildlife directly into the totem.",sp=0},{t="The Nature of the Hunt",b="Tauren tradition sees a profound distinction between hunting for sustenance or spiritual balance and the wholesale plunder practiced by the goblins. Mull emphasizes that the purity with which native predators hunt is vital for purifying corrupted earth. Where goblin machinery defiles the springs with oil and industrial waste, the natural strength of the beasts contending with the land provides the spiritual catalyst needed to restore the Thunderhorn Water Well.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need predator claws?",a="Mull uses the claws of prairie stalkers and flatland cougars to channel the pure, primal predatory spirit of the plains into a cleansing totem. He believes the pure spirit of natural hunters counteracts artificial, industrial corruption.",al={"why claws","purpose of stalker claws","cougar claws purpose"}},{q="Who is corrupting the water in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company goblins operating in Mulgore are polluting the land and water. Their reckless resource extraction and mining rigs introduce toxic sludge into the aquifers feeding the tribal wells.",al={"who tainted the well","why is the well poisoned","what poisoned mulgore"}},{q="Where are the prairie stalkers and flatland cougars found?",a="Both beasts roam the open plains of central Mulgore, primarily spread out across the rolling savannas directly east and west of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"stalker location","where to find cougars","cougar claw locations"}},{q="What happens after crafting the Thunderhorn Totem?",a="Once Mull carves and enchants the totem using the claws, he sends you directly to the tainted Thunderhorn Water Well to perform the cleansing ritual and drive out the poison.",al={"what next","after thunderhorn totem","totem follow up"},sp=1},{q="How does this relate to the previous cleansing at Winterhoof Well?",a="This is part of an ongoing tribal effort to preserve Mulgore's sacred waters. The Winterhoof Water Well was purified first, and now the contamination threatens the Thunderhorn well further north.",al={"winterhoof connection","previous totem quest"}},{q="Why don't the tauren simply use herbal remedies instead of animal claws?",a="Tauren ritual craft draws on distinct elemental and physical aspects of nature. The sharp purity of apex predators provides an active, contending spiritual force specifically required to cut through aggressive goblin industrial poisons.",al={"why not herbs","shamanic totem materials"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Defending Mulgore's aquifers from foreign exploitation is a sacred tribal duty, reflecting the Earth Mother's mandate to live in balance rather than plunder.",["class:Shaman"]="Totemic restoration relies on invoking the living spirits of the land to cleanse water corrupted by unnatural, careless enterprise.",["class:Hunter"]="Gathering the claws requires understanding the native stalking beasts of the plains, honoring the kill to preserve the ecosystem."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing","cougar claws","flatland cougar","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","prairie stalker","stalker claws","thunderhorn totem","water well"},rel={"quest:758","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:thunderhorn-water-well","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="7",faction="Horde",id=756},src={"Thunderhorn Totem (Classic)"}}
E["quest:757"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Strength",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Strength is the initial trial in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother undertaken by young tauren at Camp Narache. To demonstrate their bravery and defense of the tribe, initiates must cull encroaching Bristleback quilboar in Brambleblade Ravine and present their belts to Chief Hawkwind.",h="The Rite of Strength is the initial trial in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother undertaken by young...",sec={{t="The First Trial",b="For a young tauren seeking recognition from tribal elders and eventual acceptance in Thunder Bluff, completing the Rites of the Earthmother is an essential duty. Bestowed by Seer Graytongue, the Rite of Strength serves as the starting trial. It demands physical courage and decisive action in defense of Red Cloud Mesa, testing whether an initiate can face the tribe's mortal foes without flinching.",sp=0},{t="The Bristleback Threat",b="The chosen crucible for this trial is Brambleblade Ravine, a thorn-choked gash east of Camp Narache occupied by Bristleback quilboar. These aggressive interlopers continuously trespass on ancestral lands, ambushing hunting parties and raiding camp supplies under cover of darkness. Slaying them and claiming their belts serves as both proof of valor and a necessary measure to keep the settlement secure.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Rite of Strength?",a="It is the foundational step of the Rites of the Earthmother, designed to test a young tauren's bravery in combat. By eliminating hostile threats to the camp, an initiate earns the right to advance toward true respect in Thunder Bluff.",al={"why do rite of strength","rite of strength lore","purpose of rite of strength"}},{q="Who assigns and judges this trial?",a="Seer Graytongue delivers the charge to the initiate, outlining the trial's spiritual and practical requirements. Once twelve Bristleback belts are gathered, the proof of valor must be presented directly to Chief Hawkwind in Camp Narache.",al={"who gives rite of strength","who turns in rite of strength"}},{q="Where is Brambleblade Ravine located?",a="Brambleblade Ravine lies directly to the east of Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa. It is a thorny canyon swarming with hostile Bristleback quilboar.",al={"where is brambleblade ravine","brambleblade location"}},{q="Why are the Bristlebacks being targeted?",a="The Bristlebacks are encroaching upon the tribe's ancestral hunting grounds. They repeatedly stage ambushes against tauren hunters and steal provisions from Camp Narache in the dark, making them direct enemies of the tribe.",al={"why kill bristlebacks","what did the quilboar do"}},{q="Why collect belts instead of other trophies?",a="Belts serve as tangible tokens of defeated warriors, proving that the initiate faced the quilboar in close quarters and brought down adult combatants rather than merely scavenging from a distance.",al={"why bristleback belts","why belts"}},{q="What comes after completing the Rite of Strength?",a="Passing the Rite of Strength proves one's valor and qualifies the initiate for the subsequent trials of the Earthmother, continuing the formal path of adulthood and spiritual standing within the tribe.",al={"what is after rite of strength","next earthmother rite"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Every young tauren begins their journey through these ancient rites, demonstrating readiness to protect their people from the quilboar threat before leaving Red Cloud Mesa.",["faction:Horde"]="Defending the mesa secures one of the Horde's core recruiting grounds, ensuring young warriors and shamans can mature safely to aid the new union."},kw={"brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","earthmother","mulgore","quilboar","rite of strength","seer graytongue"},rel={"quest:763","npc:seer-graytongue","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","subzone:camp-narache","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Seer Graytongue",["end"]="Chief Hawkwind",level="4",faction="Horde",id=757},src={"Rite of Strength (Classic)"}}
E["quest:758"] = {t="quest",n="Thunderhorn Cleansing",z="mulgore",s="Thunderhorn Cleansing is an essential spiritual rite in Mulgore, charging travelers with purifying the corrupted Thunderhorn Water Well. Armed with a ritual totem prepared by Mull Thunderhorn, adventurers restore the contaminated spring vital to the local tribe. Successful purification earns the heartfelt approval of the Thunderhorn ancestors.",h="Thunderhorn Cleansing is an essential spiritual rite in Mulgore, charging travelers with purifying the...",sec={{t="A Tainted Lifeline",b="Water wells across the sweeping plains of Mulgore are vital lifelines for the tauren nomadic tribes who recently settled the grasslands. When foul poisons taint these underground springs, both wildlife and nearby settlements face devastating consequences. The Thunderhorn Water Well, situated north of Bloodhoof Village, fallen victim to contamination, requires deliberate spiritual intervention to heal the troubled land.",sp=0},{t="Ancestral Cleansing",b="To counteract the corruption, Mull Thunderhorn crafts a consecrated Cleansing Totem imbued with spiritual potency. Carrying this relic directly to the poisoned waters allows adventurers to channel ancestral power into the spring, dispersing the toxins and restoring pristine natural balance. Mull receives direct confirmation of the deed's success when the spirits of Thunderhorn ancestors visit him in a dream to express their enduring gratitude.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Thunderhorn Water Well located?",a="The Thunderhorn Water Well lies in the plains north of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. It serves as one of several vital watering holes supplying the local tauren clans.",al={"thunderhorn well location","where is well","where do i use totem"}},{q="Who initiates this cleansing ritual?",a="Mull Thunderhorn initiates the rite. He can be found in Bloodhoof Village, where he works to fashion specialized totems to heal Mulgore's distressed environment.",al={"who gives quest","mull thunderhorn location","quest giver"}},{q="Why must the water wells in Mulgore be cleansed?",a="The wells across Mulgore have fallen prey to unnatural poisoning and corruption, which threatens the delicate balance of the plains and the survival of the tauren tribes who depend on clean water.",al={"why cleanse wells","what happened to the water","why is well poisoned"}},{q="How does Mull know the ritual was successful?",a="Upon completing the rite at the well, the spirits of the Thunderhorn clan's ancestors appear directly to Mull in a dream, praising the adventurer's deeds and offering ancestral thanks.",al={"ancestor dream","how mull knows","well cleansed result"}},{q="What follows the cleansing of the Thunderhorn well?",a="Purifying the Thunderhorn well is part of a broader effort to safeguard Mulgore's springs. Next, Mull sends travelers onward to aid another troubled region by constructing the Wildmane Totem.",al={"what next","next quest after thunderhorn","wildmane totem next"}},{q="What is the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem?",a="The Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem is a ritually carved spiritual focus prepared by Mull Thunderhorn specifically designed to purify the corrupted waters of his clan's ancestral well.",al={"cleansing totem","what is the totem item","mull totem"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Restoring the sacred springs honors the Earth Mother and protects your kin as they carve out a permanent, peaceful home in Mulgore.",["class:Shaman"]="Channeling ancestral energy through totems to heal corrupted natural springs is the very foundation of the shamanic calling.",["class:Druid"]="Cleansing poisoned waters halts the spread of corruption across Mulgore's ecosystem, preserving the natural harmony of the plains.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting the tauren with their sacred springs strengthens the bond between the young Horde and its proud Shu'halo allies."},kw={"ancestral spirits","bloodhoof village","cleansing totem","earth mother","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","thunderhorn cleansing","thunderhorn water well"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:thunderhorn-water-well","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="8",faction="Horde",id=758},src={"Thunderhorn Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["quest:759"] = {t="quest",n="Wildmane Totem",z="mulgore",s="Wildmane Totem represents the third and final stage in Mull Thunderhorn's efforts to craft cleansing totems for Mulgore's poisoned wells. To restore the Wildmane Well, the ritual demands offerings taken from the territory's fiercest natural predators: prairie wolf alphas.",h="Wildmane Totem represents the third and final stage in Mull Thunderhorn's efforts to craft cleansing totems...",sec={{t="The Final Well",b="After securing the materials required to consecrate totems for the Winterhoof and Thunderhorn wells, only one afflicted source remains in central Mulgore: the Wildmane Well. The poisoned water across the plains threatens both migrating wildlife and settlements, demanding powerful primal ingredients to restore the earth's natural balance.",sp=0},{t="The Predator's Strength",b="To counter the potent corruption lingering in the northern waters, Mull Thunderhorn requires a sacrifice of ferocity. Prairie wolf alphas prowl the grasslands, particularly around the towering bluffs beneath Thunder Bluff. Harvesting their teeth channels the relentless drive and resilience of Mulgore's apex hunters directly into the crafting of the cleansing totem.",sp=0},{t="Sacrifice and Balance",b="In tauren spiritual tradition, drawing upon the land's creatures carries emotional and elemental weight. Mull views the gathering of alpha teeth not as conquest, but as a somber necessity where the sacrifice of Mulgore's predators helps heal the Earth Mother's stricken waters.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Wildmane Totem needed for?",a="The totem is crafted by Mull Thunderhorn to cleanse the Wildmane Well, the final contaminated water source endangering the tauren of Mulgore.",al={"why make wildmane totem","wildmane totem purpose"}},{q="Why are prairie wolf alpha teeth specifically required?",a="Purifying the deep contamination at the Wildmane Well requires channeling the fierce, unyielding strength of an apex predator. The teeth of the pack leaders provide the necessary primal power.",al={"why wolf teeth","prairie alpha teeth purpose"}},{q="Where are prairie wolf alphas found in Mulgore?",a="Prairie wolf alphas wander the northern plains of Mulgore, commonly prowling the grasslands surrounding the base of Thunder Bluff.",al={"prairie wolf alpha location","where to find prairie alphas"}},{q="Who crafts the totem?",a="Mull Thunderhorn, a spiritual elder stationed in Bloodhoof Village, crafts each cleansing totem after an initiate gathers the necessary offerings.",al={"who makes the totem","totem quest giver"}},{q="What follows the crafting of the Wildmane Totem?",a="Once Mull Thunderhorn assembles the totem from the alpha teeth, you are sent north with it to perform the actual purification at the Wildmane Well.",al={"what comes next","wildmane totem next quest"}},{q="How does this ritual reflect tauren beliefs?",a="The tauren view taking an animal's life as a solemn exchange with nature. Mull acknowledges the sacrifice of the wolves with both sadness for their death and pride in their role in healing the earth.",al={"tauren philosophy totem","mull thunderhorn beliefs"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The cleansing of ancestral wells underscores the tauren obligation to mend environmental imbalance and honor the sacrifices demanded by the Earth Mother.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman appreciate the delicate rites involved in harmonizing predator totems with elemental water to purge toxic blights.",["class:Hunter"]="Tracking down the cunning alpha wolves around the high bluffs tests a hunter's respect for the wilderness and its sharpest predators."},kw={"bloodhoof village","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","prairie wolf alpha","thunder bluff","totem","water well","wildmane"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="10",faction="Horde",id=759},src={"Wildmane Totem (Classic)"}}
E["quest:760"] = {t="quest",n="Wildmane Cleansing",z="mulgore",s="Wildmane Cleansing marks the culmination of the tauren ritual efforts to purify the vital watering holes of Mulgore. Entrusted by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village, heroes take the completed Wildmane Totem north to cleanse the final poisoned well. Driving back goblin pollutants restores balance to the plains and secures the pastoral livelihood of the tauren tribes.",h="Wildmane Cleansing marks the culmination of the tauren ritual efforts to purify the vital watering holes of...",sec={{t="Purifying the Earth Mother's Gift",b="Water is sacred lifeblood across the sun-drenched plains of Mulgore, sustaining the herds, the soil, and the nomadic tribes who have recently gathered around Thunder Bluff. When the Venture Company and encroaching goblins began contaminating these aquifers with toxic industrial runoff, the tauren elders recognized an existential threat. Following the purification of the Winterhoof and Thunderhorn wells, the Wildmane Water Well stands as the third and final corrupted site requiring spiritual intervention.",sp=0},{t="The Wildmane Totem",b="Crafted from specific elements gathered across the region, the Wildmane Cleansing Totem channels the restorative power of ancestral spirits and elemental earth. Planting the totem beside the foul waters north of Thunder Bluff disperses the foul chemicals, drawing out the toxins and reviving the natural flow. By completing the ritual, the tauren safeguard their people from disease and send an unmistakable message that the despoiling of their sacred ground will not be tolerated.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why was the Wildmane Water Well poisoned?",a="Goblin operations, driven by industrial greed and logging enterprises, introduced foul poisons and chemical waste into Mulgore's subterranean waters, threatening local wildlife and tribal settlements alike.",al={"who poisoned the well","why is the well dirty","goblin poison"}},{q="Where is the Wildmane Water Well located?",a="The Wildmane Water Well is situated on the plains directly north of the towering mesas of Thunder Bluff.",al={"wildmane well location","where is wildmane well","finding wildmane well"}},{q="Who initiates this cleansing ritual?",a="Mull Thunderhorn, an elder stationed in Bloodhoof Village, guides you through the creation of the totems and sends you to purify the wells.",al={"who gives wildmane cleansing","quest giver","mull thunderhorn location"}},{q="What other wells are part of this purification effort?",a="The Wildmane Well is the third in a trio of vital water sources, preceded by the Winterhoof Water Well and the Thunderhorn Water Well, both to the south.",al={"other wells","mulgore wells","winterhoof thunderhorn wells"}},{q="How does the cleansing totem work?",a="The totem is infused with ancestral magic and purified natural elements, channeling restorative spiritual energy into the water table to neutralize goblin pollutants.",al={"cleansing totem mechanic","how does totem clean water"}},{q="What does completing this ritual mean for Bloodhoof Village?",a="Cleansing the three wells guarantees safe drinking water for all tribes in central Mulgore and honors the tauren sacred duty to heal the Earth Mother from industrial exploitation.",al={"outcome of quest","why cleanse the wells","tauren lore importance"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Restoring Mulgore's three ancestral wells is a foundational rite of passage that proves a young brave's devotion to preserving the Earth Mother's gifts.",["class:Shaman"]="Employing ritual totems to undo mechanical pollution exemplifies the shamanic duty to maintain harmony between natural elements and living communities.",["class:Druid"]="Reversing goblin environmental destruction directly reflects the Cenarion mandate to heal blighted ecosystems and protect verdant pastures."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing","earth mother","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","totem","venture company","water well","wildmane"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","quest:759","subzone:wildmane-water-well","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:goblin"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="10",faction="Horde",id=760},src={"Wildmane Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["quest:761"] = {t="quest",n="Swoop Hunting",z="mulgore",s="Swoop Hunting is a rite of tracking and marksmanship offered by Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village. To prove their cleverness and patience, hunters and travelers are challenged to stalk the elusive birds across the rolling plains of Mulgore. Returning with their pristine trophy quills demonstrates both dedication to the hunt and respect for the natural trials of the Earth Mother.",h="Swoop Hunting is a rite of tracking and marksmanship offered by Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="A Test of Keen Eyes",b="The grassy steppes of Mulgore are teeming with beasts, but few test a pursuer's instincts like the native swoop. These large predatory birds blend seamlessly into the skyline and plains, wheeling overhead or skimming low across the tall grass. Unlike lumbering plainstriders or grazing kodo, swoops demand acute focus and swift feet to run down, making their pursuit a traditional proving exercise for younger hunters seeking to earn their standing among the tribes.",sp=0},{t="Badges of the Hunt",b="For Harken Windtotem, gathering trophy quills is less about culling wildlife and more about cultivating discipline. In tauren culture, carrying tokens from a hard-won stalk signifies cleverness and perseverance. Presenting these unblemished quills to elders in Bloodhoof Village marks an adventurer as capable of sustaining and defending the community, demonstrating that their hooves are swift and their senses sharp enough to master the wider world.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of hunting swoops for Harken Windtotem?",a="Harken Windtotem tasks hunters with tracking swoops across Mulgore to test their cleverness, speed, and dedication. Bringing back intact trophy quills serves as recognized proof of a hunter's skill and earns honor among the tauren of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"why hunt swoops","harken windtotem quest purpose","swoop hunting purpose"}},{q="Where are swoops typically encountered?",a="Swoops can be found scattered throughout the open plains of Mulgore, soaring above the prairies or scanning the grasses for prey outside settlements like Bloodhoof Village.",al={"where are swoops","swoop locations mulgore","find swoops"}},{q="Why are swoops considered tricky to hunt?",a="Unlike herd animals that move together across the flats, swoops are swift, alert raptors that remain widespread and observant. Stalking them requires sharp vision to spot their flight paths and quick strides to bring them down before they escape.",al={"why are swoops hard to catch","swoop behavior"}},{q="What do the trophy quills signify to the tauren?",a="Within tauren traditions, trophy swoop quills represent patience, sharp vision, and successful pursuit. Displaying them signifies that a hunter possesses the instincts necessary to provide for and protect the tribe.",al={"trophy swoop quill meaning","significance of quills"}},{q="Who is Harken Windtotem?",a="Harken Windtotem is a respected tauren resident of Bloodhoof Village who observes newcomers and aspiring hunters, evaluating their resourcefulness through traditional trials across the plains.",al={"who gives swoop hunting","harken windtotem lore"}},{q="Does this hunt impact the balance of nature in Mulgore?",a="The tauren approach hunting as a sacred dialogue with the Earth Mother rather than wanton slaughter. Gathering quills is a measured test of discipline that honors the cleverness of the prey while sustaining tribal balance.",al={"is swoop hunting overhunting","swoop balance mulgore"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren youths and wanderers, hunting the swift swoops of Mulgore is a venerable rite of passage that sharpens tracking instincts and upholds ancestral hunting customs.",["class:Hunter"]="The hunt offers an early masterclass in leading aerial quarry, assessing behavioral patterns, and executing precise strikes against vigilant predators.",["class:Druid"]="Tracking swoops across the windswept mesas brings druids into closer communion with the predatory birds that maintain the balance of Mulgore's skies."},kw={"bloodhoof village","harken windtotem","mulgore","swoop","swoop hunting","tauren trial","trophy swoop quill"},rel={"npc:harken-windtotem","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Harken Windtotem",faction="Horde",id=761},src={"Swoop Hunting"}}
E["quest:763"] = {t="quest",n="Rites of the Earthmother",z="mulgore",s="Rites of the Earthmother marks the transition of a young tauren out of the secluded Red Cloud Mesa and into the wider plains of Mulgore. Having proven your mettle in Camp Narache, Chief Hawkwind sends you north to Bloodhoof Village with a carved totem to continue the ancient trials under Baine Bloodhoof.",h="Rites of the Earthmother marks the transition of a young tauren out of the secluded Red Cloud Mesa and into...",sec={{t="Leaving the Mesa",b="For generations, young tauren have tested their strength within the safe cradle of Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. Completing the initial trials proves one has the discipline and resolve to survive, but the sacred Rites of the Earthmother require far more than surviving local wildlife. Chief Hawkwind recognizes when an initiate has outgrown the mesa's sheltered hunting grounds and must step into the broader community.",sp=0},{t="A Token for Baine",b="To bridge the journey between Narache and the central settlements, Chief Hawkwind provides a hand-carved totem as a mark of introduction. Bearing this emblem down the winding path to Bloodhoof Village, you present yourself to Baine Bloodhoof, the young chieftain of the village and son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. The totem serves as testament to your readiness for the rites of spirit and wisdom that lie ahead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this quest?",a="It serves as the rite of passage that guides you out of the starting area of Camp Narache and directs you toward Bloodhoof Village, connecting you to the next chapter of the tauren ceremonial trials.",al={"why do rites of the earthmother","what does this quest do","purpose of quest"}},{q="Who is Baine Bloodhoof?",a="Baine Bloodhoof is the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. He leads Bloodhoof Village, serving as a respected local chieftain who oversees the ongoing spiritual and martial testing of young tauren.",al={"who is baine","baine lore","son of cairne"}},{q="What is the Totem of Hawkwind?",a="It is a wooden totem hand-carved by Chief Hawkwind of Camp Narache. It acts as an official credential and seal of approval, proving to Baine Bloodhoof that you have earned Hawkwind's blessing to undertake further rites.",al={"totem of hawkwind","hawkwinds totem","carved totem"}},{q="What rites come after this?",a="Upon arriving in Bloodhoof Village and meeting with Baine, you are tasked with the Rite of Vision, which tests your spiritual attunement through sacred ancestral visions.",al={"next rites","what comes after","next quest"}},{q="Where is Bloodhoof Village located?",a="Bloodhoof Village lies north of Camp Narache, situated along the shores of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore. Travelers need only follow the main road down from the mesa and head north.",al={"where is bloodhoof village","how to find bloodhoof village","path to bloodhoof"}},{q="Why must the Earthmother rites be completed?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother are deeply sacred traditions among the tauren. Passing all stages of the rites demonstrates physical fortitude, spiritual connection, and wisdom, earning the initiate true recognition from the elders of Thunder Bluff.",al={"why earthmother rites","tauren trials purpose","rites of earthmother lore"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Every young tauren must walk the path of the Earthmother to prove their worth to the elders and find their place among the united tribes.",["faction:Horde"]="The traditional rites reinforce tribal unity across Mulgore, ensuring that new defenders of the Horde possess both warrior prowess and deep spiritual grounding."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","cairne bloodhoof","camp narache","chief hawkwind","mulgore","rites of the earthmother","totem of hawkwind"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","subzone:bloodhoof-village","npc:chief-hawkwind","npc:baine-bloodhoof","quest:767","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Chief Hawkwind",["end"]="Baine Bloodhoof",level="5",faction="Horde",id=763},src={"Rites of the Earthmother (2)"}}
E["quest:764"] = {t="quest",n="The Venture Co.",z="mulgore",s="Investigating the raided trade caravans of Mulgore uncovers an aggressive buildup of Venture Company goblins digging into the eastern hills. Having had their formal requests for mining permits flatly rejected, the company chose to quarry the Earth Mother's soil anyway, prompting Morin Cloudstalker to commission a lethal response.",h="Investigating the raided trade caravans of Mulgore uncovers an aggressive buildup of Venture Company goblins...",sec={{t="Industrial Intrusion",b="The tauren of Mulgore revere the land as an extension of the Earth Mother, cherishing its pristine plains and balanced wildlife. When the Venture Company approached the tribes seeking rights to blast and excavate the rolling hills for raw ore, their petitions were categorically denied. Undeterred by tribal decrees or territorial sovereignty, the relentless cartel sneaked heavy machinery, laborers, and overseers directly into Mulgore's eastern ridges to mine illicitly.",sp=0},{t="An Unmistakable Message",b="Tauren scouts like Morin Cloudstalker recognize that corporate greed cannot be reasoned with through diplomatic appeals once picks hit the stone. With evidence mounting that the company's presence is linked to local disruptions and attacks on trade routes, Morin dispatches Horde champions to cut down both the taskmasters cracking the whips and the laborers driving the drills. The bloody reprisal is intended to ensure the Venture Company calculates the cost of trespassing as far too steep.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is the Venture Company in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company established an illegal outpost and mine along the eastern hills of Mulgore to extract minerals and natural resources. After the tauren formally denied them official mining rights, the cartel ignored tribal authority and set up operations regardless.",al={"why are goblins in mulgore","venture co mulgore motive","what does venture co want"}},{q="Who gave the order to attack the miners?",a="Morin Cloudstalker, a tauren scout keeping watch over the southern road and trade caravans, issues the order. He views the cartel's refusal to accept tribal refusal as a direct violation that demands physical force rather than further talk.",al={"who gives this quest","morin cloudstalker role","quest giver"}},{q="Where is the Venture Company mine located?",a="The main Venture Company Mine sits in the foothills east of the Ravaged Caravan and Bloodhoof Village. A smaller gathering of company personnel can also be encountered at an outpost tucked into the bluffs northeast of Thunder Bluff's northern elevators.",al={"venture co mine location","where are the supervisors","where to find venture co"}},{q="Did the tauren ever try to negotiate with the goblins?",a="Yes. The Venture Company petitioned the tauren for formal mining rights in Mulgore. The tribal leaders refused to let them strip-mine their ancestral lands, but the goblins simply chose to dig without permission anyway.",al={"did tauren permit mining","mining rights mulgore","did goblins ask permission"}},{q="Are these goblins part of the Steamwheedle Cartel?",a="No. The Venture Company is an independent, notoriously predatory industrial cartel distinct from neutral trading groups like the Steamwheedle Cartel. They maintain hostile relations with local populations wherever they exploit resources.",al={"are these ratchet goblins","is venture co friendly","venture company faction"}},{q="How does this connect to the ravaged caravans?",a="Investigating the ravaged supply wagons along Mulgore's roads reveals tracks and signs leading directly toward the illicit Venture Company buildup, indicating that the cartel's encroachments are actively destabilizing trade across the plains.",al={"caravan connection","did venture co attack caravans","the ravaged caravan connection"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, the Earth Mother is sacred. The Venture Company's strip-mining is not just trespassing, but a violent desecration of their ancestral home that warrants an immediate, forceful eviction.",["faction:Horde"]="Defending Mulgore against commercial exploitation cements your dedication to protecting Cairne Bloodhoof's people as they work to secure their new homeland.",["profession:Mining"]="The Venture Company operates purely through brute-force extraction, cutting corners and destabilizing the land with no regard for the natural balance or safe mining craft."},kw={"goblin","mining rights","morin cloudstalker","mulgore","ravaged caravan","venture co","venture co mine","venture company"},rel={"subzone:venture-co-mine","quest:765","quest:749","npc:morin-cloudstalker","subzone:ravaged-caravan","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","topic:goblin"},m={start="Morin Cloudstalker",level="10",faction="Horde",id=764},src={"The Venture Co. (Classic)"}}
E["quest:765"] = {t="quest",n="Supervisor Fizsprocket",z="mulgore",s="Supervisor Fizsprocket is a high-ranking Venture Company overseer stationed within the mining tunnels of Mulgore. Charged by Morin Cloudstalker with putting an end to the goblin enterprise's calculated pillaging, Horde adventurers enter the mine to eliminate him and recover his detailed clipboard.",h="Supervisor Fizsprocket is a high-ranking Venture Company overseer stationed within the mining tunnels of...",sec={{t="A Plot Against the Plains",b="Documents recovered from a destroyed tauren caravan revealed that the Venture Company was not merely digging for ore—they were deliberately formulating schemes to drive the tauren from Mulgore entirely. By uprooting the locals, the rapacious corporation sought unchecked freedom to strip the rolling hills and deep subterranean veins of every natural resource.",sp=0},{t="Strike at the Foreman",b="Morin Cloudstalker, enraged by the corporate atrocities plotted against his people, directs champions into the Venture Company mine located just east of the ravaged trade wagons. Tucked deep within the right-hand tunnels surrounded by laborers and sub-bosses, Supervisor Fizsprocket manages operations. Striking down Fizsprocket and claiming his clipboard shatters local coordination and yields critical intelligence regarding the company's future designs.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Supervisor Fizsprocket?",a="Supervisor Fizsprocket is a Venture Company boss running operations inside the mining tunnels in eastern Mulgore. He oversees the workforce tearing into the earth and executes corporate strategies aimed at expanding their operations across the region.",al={"fizsprocket","who is fizsprocket","fizsprocket lore"}},{q="Why does Morin Cloudstalker want Fizsprocket dead?",a="Morin discovered documents on a raided caravan proving the Venture Company plotted to drive the tauren off their ancestral homeland for profit. Fizsprocket is the direct supervisor carrying out these calculated exploitation schemes.",al={"why kill fizsprocket","morin cloudstalker motivation"}},{q="Where is Supervisor Fizsprocket located?",a="He is located inside the Venture Company mine directly east of the Ravaged Caravan in Mulgore. Inside the mine, follow the far right tunnel to a chamber where he stands surrounded by workers and supervisors.",al={"where is fizsprocket","fizsprocket mine location","fizsprocket map"}},{q="What proof does Morin require of Fizsprocket's defeat?",a="Morin Cloudstalker requires Fizsprocket's personal clipboard, which contains ledgers and records that might reveal further Venture Company ambitions in Mulgore.",al={"proof of death","fizsprocket clipboard"}},{q="Why is the Venture Company in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company is an unscrupulous commercial cartel bent on stripping virgin territory of timber, minerals, and valuable subterranean wealth, completely disregarding indigenous inhabitants or environmental balance.",al={"venture co mulgore","why are goblins in mulgore"}},{q="Does killing Fizsprocket drive the Venture Company away completely?",a="No. While Fizsprocket's demise disrupts their local leadership and provides Morin Cloudstalker with vital notes on their plans, the Venture Company remains a persistent threat embedded in Mulgore's hills.",al={"is venture co gone","after fizsprocket dies"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Venture Company's open scheme to displace your tribe and plunder the sacred earth makes slaying Fizsprocket an act of vital territorial defense.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting the tauren against encroaching corporate pillagers solidifies the Horde's protection over newly claimed heartlands in Kalimdor."},kw={"clipboard","fizsprocket","mine","morin cloudstalker","mulgore","supervisor","venture co"},rel={"npc:morin-cloudstalker","quest:764","quest:749","subzone:ravaged-caravan","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Morin Cloudstalker",level="12",faction="Horde",id=765},src={"Supervisor Fizsprocket (Classic)"}}
E["quest:7662"] = {t="quest",n="New Kodo - Teal",z="mulgore",s="Offered by Harb Clawhoof at Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, this transaction allows seasoned riders to exchange an aging Teal Kodo for a great brown, gray or white kodo from the newer herds. It represents the careful breeding and husbandry practiced by tauren kodo handlers across the plains.",h="Offered by Harb Clawhoof at Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, this transaction allows seasoned riders to exchange...",sec={{t="A Change of Mount",b="In the grasslands of Mulgore, kodos are essential partners to the nomadic and sedentary life of the tauren tribes. Kodo breeders like Harb Clawhoof tend herds outside Bloodhoof Village, ensuring healthy stock for travelers, messengers, and warriors. This offer allows a rider carrying an aging Teal Kodo to exchange it directly for a great kodo from the freshly raised stock without sacrificing riding performance.",sp=0},{t="Herds of Bloodhoof Village",b="Harb Clawhoof maintains the corral where riders can inspect the magnificent kodos on display. Tauren respect their beasts of burden deeply, viewing them as living spirits of the plains rather than mere equipment. Exchanging an older beast back to the breeder ensures the animal remains cared for among the herd while providing active adventurers with a vibrant companion.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this quest?",a="It allows a rider who owns an aging Teal Kodo to exchange it with Harb Clawhoof for a newly bred great brown, gray or white kodo, granting an updated appearance while retaining equal speed.",al={"why exchange kodo","new kodo teal purpose","what does harb clawhoof do"}},{q="Where does this exchange take place?",a="Harb Clawhoof manages the kodo pens outside Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore, where he tends the corralled herds.",al={"where is harb clawhoof","kodo breeder location mulgore"}},{q="Is the new teal kodo faster than the old mount?",a="No. Harb Clawhoof explicitly states that the new great kodo will be just as fast as the old Teal Kodo you trade in, serving strictly as a visual replacement.",al={"is teal kodo faster","teal kodo mount speed"}},{q="Who can complete this trade?",a="Only Horde riders who already possess an aging eligible kodo and have learned the proper riding techniques can arrange this exchange with Harb Clawhoof.",al={"can alliance get teal kodo","who can do new kodo quest"}},{q="Can I preview the teal kodo before trading?",a="Yes. Harb encourages riders to examine the great kodos roaming his corral around the yard to see the coloration and look of the beasts before deciding.",al={"see teal kodo beforehand","where to see new kodos"}},{q="What happens to the old kodo?",a="The aging mount is returned to Harb Clawhoof's care to live out its days among the local herd in Mulgore rather than braving dangerous expeditions.",al={"where does aging kodo go","does my old kodo die"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the Shu'halo, kodos are sacred partners granted by the Earth Mother. Ensuring aging companions are retired responsibly into peaceful herds is a matter of profound cultural honor.",["faction:Horde"]="The sturdy kodos of Mulgore serve as heavy beasts of burden across Kalimdor, supporting Horde supply trains and champions alike."},kw={"bloodhoof village","harb clawhoof","kodo mount","mount exchange","mulgore","tauren mount","teal kodo"},rel={"npc:harb-clawhoof","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Harb Clawhoof",level="1",faction="Horde",id=7662},src={"New Kodo - Teal"}}
E["quest:7663"] = {t="quest",n="New Kodo - Green",z="mulgore",s="Offered by Harb Clawhoof at Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, this exchange allows seasoned riders to trade their aging Green Kodo for a fresh mount from the newer herd. It provides tauren and Horde champions an opportunity to ride a great brown, gray or white kodo across Kalimdor.",h="Offered by Harb Clawhoof at Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, this exchange allows seasoned riders to trade their...",sec={{t="A New Generation of Kodos",b="Tauren rely on the powerful kodo beasts of Mulgore for labor, travel, and warfare. Breeding and caring for these enormous creatures requires steady patience and deep respect for the Earthmother's gifts. At Bloodhoof Village, breeder Harb Clawhoof tends to the corral, overseeing fresh stock raised to endure long marches across Kalimdor's rugged plains.",sp=0},{t="The Exchange",b="Over time, beasts of burden grow weary, and older mounts may be retired in favor of younger, spirited animals. Harb Clawhoof offers seasoned riders the chance to bring forth their older green kodos and swap them for a newly trained great kodo, ensuring the rider remains mounted on a creature fit for the trials facing the Horde.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this trade?",a="Harb Clawhoof allows owners of an older Green Kodo to exchange it for a beast from the newly raised herd, a great brown, gray or white kodo, matching the speed and stamina of their previous mount while sporting a fresh appearance.",al={"why trade kodo","new kodo green exchange","kodo swap mulgore"}},{q="Who offers this exchange?",a="Harb Clawhoof, the kodo breeder and mount vendor stationed by the pens in Bloodhoof Village within Mulgore, manages the trade.",al={"who gives new kodo green","quest giver","harb clawhoof kodo"}},{q="Where does this trade take place?",a="The exchange takes place at the kodo pens in Bloodhoof Village, a tauren settlement located in central Mulgore, south of Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is harb clawhoof","bloodhoof village kodo","kodo corral location"}},{q="Why are kodos so important to the tauren?",a="Kodos are hardy, massive herbivores native to central Kalimdor. Their incredible endurance and broad frames make them ideal companions for the physically imposing tauren, who require exceptionally sturdy mounts for both war and nomadic travel.",al={"tauren mounts lore","kodo importance","why kodos"}},{q="Are the new kodos faster than the old ones?",a="No. Harb Clawhoof notes that the new kodos possess the exact same speed and endurance as the older beasts; the primary distinction is their refreshed vitality and the coloration of their hide.",al={"is green kodo faster","green kodo speed","mount speed difference"}},{q="Can members of other Horde races obtain this mount?",a="Tauren can acquire kodos naturally, while other members of the Horde must earn deep respect and exalted standing among the Thunder Bluff tauren before Harb Clawhoof will trust them with one of their prized beasts.",al={"orc ride kodo","troll green kodo","can undead get kodo"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Tending and riding kodos is an ancient tauren tradition, reflecting a lifelong bond with the spirits and wildlife of Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="Sturdy mounts are vital for coordinating defenses across the vast frontiers of the Barrens, Mulgore, and Durotar."},kw={"bloodhoof village","green kodo","harb clawhoof","kodo","mount","mulgore","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:harb-clawhoof","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Harb Clawhoof",level="1",faction="Horde",id=7663},src={"New Kodo - Green"}}
E["quest:767"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Vision",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Vision is a pivotal coming-of-age trial within the Rites of the Earthmother, testing a young adventurer's spiritual maturity. Initiated by Baine Bloodhoof, the quest directs aspirants to seek counsel from the village seer, Zarlman Two-Moons. Undertaking this journey is essential for anyone seeking the blessings and respect of the elders on the rises of Thunder Bluff.",h="The Rite of Vision is a pivotal coming-of-age trial within the Rites of the Earthmother, testing a young...",sec={{t="A Sacred Stepping Stone",b="For generations, the nomadic tauren have turned to the Earthmother to guide their people across the plains of Kalimdor. Now gathered within the safe borders of Mulgore, their spiritual traditions remain the cornerstone of their society. The Rite of Vision is one of the ancient trials known as the Rites of the Earthmother. While earlier trials test an initiate's physical strength and connection to the hunt, the Rite of Vision demands an inward journey, directing young tauren and honored allies to commune directly with the spirits.",sp=0},{t="The Seer's Guidance",b="Before any spiritual vision can be sought, an initiate must be prepared by someone steeped in ancient wisdom. In Bloodhoof Village, that responsibility falls to the revered shaman Zarlman Two-Moons. Entrusted by Baine Bloodhoof to prepare travelers, Zarlman possesses the keen sight to recognize those walking the path of the ancestors, offering the preliminary rites and sacred preparations necessary to pierce the veil of the physical world.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Rite of Vision?",a="The Rite of Vision is a sacred ritual within the Rites of the Earthmother. It is designed to broaden an adventurer's spiritual sight, allowing them to commune with ancestral spirits and demonstrate their spiritual readiness to the elders of Thunder Bluff.",al={"rite of vision quest","what is rite of vision","rite of vision lore"}},{q="Who starts this quest?",a="The quest is given by Baine Bloodhoof, the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. Baine watches over the settlement of Bloodhoof Village and directs worthy travelers toward the village's spiritual elders.",al={"who gives rite of vision","rite of vision quest giver"}},{q="Who is Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is a seasoned tauren shaman and the spiritual leader of Bloodhoof Village. He prepares travelers and initiates who seek to undergo sacred trials such as the Rite of Vision.",al={"who is zarlman","zarlman two moons lore"}},{q="Why are the Rites of the Earthmother important to the tauren?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother represent a traditional rite of passage for tauren coming of age. Successfully completing them demonstrates physical capability, spiritual attunement, and an enduring respect for the land, earning the honor of the high elders.",al={"earthmother rites meaning","why do rites of earthmother"}},{q="Can non-tauren undertake the Rite of Vision?",a="Yes. Horde allies who travel through Mulgore and earn the trust of the Bloodhoof tribe are permitted to walk the path of the Earthmother, showing respect to tauren tradition in exchange for the elders' favor.",al={"can orcs do rite of vision","non tauren earthmother"}},{q="Where does this trial take place?",a="The trial begins in Bloodhoof Village, a prominent tauren settlement situated near the banks of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore, south of Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is rite of vision","rite of vision location"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Rite of Vision is an essential cultural milestone for every young tauren, marking your transition into a trusted defender and spirit-walker of the tribes.",["class:Shaman"]="Seeking Zarlman Two-Moons and walking the path of visions resonates deeply with the shamanic duty to balance worldly affairs with the will of the ancestors.",["class:Druid"]="Communing with the Earthmother's visions directly reinforces the druidic calling to listen closely to the wisdom and whispers of the living world.",["faction:Horde"]="Undertaking these traditional sacred rites proves your loyalty and cultural reverence to the tauren elders who help guide the Horde."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","mulgore","rite of vision","rites of the earthmother","tauren trial","thunder bluff","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:763","npc:baine-bloodhoof","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Baine Bloodhoof",["end"]="Zarlman Two-Moons",level="6",faction="Horde",id=767},src={"Rite of Vision"}}
E["quest:770"] = {t="quest",n="The Demon Scarred Cloak",z="mulgore",s="The Demon Scarred Cloak is a ragged pelt recovered from the legendary white wolf Ghost Howl, who roams the grassy plains of Mulgore. Marked by an enduring wound inflicted during past clashes with the Burning Legion, the pelt serves as proof of the beast's release from torment. Bringing it to the elders of Camp Sungraze brings closure to the story of a tragic local legend.",h="The Demon Scarred Cloak is a ragged pelt recovered from the legendary white wolf Ghost Howl, who roams the...",sec={{t="A Relic of Fel Wounds",b="Ghost Howl is an elusive white wolf known across Mulgore for both his ferocity and his sorrowful howl. When slain, the beast leaves behind a pelt that is visibly scarred by foul demonic corruption. These lingering marks date back to the wolf's desperate battles against the invading demons of the Burning Legion, a confrontation that left Ghost Howl wounded in both flesh and spirit long after the Third War came to a close.",sp=0},{t="Peace for a Troubled Spirit",b="The tauren of Mulgore maintain a deep respect for the wild beasts of their homeland, revering them as children of the Earth Mother. Presenting the scarred cloak to Skorn Whitecloud at Camp Sungraze confirms the demise of the tormented predator. Skorn recognizes the immense skill required to track and defeat Ghost Howl, expressing the somber hope that in death, the wolf's tortured spirit has finally attained peace.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Demon Scarred Cloak?",a="It is the ragged hide harvested from Ghost Howl, a legendary white wolf in Mulgore. The pelt is visibly scarred by fel energies from an old battle against the Burning Legion.",al={"what is demon scarred cloak","demon scarred cloak item","ghost howl pelt"}},{q="Where did the demon scars on the cloak come from?",a="Ghost Howl earned his wounds during clashes against the Burning Legion when the demonic invaders pushed across Kalimdor during the Third War.",al={"why is ghost howl scarred","how did ghost howl get scarred","ghost howl legion"}},{q="Who do I bring the cloak to?",a="Bring the cloak to Skorn Whitecloud, an elder tauren hunter who resides at Camp Sungraze in northeastern Mulgore.",al={"where do i turn in demon scarred cloak","who wants demon scarred cloak","skorn whitecloud turn in"}},{q="Why does Skorn Whitecloud care about Ghost Howl?",a="Skorn Whitecloud is a retired hunter who recognizes Ghost Howl as a formidable beast that suffered deeply from demonic corruption. He respects the skill required to fell such a creature and hopes its spirit can finally rest.",al={"skorn whitecloud lore","why does skorn want ghost howl"}},{q="Where is Ghost Howl found?",a="Ghost Howl is a rare white wolf that roams the rolling plains of Mulgore, typically sighted in the northern or central open fields between Red Cloud Mesa and Thunder Bluff.",al={"where to find ghost howl","ghost howl location","ghost howl spawn"}},{q="Why do the tauren hope the wolf's spirit finds peace?",a="Tauren culture emphasizes harmony with nature and reverence for wild spirits. Because demonic corruption brought unending torment to Ghost Howl's life, his defeat is viewed as a merciful release back to the Earth Mother.",al={"tauren belief ghost howl","ghost howl spirit"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the Shu'halo, hunting Ghost Howl is not an act of cruelty or conquest, but a somber ritual to grant an agonized creature peace from the lingering taint of the Burning Legion.",["class:Hunter"]="Tracking down a lone, scarred alpha like Ghost Howl is an essential rite of passage, earning high praise and a master hunter's gift from the elder Skorn Whitecloud.",["class:Druid"]="Druids view the demon-scarred pelt as a grim reminder that the land and its wild inhabitants still bear unhealed wounds from the demonic invasion of Kalimdor."},kw={"burning legion","camp sungraze","demon scarred cloak","ghost howl","mulgore","skorn whitecloud","white wolf"},rel={"npc:skorn-whitecloud","zone:mulgore","topic:burning-legion"},m={start="Demon Scarred Cloak",["end"]="Skorn Whitecloud",faction="Horde",id=770},src={"The Demon Scarred Cloak"}}
E["quest:771"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Vision",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Vision is a foundational spiritual trial undergone by young tauren stepping beyond the Red Cloud Mesa into greater Mulgore. To pierce the veil and receive guidance from ancestral spirits, initiates must gather natural reagents to brew the Water of the Seers under the watchful eye of Zarlman Two-Moons in Bloodhoof Village.",h="The Rite of Vision is a foundational spiritual trial undergone by young tauren stepping beyond the Red Cloud...",sec={{t="The Reagents of the Land",b="Before any vision can be granted, the initiate must demonstrate an intimate understanding of the natural gifts found across Mulgore. Zarlman Two-Moons instructs the seeker to collect well stones found near the sacred communal water wells and ambercorn fallen beneath the roots of Mulgore's ancient trees. These natural elements are not merely physical objects; to the tauren, they hold the latent magic and lifeblood of the Earth Mother, ready to be channeled into ritual use.",sp=0},{t="Brewing the Water of the Seers",b="Once gathered, Zarlman combines the stones and ambercorn into the potent Water of the Seers. When consumed beside the village's tribal flame, this ceremonial draft clears the mind and summons an ethereal guide. Following this manifestation across the plains forms the true heart of the rite, testing the initiate's ability to walk between the physical world and the spiritual whispers of the land.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Rite of Vision?",a="The Rite of Vision is a spiritual test for tauren coming of age and seeking their path. By preparing and drinking the Water of the Seers, the initiate learns to commune with spirits and follow ancestral omens across the plains of Mulgore.",al={"what is rite of vision","why do rite of vision","purpose of rite of vision"}},{q="Who is Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is a tauren shaman and seer stationed in Bloodhoof Village. He oversees spiritual trials for young adventurers, preparing ritual draughts such as the Water of the Seers and interpreting the signs of the Earth Mother.",al={"who is zarlman","zarlman two moons role","questgiver zarlman"}},{q="Where are well stones and ambercorn found?",a="Well stones are gathered from the ground near the freshwater wells scattered across Mulgore. Ambercorn can be found scattered at the bases of the large, sprawling trees dotting the surrounding grassy landscape.",al={"where to find ambercorn","well stones location","reagents for rite of vision"}},{q="What happens after drinking the Water of the Seers?",a="Drinking the draft near the tribal fire opens the imbiber's spiritual sight, revealing a ghostly animal guide. The seeker must follow this spirit across the plains to uncover the next stage of their sacred journey.",al={"what happens next rite of vision","water of the seers effect","vision quest follow-up"},sp=1},{q="Why do the tauren revere water wells so deeply?",a="Water is the lifeblood of Mulgore and a precious gift of the Earth Mother. The wells serve as sacred communal centers that nourish both the land and its herds, making the stones gathered beside them spiritually charged.",al={"tauren water wells","why are wells sacred","mulgore water"}},{q="Does this quest lead to further rites?",a="Yes. This task is part of a multi-stage ceremony. Once Zarlman prepares the Water of the Seers, initiates must drink it, track the vision that appears, and present themselves to elder leaders across Mulgore.",al={"next quest in rite of vision","is rite of vision a chain","tauren rites chain"},sp=1}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren, the Rite of Vision is an essential cultural milestone, binding an emerging warrior, hunter, or mystic to the spiritual heritage of the Earth Mother and the ancestors.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman will recognize the preparation of the Water of the Seers as a traditional grounding ritual, attuning the spirit to read unseen elements before conversing with spirits directly.",["class:Druid"]="Druids hold deep reverence for the ambercorn and well stones, seeing them as direct manifestations of the flora and life-giving waterways that sustain Mulgore's balance."},kw={"ambercorn","bloodhoof village","earth mother","mulgore","rite of vision","water of the seers","well stones","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:767","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","quest:92472"},m={start="Zarlman Two-Moons",level="7",faction="Horde",id=771},src={"Rite of Vision (2)"}}
E["quest:772"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Vision",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Vision is the spiritual climax of an ancient trial undertaken by young tauren in Bloodhoof Village. By drinking the sacred Water of the Seers beside the tribal bonfire, initiates summon a spectral guide to lead them toward their destiny.",h="The Rite of Vision is the spiritual climax of an ancient trial undertaken by young tauren in Bloodhoof...",sec={{t="The Water of the Seers",b="After gathering the necessary ritual herbs and passing the initial trials of faith, an initiate is granted the Water of the Seers by the village shaman, Zarlman Two-Moons. This potent draught must be ingested before the roaring tribal fire at the heart of Bloodhoof Village. When consumed with a focused and respectful mind, the sacred waters expand the drinker's perception beyond physical sight, bridging the veil between the material plane and the spiritual realm.",sp=0},{t="Following the Ghost Wolf",b="Upon drinking the brew, the flames yield an ethereal apparition: an ancestral ghost wolf. The spirit does not speak or fight, but silently lopes northwest across the rolling plains of Mulgore. A true initiate must heed its quiet path, tracking its journey away from the village and deep into the rocky hills where the hermit Seer Wiserunner awaits in secluded meditation.",sp=0},{t="A Walk to Wisdom",b="Reaching Seer Wiserunner at the mouth of his mountain cavern signifies the successful completion of the vision. This journey across the grassy hills tests an initiate's patience, trust in ancestral guidance, and attunement to the Earth Mother. Passing this rite grants the young adventurer the right to undergo the ultimate test of adulthood: the Rite of Wisdom.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What happens when I drink the Water of the Seers?",a="When ingested near the tribal fire in Bloodhoof Village, the water opens your spiritual senses. Shortly after drinking, a ghostly spirit wolf appears in front of the flames to guide your path.",al={"how does water of seers work","drinking water of the seers","what does the water do"}},{q="Where does the spirit guide lead me?",a="The spectral wolf trots northwest out of Bloodhoof Village across the hills of Mulgore, leading straight to a rocky cave where Seer Wiserunner dwells.",al={"where does ghost wolf go","who does the wolf lead to","wolf destination"}},{q="Who is Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is a prominent tauren shaman in Bloodhoof Village tasked with preparing initiates for spiritual adulthood through ancestral rites and sacred concoctions.",al={"who gives the rite of vision","zarlman two moons role"}},{q="Who is Seer Wiserunner?",a="Seer Wiserunner is an elder tauren mystic who lives in contemplation within the cliffs northwest of Bloodhoof Village. Initiates who follow their spirit vision find him to finish the trial.",al={"who is wiserunner","seer in cave mulgore"}},{q="Why must the water be drunk near the tribal fire?",a="The tribal bonfire acts as a sacred anchor for Bloodhoof Village's ancestral spirits. The combination of its consecrated flames and the holy water allows the vision to bridge into the waking world.",al={"why tribal fire","bonfire rite of vision"}},{q="What rite comes after the Rite of Vision?",a="Upon completing the Rite of Vision with Seer Wiserunner, initiates are directed to undertake the Rite of Wisdom, the final spiritual trial of their early journey in Mulgore.",al={"next quest after rite of vision","what comes next"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, the Rite of Vision is a deeply sacred passage into adulthood, demonstrating communion with the ancestors and obedience to spiritual guidance.",["class:Shaman"]="Following an ancestral spirit wolf is an early lesson in walking between worlds, grounding a shaman's bond with the spirits that guide the Horde.",["class:Druid"]="The rite reinforces reverence for the natural world and spiritual humility before the guidance of animal forms."},kw={"bloodhoof village","ghost wolf","mulgore","rite of vision","seer wiserunner","tribal fire","water of the seers","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:767","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Zarlman Two-Moons",["end"]="Seer Wiserunner",level="7",faction="Horde",id=772},src={"Rite of Vision (3)"}}
E["quest:773"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Wisdom",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Wisdom is the concluding trial in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother undertaken by young adventurers in Mulgore. After purifying their sight through the Rite of Vision, petitioners travel to the sacred burial grounds of Red Rocks to commune with an Ancestral Spirit and receive the blessing of the dead.",h="The Rite of Wisdom is the concluding trial in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother undertaken by young...",sec={{t="The Trial of Red Rocks",b="Having cleared their perception through the Rite of Vision, young protectors are directed east of Thunder Bluff toward Red Rocks. This ancient burial ground holds the physical remains and lingering spirits of those who fought and died to carve out a peaceful existence in Mulgore. Passing the trial requires approaching the resting grounds with proper reverence and communion, facing whatever territorial threats roam the hills to seek the counsel of the honored dead.",sp=0},{t="Passing the Mantle",b="At the summit of Red Rocks, the communion with the Ancestral Spirit is not a test of martial strength, but of spiritual readiness and continuity. The spirits resting there represent the generation that fought alongside Cairne Bloodhoof to establish Thunder Bluff and safeguard the tribes. By receiving their blessing, the supplicant officially inherits the solemn duty of protecting the city, earning their place as a recognized adult and defender worthy of entering Thunder Bluff with pride.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Rite of Wisdom?",a="The Rite of Wisdom completes the Rites of the Earthmother, bridging the visionary communion of youth with the solemn responsibilities of adulthood. By gaining the blessing of the ancestral spirits, petitioners prove they respect the sacrifices of those who came before and are prepared to defend Thunder Bluff.",al={"why do rite of wisdom","what does rite of wisdom do","rite of wisdom purpose"}},{q="Who sends you on the Rite of Wisdom?",a="Seer Wiserunner, in his cave northwest of Bloodhoof Village, sends initiates on this trial once they have successfully completed the Rite of Vision and drunk from the Water of the Seers.",al={"who gives rite of wisdom","where do i start rite of wisdom"}},{q="Where do you find the Ancestral Spirit?",a="The Ancestral Spirit is located at Red Rocks, an ancient tauren burial ground nestled in the rocky bluffs directly east of Thunder Bluff.",al={"ancestral spirit location","where is the spirit","where to find ancestral spirit"}},{q="Why is the Rite of Vision required first?",a="Only those who have drunk from the Water of the Seers and had their spiritual sight cleansed are able to perceive and receive the blessing of the ancient spirits at Red Rocks.",al={"why do vision first","rite of vision prerequisite","water of seers requirement"}},{q="What happens after completing the Rite of Wisdom?",a="Having earned the approval of the ancestors, initiates are instructed to make their official journey into the high rises of Thunder Bluff.",al={"what next after rite of wisdom","next quest after rite of wisdom"}},{q="What creatures threaten the grounds of Red Rocks?",a="The sacred graveyard at Red Rocks has been encroached upon by aggressive Palemane gnolls, who lurk along the rocky trails leading up to the ancient burial site.",al={"enemies at red rocks","what guards red rocks","gnolls at red rocks"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the shu'halo, receiving the mantle from the ancestors at Red Rocks is the culminating moment of passage into full adulthood and acceptance among the tribal elders.",["class:Shaman"]="Communing directly with an Ancestral Spirit reinforces the core shamanic duty of honoring the dead and keeping faith with the spirits that watch over Mulgore.",["class:Druid"]="The rite demonstrates the balance between the living world and the eternal cycle overseen by the Earthmother, grounding young druids in their duty to the land."},kw={"ancestral spirit","mulgore","red rocks","rite of wisdom","rites of the earthmother","seer wiserunner","thunder bluff","water of the seers"},rel={"quest:767","quest:763","subzone:red-rocks","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","topic:gnoll"},m={start="Seer Wiserunner",["end"]="Ancestral Spirit",level="10",faction="Horde",id=773},src={"Rite of Wisdom (Classic)"}}
E["quest:775"] = {t="quest",n="Journey into Thunder Bluff",z="mulgore",s="Journey into Thunder Bluff is the culminating pilgrimage marking the end of a young hero's trials across Mulgore. Having completed the sacred Rites of the Earthmother under the guidance of the spirits, the adventurer is granted an audience with the High Chieftain of the tauren, Cairne Bloodhoof.",h="Journey into Thunder Bluff is the culminating pilgrimage marking the end of a young hero's trials across...",sec={{t="A Rite of Passage Concluded",b="Before any traveler or fledgling warrior can truly claim to have proven themselves in Mulgore, they must undergo the ancient Rites of the Earthmother. These trials test strength, vision, and wisdom across the rolling plains and ancestral sites of the land. Once the Ancestral Spirit acknowledges the final rite, the initiate is deemed worthy of entering the city of Thunder Bluff not as a mere visitor, but as an honored defender of the tauren people.",sp=0},{t="Ascending to the High Rise",b="The quest directs the traveler from the sacred ground where the spirits wander straight to the towering heights of Thunder Bluff. Perched atop the mesas, the city is a beacon of peace carved from red stone and supported by sweeping rope bridges and massive wooden elevators. There, on the High Rise, High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof awaits to personally greet those who have walked the path of the ancestors.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who sends you on this journey?",a="The quest is bestowed by an Ancestral Spirit after you finish the Rite of Wisdom, the final step in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"who gives quest","ancestral spirit quest","quest giver"}},{q="Where can I find Cairne Bloodhoof?",a="Cairne Bloodhoof resides on the High Rise, the central elevated mesa in Thunder Bluff overlooking the surrounding red plateaus and plains.",al={"cairne location","where is cairne","thunder bluff leader"}},{q="What is the significance of completing this quest?",a="It serves as the formal graduation from the starting adventures of Mulgore. Presenting yourself to Cairne marks you as fully recognized by the tauren leadership and ready to take on duties throughout the wider Horde.",al={"why visit cairne","rites meaning","significance of thunder bluff journey"}},{q="Do only tauren complete this journey?",a="While it is a deeply traditional tauren rite of passage, any member of the Horde who proves their dedication through the Rites of the Earthmother is granted the same honor and sent to meet the High Chieftain.",al={"can orcs do this","who can do journey to thunder bluff","races"}},{q="What happens after speaking to Cairne?",a="Cairne welcomes you to his home, acknowledges your deeds, and points you forward into the broader conflicts facing the Horde across Kalimdor.",al={"next quest","what after cairne","after journey into thunder bluff"}},{q="How do you reach the High Rise in Thunder Bluff?",a="Take the great mechanical elevators from the plains below up to the mesas, then cross the rope and timber bridges that lead toward the central High Rise platform where the Chieftain's lodge stands.",al={"getting to high rise","how to get to cairne","thunder bluff elevators"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For a young tauren, standing before Cairne Bloodhoof after completing the Earthmother's rites is the definitive threshold of adulthood and tribal honor.",["faction:Horde"]="The willingness of the tauren to welcome any allied race who completes their sacred traditions speaks to the mutual respect holding the young Horde together."},kw={"ancestral spirit","cairne bloodhoof","high rise","journey into thunder bluff","mulgore","rites of the earthmother","thunder bluff"},rel={"quest:763","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","zone:thunderbluff","subzone:high-rise","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Ancestral Spirit",["end"]="Cairne Bloodhoof",level="10",faction="Horde",id=775},src={"Journey into Thunder Bluff (Classic)"}}
E["quest:780"] = {t="quest",n="The Battleboars",z="mulgore",s="The Battleboars is a rite-of-passage hunting quest given by Grull Hawkwind in Camp Narache. Young tauren are tasked with culling the vicious war-trained boars of Brambleblade Ravine to defend tribal lands and feed the settlement's youth.",h="The Battleboars is a rite-of-passage hunting quest given by Grull Hawkwind in Camp Narache.",sec={{t="A Threat on the Border",b="To the east of Red Cloud Mesa lies Brambleblade Ravine, a thorn-choked scar held by the Bristleback quilboar. As tensions between the Shu'halo and the quilboar escalate into open war, the Bristlebacks have trained vicious battleboars to probe tauren boundaries and terrorize their traditional hunting grounds. Left unchecked, these aggressive beasts threaten not only game trails but the safety of Camp Narache itself.",sp=0},{t="Feeding the Tribe",b="For the tauren, hunting serves both defensive necessity and communal sustenance. Grull Hawkwind instructs initiates to bring back the snouts and flanks of the slain battleboars. Even beasts trained by quilboar hands are not wasted; their meat and gristle are harvested to prepare hearty stew for the tribe's youth, turning a quilboar weapon into nourishment for the next generation of tauren defenders.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are the battleboars attacking the mesa?",a="The battleboars have been deliberately bred and trained by the Bristleback quilboar inhabiting Brambleblade Ravine. The quilboar use them as shock animals to harass the tauren and encroach upon Red Cloud Mesa's tribal hunting grounds.",al={"battleboar aggression","why battleboars hostile","battleboars attacking"}},{q="Who sends players on this hunt?",a="Grull Hawkwind, a prominent hunter and elder stationed at Camp Narache, directs young tauren through the concluding trials of their early training.",al={"quest giver battleboars","who gives the battleboars quest","grull hawkwind role"}},{q="Where are the battleboars found?",a="They wander near and within Brambleblade Ravine, a rugged canyon located just east of Camp Narache on the eastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"battleboar location","where are battleboars","brambleblade ravine boars"}},{q="What happens to the meat collected from the boars?",a="The battleboar flanks and snouts are brought back to Camp Narache to be simmered into stew for the settlement's children and elders, honoring the tauren tradition of utilizing what they hunt.",al={"battleboar flanks use","what is boar meat for","boar stew"}},{q="How does this quest fit into the local quilboar conflict?",a="It serves as an early introduction to the active hostility between the tauren and the Bristleback tribe. Culling their war animals sends a direct message to the quilboar before open clashes erupt against the Bristlebacks themselves.",al={"tauren quilboar war","bristleback conflict","quilboar lore"}},{q="What quest precedes The Battleboars?",a="It is preceded by The Hunt Continues, an earlier task from Grull Hawkwind focused on learning the hunting grounds of Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"battleboars prerequisite","previous quest battleboars","hunt continues chain"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="This task embodies the tauren balance of defending the Earth Mother's territories while ensuring nothing taken in battle is wasted, turning enemies' war beasts into sustenance for the camp's calves.",["class:Hunter"]="Facing beasts trained for malice by the Bristlebacks teaches young hunters the sharp contrast between natural wildlife and animals twisted into weapons of war.",["profession:Cooking"]="The request for flanks and snouts highlights the fundamental tauren culinary ethos: coarse, tough meats can always be rendered down into hearty, sustaining stews."},kw={"battleboar","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","grull hawkwind","mulgore","quilboar","red cloud mesa"},rel={"npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","subzone:camp-narache","topic:quilboar","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Grull Hawkwind",level="4",faction="Horde",id=780},src={"The Battleboars"}}
E["quest:781"] = {t="quest",n="Attack on Camp Narache",z="mulgore",s="Attack on Camp Narache is a vital discovery made within the quilboar den of Brambleblade Ravine. A dirt-stained map reveals that the aggressive Bristleback tribe is actively plotting an organized siege against Camp Narache. Delivering these plans to Chief Hawkwind alerts the Red Cloud tauren in time to summon aid from Bloodhoof Village.",h="Attack on Camp Narache is a vital discovery made within the quilboar den of Brambleblade Ravine.",sec={{t="A Plot in the Brambles",b="While scouting deep inside the caverns of Brambleblade Ravine, young tauren adventurers uncover a crude, dirt-stained map laying on the rock floor. Upon closer inspection, the parchment reveals deliberate tactical markings made by the Bristleback quilboar. Rather than engaging in mere opportunistic skirmishes, the quilboar are coordinating a full-scale, prolonged siege against Camp Narache.",sp=0},{t="Warning the Chieftain",b="Carrying the attack plans back to Camp Narache provides Chief Hawkwind with crucial intelligence. Recognizing the dire threat posed to the relatively undefended camp and its young initiates, Hawkwind prepares to send word to Bloodhoof Village for reinforcements. The intercepted map transforms what could have been a catastrophic ambush into a prepared defense.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where are the Bristleback attack plans found?",a="The plans originate from a dirt-stained map located inside a cave in Brambleblade Ravine, just up a path to the right of Chief Sharptusk's hut.",al={"map location","where is dirt-stained map","find the plans"}},{q="Who receives the Bristleback attack plans?",a="The plans must be delivered directly to Chief Hawkwind at the center of Camp Narache.",al={"who to turn in to","who gets the map","quest ender"}},{q="Why are the Bristleback quilboar targeting Camp Narache?",a="The Bristleback tribe seeks to drive the tauren from the Red Cloud Mesa and reclaim the hunting grounds and sacred plains of Mulgore for their own kind.",al={"why attack camp narache","quilboar motives mulgore","bristleback siege"}},{q="How does Chief Hawkwind plan to counter the siege?",a="With early warning provided by the intercepted map, Chief Hawkwind intends to call upon their brethren in Bloodhoof Village to reinforce the mesa and repel the assault.",al={"hawkwind plan","reinforcements camp narache"}},{q="Does this quest require speaking to anyone before picking up the map?",a="No. The quest begins directly from the Dirt-stained Map lying on the cave floor in Brambleblade Ravine, turning into the battle plans once retrieved.",al={"quest starter","dirt stained map starter","how to start attack on camp narache"}},{q="What happens if Camp Narache falls?",a="Camp Narache is the primary haven for young tauren braves and rites of passage; losing it would compromise the security of Red Cloud Mesa and sever Mulgore's spiritual training grounds.",al={"stakes of camp narache","why defend narache"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren braves undertaking their earliest rites on the mesa, intercepting this map proves your loyalty to your tribe and protects your kin from a sudden massacre.",["faction:Horde"]="Preserving the tauren's foothold in Mulgore ensures the Horde retains its vital southern anchor in central Kalimdor."},kw={"attack plans","bloodhoof village","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","dirt-stained map","quilboar","red cloud mesa"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Dirt-stained Map",["end"]="Chief Hawkwind",level="4",faction="Horde",id=781},src={"Attack on Camp Narache"}}
E["quest:833"] = {t="quest",n="A Sacred Burial",z="mulgore",s="A Sacred Burial is a quest entrusted by Lorekeeper Raintotem, tasking Horde adventurers with protecting Red Rocks in eastern Mulgore. The sacred ancestral burial grounds have been overrun by marauding Bristleback quilboar who are defiling the graves of esteemed tauren warriors. Driving off these interlopers restores honor and peace to those who gave their lives founding and defending Thunder Bluff.",h="A Sacred Burial is a quest entrusted by Lorekeeper Raintotem, tasking Horde adventurers with protecting Red...",sec={{t="Hallowed Ground of the Shu'halo",b="Red Rocks serves as one of the most revered sanctuaries in all of Mulgore. Tucked into the crags beneath the eastern mountains, it is the final resting place reserved exclusively for the most valiant tauren. The heroes interred here are legendary warriors who shed blood to protect their tribes, uphold their chieftains, and carve out a permanent homeland atop the mesas of Thunder Bluff. To the Shu'halo, disturbing their rest is an affront not just to tradition, but to the Earth Mother herself.",sp=0},{t="The Bristleback Desecration",b="The nomadic quilboar of the Bristleback tribe have persistently contested tauren control over central Kalimdor, but their recent push into Red Rocks represents a grave escalation. Bands of Bristleback Interlopers have crept onto the holy grounds, ravaging the earth and disturbing the resting sites. With the guardian Lorekeeper Raintotem too aged to single-handedly drive the invaders away, young champions of the Horde must take up weapons to purge the site of the intruders and defend the honored dead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Red Rocks?",a="Red Rocks is a sacred ancestral cemetery located along the eastern mountains of Mulgore. It is reserved specifically for the most valiant tauren warriors, particularly those who died defending their tribes and founding Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is red rocks","red rocks mulgore","tauren burial ground"}},{q="Who is Lorekeeper Raintotem?",a="Lorekeeper Raintotem is an elder tauren tasked with tending and safeguarding the sacred grounds of Red Rocks. Recognizing that he is past his prime and cannot physically repel the invading Bristlebacks alone, he calls upon capable adventurers to defend the dead.",al={"raintotem","lorekeeper raintotem","who gives a sacred burial"}},{q="Why are the Bristleback quilboar at Red Rocks?",a="The Bristleback tribe continuously presses against tauren territory across Mulgore and the Barrens. Desperate for territory and resources, bands of interlopers have pushed into the crags of Red Rocks, desecrating the gravesites in the process.",al={"why are quilboar at red rocks","bristleback interlopers","quilboar invasion"}},{q="Why is this desecration so offensive to the tauren?",a="Tauren culture places immense reverence upon honoring their ancestors and living in balance with the spirits of the fallen. Allowing savage quilboar to ravage the earth and disturb the bones of heroes who died for Thunder Bluff is a deeply spiritual insult.",al={"tauren funerary customs","why tauren care about burial"}},{q="Where does this quest lead next?",a="A Sacred Burial is a standalone defensive task offered by Lorekeeper Raintotem. Once the Bristleback Interlopers are dealt with, the immediate desecration is halted, though other tasks at Red Rocks delve into the spiritual unrest within the bluffs.",al={"sacred burial follow up","what comes after sacred burial"}},{q="Who is buried at Red Rocks?",a="The site is reserved for champion tauren who fought for the survival of their tribes and chieftains, especially those whose sacrifices made the establishment and defense of Thunder Bluff possible.",al={"who is buried here","red rocks graves"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Defending Red Rocks is a matter of profound ancestral honor, ensuring that the warriors who made your people's unified homeland possible rest undisturbed.",["class:Shaman"]="The desecration of sacred grounds agitates the spirits of the honored ancestors, making your intervention necessary to restore balance to the earth.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting the tauren elders in securing their sacred monuments solidifies the Horde's pledge to defend Mulgore alongside their allies."},kw={"a sacred burial","ancestors","bristleback interloper","lorekeeper raintotem","mulgore","quilboar","red rocks","thunder bluff"},rel={"subzone:red-rocks","npc:lorekeeper-raintotem","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Lorekeeper Raintotem",faction="Horde",id=833},src={"A Sacred Burial"}}
E["quest:861"] = {t="quest",n="The Hunter's Way",z="mulgore",s="The Hunter's Way is a rite of passage offered to aspiring trackers in Mulgore by the elder Skorn Whitecloud. To prove their emerging skill and respect for the wild, wanderers must fell the cunning flatland prowlers and present their claws to Melor Stonehoof atop Thunder Bluff's Hunter Rise.",h="The Hunter's Way is a rite of passage offered to aspiring trackers in Mulgore by the elder Skorn Whitecloud.",sec={{t="A Tradition of the Plains",b="For the tauren, hunting is neither mere sport nor simple subsistence; it is considered one of their people's highest honors. It demands patience, awareness of the Earth Mother's balance, and reverence for the beasts that roam the rolling plains. Elder Skorn Whitecloud, resting in Mulgore after decades of tracking across Kalimdor, recognizes this restlessness in young adventurers. Rather than allowing them to wander aimlessly, he channels their ambition into a traditional trial of maturity and discipline.",sp=0},{t="The Stalkers of the Grasslands",b="To demonstrate that their tracking instincts are genuine, candidates are tasked with taking down Mulgore's flatland prowlers. These great cats are swift, stealthy, and cunning ambush predators that blend easily into the high grass of the plains. Gathering four sets of claws proves that a fledgling wanderer can spot a predator before becoming prey, turning the hunter's own ferocity back upon them.",sp=0},{t="Reaching Hunter Rise",b="Upon securing the trophies, the initiate ascends the soaring wooden towers and bridges leading up to Thunder Bluff. There, upon the Hunter Rise, Melor Stonehoof receives the claws. As a master tracker who values Skorn Whitecloud's ancient wisdom, Stonehoof accepts the offering as proof that the initiate possesses the right spirit to be guided further along the predatory paths of Kalimdor.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers the task to walk the Hunter's Way?",a="The quest is offered by Skorn Whitecloud, a venerable tauren plains-wanderer found resting in Mulgore who mentors young adventurers looking to master the wilderness.",al={"quest giver","who gives the quest","skorn whitecloud"}},{q="Where do I take the flatland prowler claws?",a="Take the claws to Melor Stonehoof, who resides on the Hunter Rise in the tauren capital of Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is melor stonehoof","turn in location","hunter rise"}},{q="Why are flatland prowler claws chosen as the tribute?",a="Flatland prowlers are stealthy and dangerous apex hunters of the Mulgore grasslands. Successfully tracking and defeating them proves that an initiate possesses the keen eyes, patience, and combat discipline necessary for advanced hunting.",al={"why flatland prowlers","prowler claws lore","what are prowlers"}},{q="Is this quest restricted only to the Hunter class?",a="No. Despite the name, Skorn Whitecloud offers this test of resolve to any young Horde adventurer who displays the hunger to wander and learn the traditions of the plains.",al={"hunters only?","can other classes do this","is it for hunters"}},{q="Where does the hunter's journey lead after speaking to Melor?",a="Once Melor Stonehoof recognizes your burgeoning talent, he begins directing your steps outward into the harsher territory of the Barrens, eventually sending you toward experienced trackers like Sergra Darkthorn.",al={"what comes next","next quest","where to go after melor"},sp=1},{q="What significance does hunting hold in tauren culture?",a="In tauren belief, hunting is among the highest honors. It requires living in direct harmony with the Earth Mother's creation, taking only what is needed, and showing profound respect to the quarry that sustains their tribes.",al={"tauren hunting lore","hunting honor tauren","why is hunting important to tauren"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, presenting prowler claws to Hunter Rise is a foundational rite of passage that connects them to generations of ancestors who hunted these same sweeping plains.",["class:Hunter"]="The trial directly tests the core principles of the wild—stalking an ambush predator before it strikes first and reading the subtle signs of the grasslands.",["faction:Horde"]="This rite serves as a bridge for allied Horde wanderers to understand tauren reverence for nature and earn their trust on the heights of Thunder Bluff."},kw={"flatland prowler","flatland prowler claws","hunter rise","melor stonehoof","mulgore","skorn whitecloud","the hunter's way","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:skorn-whitecloud","npc:melor-stonehoof","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren","quest:6065"},m={start="Skorn Whitecloud",["end"]="Melor Stonehoof",level="10",faction="Horde",id=861},src={"The Hunter's Way (Classic)"}}
E["quest:8673"] = {t="quest",n="Bloodhoof the Elder",z="mulgore",s="Bloodhoof the Elder is an encounter with one of the venerated ancestral spirits who manifests during the Lunar Festival. Located just outside Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, this honored spirit acknowledges the respect paid by wandering travelers and grants an ancestral token in return.",h="Bloodhoof the Elder is an encounter with one of the venerated ancestral spirits who manifests during the...",sec={{t="A Visit from the Ancestors",b="During the celebration of the Lunar Festival, the spirits of great heroes and elders from ages past return to Azeroth. Shrouded in celestial radiance, Elder Bloodhoof appears just north of Bloodhoof Village near the bridge. Approaching these venerated ancestors is an act of honoring past sacrifices, and in exchange, Elder Bloodhoof offers words of encouragement and a sacred Coin of Ancestry.",sp=0},{t="The Bloodhoof Legacy",b="Standing on the ancestral lands of Mulgore, Elder Bloodhoof serves as a bridge between the living tauren tribes and the memory of their forebears. For the tauren, honoring the dead is a pillar of spiritual life, reinforcing the harmony between the Earth Mother, the ancestors, and those currently walking the plains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find Elder Bloodhoof?",a="Elder Bloodhoof stands illuminated by a beam of celestial light just outside Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, near the bridge and Ahab Wheathoof.",al={"elder bloodhoof location","where is elder bloodhoof","bloodhoof elder spot"}},{q="What is the purpose of seeking out Elder Bloodhoof?",a="Travelers seek out Elder Bloodhoof during the Lunar Festival to pay their respects to the ancestors. In recognition of this honor, the spirit rewards visitors with a Coin of Ancestry and wisdom about neighboring spirits, such as Elder Wheathoof.",al={"why talk to elder bloodhoof","bloodhoof elder purpose","what does elder bloodhoof give"}},{q="Why is there a beam of light shining down on him?",a="The shaft of light marks the spirit's presence as an ancestral apparition manifested during the Lunar Festival, symbolizing a sacred connection to the skies and the spirits of the past.",al={"why is elder bloodhoof glowing","light on elder bloodhoof"}},{q="Can Alliance characters pay homage to Elder Bloodhoof?",a="The Lunar Festival ancestors honor all mortals who show reverence, regardless of faction. However, Alliance travelers must tread carefully, as Elder Bloodhoof is located right on the doorstep of Horde territory.",al={"can alliance do this quest","is elder bloodhoof horde only"}},{q="Who is Elder Wheathoof that this elder mentions?",a="Elder Wheathoof is another ancestral spirit honoring the festival, located elsewhere in Mulgore. Elder Bloodhoof can provide guidance on where to seek him out.",al={"elder wheathoof","who is wheathoof"}},{q="What do the Coins of Ancestry represent?",a="Coins of Ancestry represent tokens of favor from the honored spirits of Azeroth. They demonstrate that a traveler has shown respect to the past, and festival celebrants trade them for festive attire, schematics, and celebratory wares.",al={"what are coins of ancestry","lunar festival tokens"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Greeting Elder Bloodhoof is a sacred duty on home soil, honoring the legacy of the tribe that helped lead the tauren to their peaceful haven in Mulgore.",["class:Druid"]="Druids hold the spirits of the Lunar Festival in high regard, communing with the elders to strengthen their natural bond with the earth and stars.",["class:Shaman"]="For a shaman, conversing with manifest ancestors like Elder Bloodhoof directly reinforces the living communion between the mortal realm and ancestral spirits.",["faction:Alliance"]="Reaching Elder Bloodhoof requires slipping into the heart of Mulgore, making this a bold journey through hostile Horde plains."},kw={"ahab wheathoof","ancestor","bloodhoof village","coin of ancestry","elder bloodhoof","lunar festival","mulgore"},rel={"npc:elder-bloodhoof","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:light"},m={start="Elder Bloodhoof",id=8673},src={"Bloodhoof the Elder"}}
E["quest:96659"] = {t="quest",n="The Adventurer",z="mulgore",s="As young tauren prepare to depart their ancestral training grounds on Red Cloud Mesa, Chief Hawkwind asks them to perform a final favor. He entrusts them with a bundle of essential supplies meant for Kaga Wildhoof, a seasoned wanderer camped near Bloodhoof Village who once aided the chief in his own past.",h="As young tauren prepare to depart their ancestral training grounds on Red Cloud Mesa, Chief Hawkwind asks...",sec={{t="A Chieftain's Repayment",b="Before travelers descend the mesa path toward the broader plains of Mulgore, Chief Hawkwind pauses to address unfinished personal debts. Long before guiding new generations of hunters and shamans on Camp Narache, Hawkwind crossed paths with Kaga Wildhoof. Described by the chief as an accomplished adventurer, Wildhoof provided vital aid when he needed it most. Hearing that she has pitched camp just outside Bloodhoof Village, Hawkwind uses the departure of newly proven novices to send provisions to his old benefactor.",sp=0},{t="Leaving the Mesa",b="This errand marks a pivotal rite of passage for young Horde champions leaving Red Cloud Mesa. The trail down the plateau leads directly toward Bloodhoof Village, the cultural and logistical heart of central Mulgore. Carrying the supply bundle across the plains bridges the sheltered training life of the mesa with the wider, wilder struggles of the United Tauren Tribes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Kaga Wildhoof?",a="Kaga Wildhoof is a veteran tauren adventurer who has recently made camp just outside Bloodhoof Village. She holds a longstanding history of friendship with Chief Hawkwind, having aided him significantly in years past.",al={"kaga wildhoof","who is wildhoof","where is kaga"}},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind send these supplies?",a="Hawkwind views the departure of young champions heading down to Bloodhoof Village as the ideal opportunity to repay an old favor to Wildhoof, ensuring his fellow adventurer has fresh supplies for her travels.",al={"hawkwind debt","why send supplies","purpose of supply bundle"}},{q="Where is Kaga Wildhoof's camp located?",a="Her camp is situated on the plains just outside Bloodhoof Village, down the winding southern path from Red Cloud Mesa into central Mulgore.",al={"kaga camp location","find kaga","bloodhoof village camp"}},{q="When does this quest become available?",a="Chief Hawkwind offers the task once a traveler has proven their mettle on Red Cloud Mesa and is ready to venture out into the broader territories of Mulgore.",al={"when do i get the adventurer","hawkwind quest timing","mesa exit quest"}},{q="Does this quest take you away from Camp Narache?",a="Yes. Carrying the supplies serves as a direct prompt to leave Camp Narache behind and travel down the trail toward Bloodhoof Village.",al={"leaving red cloud mesa","journey to bloodhoof"}},{q="What is contained within the Supply Bundle?",a="The bundle consists of essential provisions gathered by the tribes of Camp Narache to sustain an adventurer surviving outside organized settlements.",al={"what are the supplies","supply bundle contents"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren stepping beyond Camp Narache for the first time, delivering Hawkwind's debt of honor to Kaga Wildhoof represents their initial service to their kin in the wider plains of Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="Chief Hawkwind ensures that traveling Horde allies leaving the starting mesa form early ties with the experienced wanderers and outposts of Mulgore."},kw={"bloodhoof village","camp narache","chief hawkwind","kaga wildhoof","mulgore","red cloud mesa","supply bundle","the adventurer"},rel={"npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Chief Hawkwind",["end"]="Kaga Wildhoof",level="6",faction="Horde",id=96659},src={"The Adventurer (Mulgore)"},fv=true}
E["subzone:bael-dun-digsite"] = {t="subzone",n="Bael'dun Digsite",z="mulgore",s="The Bael'dun Digsite is a dwarven excavation carved deep into the mountains along the western edge of Mulgore. Established by Prospector Khazgorm's team out of Bael Modan, the quarry is viewed by the local tauren as a direct desecration of their ancestral homeland and an insult to the Earth Mother.",h="The Bael'dun Digsite is a dwarven excavation carved deep into the mountains along the western edge of Mulgore.",sec={{t="Intrusion in the Hills",b="Perched on the mountain slopes of western Mulgore, the Bael'dun Digsite represents the southernmost reach of the dwarven Explorers' League expedition based out of Bael Modan in the southern Barrens. Unconcerned with territorial borders or the spiritual traditions of the newly settled tribes, dwarven prospectors arrived with picks, shovels, and blasting powder to unearth ancient Titan relics buried beneath the plains.",sp=0},{t="Defending the Earth Mother",b="The Bloodhoof tribe viewed this invasive quarrying as an outright assault on the Earth Mother. Baine Bloodhoof initially urged caution and attempted to warn the interlopers away, but the dwarves flatly refused to abandon their trenches. Concluding that diplomacy had failed, the tauren resolved to break the expedition's tools and forcibly expel the miners before their destructive digging tears further into Mulgore's sacred earth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are dwarves digging in Mulgore?",a="The prospectors belong to the dwarven expedition headquartered at Bael Modan in the southern Barrens. Driven by the Explorers' League's quest to uncover ancient artifacts and Titan heritage, they established this outpost wherever their instruments suggested relics might lie, disregarding tauren sovereignty.",al={"why are dwarves here","dwarves in mulgore","baeldun dwarves"}},{q="How does the Bloodhoof tribe respond to the digsite?",a="The tauren view the operation as an intolerable wound against the Earth Mother. After the dwarves refused early warnings to leave peacefully, Baine Bloodhoof and the village leaders sent warriors to destroy the miners' tools and drive the trespassers out of Mulgore.",al={"what do tauren think","baine reaction","tauren response to digsite"}},{q="What is the connection between Bael'dun and Bael Modan?",a="Bael'dun is a satellite digsite established directly from the fortress and primary excavation at Bael Modan, located just across the mountain ridge in the southern Barrens.",al={"bael modan link","where do these dwarves come from"}},{q="Why didn't the tauren simply negotiate with the expedition?",a="Baine Bloodhoof initially pursued diplomatic solutions and issued formal warnings to the expedition leaders. However, the dwarves treated Mulgore as unclaimed wilderness and completely ignored tauren spiritual concerns, leaving force as the tribe's only recourse.",al={"did tauren try peace","diplomacy with dwarves"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The intrusive scarring of Mulgore's western hills makes Bael'dun a direct insult to the Earth Mother, demanding decisive action to protect ancestral soil from foreign desecration.",["faction:Alliance"]="Though driven by an earnest thirst for historical discovery, the Bael'dun miners have overextended deep into sovereign Horde territory, sparking violent friction with the locals."},kw={"bael modan","bael'dun","baeldun","baine bloodhoof","digsite","dwarves","earth mother","explorers league","mulgore"},rel={"zone:mulgore","subzone:bael-modan","npc:baine-bloodhoof","topic:earth-mother","topic:tauren"},src={"Bael'dun Digsite"}}
E["subzone:bloodhoof-village"] = {t="subzone",n="Bloodhoof Village",z="mulgore",s="Bloodhoof Village is a central tauren settlement resting along the banks of Stonebull Lake in Mulgore. Named in honor of the Bloodhoof tribe, it serves as the first major gathering hub for young hunters and braves journeying out from Camp Narache toward Thunder Bluff. Led by Baine Bloodhoof, the village coordinates local defense, trade, and traditional rites across the plains.",h="Bloodhoof Village is a central tauren settlement resting along the banks of Stonebull Lake in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Heart of the Plains",b="Situated around the clear waters of Stonebull Lake, Bloodhoof Village is a bustling center of commerce, craft, and ceremony in central Mulgore. Young tauren departing their trials at Camp Narache arrive here to hone their skills before venturing onward to the great mesas of Thunder Bluff. It is also well-known across Kalimdor for its master drum makers and the patient breeders who tame wild kodo beasts for travel.",sp=0},{t="Leadership and Rites",b="Day-to-day governance and the defense of the region fall under Baine Bloodhoof, son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. Under his watchful eye, braves are dispatched to secure the grasslands against aggressive wildlife, quilboar encroachers, and the shadowy presence of the Venture Company. Alongside Baine, spiritual guides such as Zarlman Two-Moons tend to the inner life of the tribe, directing novices through sacred journeys like the Rite of Vision.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who leads Bloodhoof Village?",a="Baine Bloodhoof, son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, leads the settlement and directs its defenses against threats to the central plains.",al={"village leader","baine bloodhoof location","who is in charge of bloodhoof village"}},{q="What major rites are conducted here?",a="Spiritual advisors such as Zarlman Two-Moons guide young tauren through the traditional Rite of Vision, an essential spiritual journey on the path to adulthood.",al={"rite of vision","spiritual trials","zarlman two-moons quest"}},{q="What is the settlement known for producing?",a="Bloodhoof Village is famed for its uniquely crafted ceremony drums and is the primary hub where wild plains kodos are tamed and trained as mounts.",al={"tauren mounts","kodo taming","bloodhoof drums"}},{q="Where do travelers go after finishing their tasks here?",a="Once their deeds in central Mulgore are complete, travelers are generally directed northward to Thunder Bluff, the capital city of the tauren.",al={"after bloodhoof","where next after bloodhoof","next hub"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, entering Bloodhoof Village marks the first real step beyond youth into broader tribal responsibility and spiritual maturity.",["faction:Horde"]="This peaceful settlement serves as a critical stopover and logistics link between the southern plains of Mulgore and the Horde capital at Thunder Bluff."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","camp narache","kodo","mulgore","stonebull lake","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"npc:baine-bloodhoof","zone:mulgore","subzone:stonebull-lake","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:camp-narache","zone:thunderbluff","quest:767","topic:tauren"},src={"Bloodhoof Village"}}
E["subzone:brambleblade-ravine"] = {t="subzone",n="Brambleblade Ravine",z="mulgore",s="Brambleblade Ravine is a rugged canyon cutting into the northeastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa. It serves as a fortified den for the Bristleback quilboar tribe, who persistently raid the nearby tauren settlement of Camp Narache. Young tauren braves are sent here to prove their strength and protect their homeland from these fierce interlopers.",h="Brambleblade Ravine is a rugged canyon cutting into the northeastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="A Thorn in the Mesa's Side",b="Carved into the sun-scorched earth of Red Cloud Mesa, Brambleblade Ravine is choked with dense, thorny briars and quilboar encampments. The Bristleback tribe uses this natural fortress as a staging ground for raids against Camp Narache, threatening the safety of young tauren coming of age. Bristleback shamans and warriors, led by Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle, dwell deep within the ravine among trained battleboars. For the Shu'halo, striking into the heart of the canyon is a crucial rite of passage and a grim necessity to secure the plains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Brambleblade Ravine?",a="Brambleblade Ravine is a canyon situated on the northeastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. It is heavily inhabited by Bristleback quilboar and serves as their local stronghold.",al={"brambleblade ravine","what is brambleblade ravine","brambleblade mulgore"}},{q="Why are the Bristleback quilboar attacking from here?",a="The Bristlebacks are fiercely territorial and view the presence of the tauren as an encroachment upon lands they claim. From Brambleblade Ravine, they launch frequent raids on the nearby settlement of Camp Narache.",al={"why quilboar attacking","bristleback motives","quilboar camp narache"}},{q="Who leads the quilboar in Brambleblade Ravine?",a="The quilboar here are led by Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle, a formidable chieftain who resides in a large hut located towards the back of the ravine.",al={"brambleblade leader","quilboar boss mulgore","chief sharptusk thornmantle"}},{q="Why do young tauren travel to the ravine?",a="Tauren elders and chieftains at Camp Narache task fledgling braves with entering the ravine to push back the Bristleback threat, cull their numbers, and defeat their leadership to keep the mesa safe.",al={"tauren quests brambleblade","why go to brambleblade","camp narache quests"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren coming of age at Camp Narache, striking into Brambleblade Ravine is one of the very first tests of martial and spiritual endurance to defend the tribe.",["faction:Horde"]="Clearing out the aggressive Bristlebacks in Brambleblade Ravine helps solidify the Horde's early foothold across Mulgore."},kw={"brambleblade","bristleback","camp narache","mulgore","quilboar","ravine","red cloud mesa","thornmantle"},rel={"topic:quilboar","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore"},src={"Brambleblade Ravine"}}
E["subzone:camp-narache"] = {t="subzone",n="Camp Narache",z="mulgore",s="Camp Narache is a secluded village nestled upon Red Cloud Mesa in southern Mulgore. It serves as the ancestral home of the Hawkwind tribe and the proving ground where young tauren begin their training. Sheltered high above the plains, the camp maintains ancient traditions while constantly guarding against encroaching quilboar and lingering centaur threats.",h="Camp Narache is a secluded village nestled upon Red Cloud Mesa in southern Mulgore.",sec={{t="Heart of the Hawkwind Tribe",b="Situated at the northern edge of the elevated Red Cloud Mesa, Camp Narache is the primary settlement of the Hawkwind tribe. The village consists of several large hide lodges and pavilions, including a communal tent for wounded braves, an armory, a bakery tent, and a spirit lounge dedicated to meditation and ancestral communion. At the center of the camp, tribal elders and path trainers stand in a circle at the heart of the camp, where the venerable Greatmother Hawkwind still offers guidance, guiding newly initiated youth as they step into their roles as defenders of the Earth Mother.",sp=0},{t="A Proving Ground Under Threat",b="Though naturally fortified by the steep sheer cliffs of the mesa, Camp Narache faces persistent unrest. Bristleback quilboar from Brambleblade Ravine continuously encroach upon Hawkwind lands, while centaur scouts linger in the distance. Young hunters, shamans, druids, and warriors prove their mettle here by culling the aggressive local wildlife, protecting the village's water well, and turning back quilboar raiders before descending the grand path into the wider valleys of Mulgore.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache is the starting village for young tauren, located atop Red Cloud Mesa in southern Mulgore. It is the central encampment of the Hawkwind tribe, serving as a sanctuary where fledgling hunters, warriors, druids, and shamans master their crafts before traveling outward to Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff.",al={"what is camp narache","narache lore","tauren starting town"}},{q="Who leads the village?",a="Chief Hawkwind guides the tribe's braves and coordinates the defenses of the mesa, alongside the tribe's revered elder, Greatmother Hawkwind. The tribe's trainers and elders gather at the center of the camp to instruct young adventurers.",al={"who leads camp narache","camp narache chief","hawkwind leader"}},{q="What threats does the camp face?",a="The encampment is continually harassed by Bristleback quilboar operating out of Brambleblade Ravine to the east. In addition to feral battleboars and striders wandering the surrounding bluffs, the lingering danger of predatory centaur bands keeps the Hawkwind braves on constant alert.",al={"camp narache enemies","who attacks camp narache","mesa threats"}},{q="Can travelers easily leave the mesa?",a="Red Cloud Mesa is naturally bounded by sheer cliffs. The only way down into the greater plains of Mulgore is along a guarded mountain path leading north, watched over by Hawkwind braves to prevent hostile beasts and quilboar from ambushing travelers.",al={"leaving red cloud mesa","exit camp narache","path off the mesa"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, Camp Narache represents childhood's end—the sacred earth where you take your first rites of the hunt and attune yourself to the ancestral spirits under the watchful gaze of the Hawkwind elders.",["class:Shaman"]="Young shaman take their very first steps along the spiritual path here, undertaking trials to earn their Earth Totem and speak directly with the elementals of the mesa."},kw={"bristleback","greatmother hawkwind","hawkwind","mulgore","narache","red cloud mesa","spirit lounge","tauren"},rel={"zone:mulgore","npc:greatmother-hawkwind","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","topic:tauren","topic:centaur"},src={"Camp Narache"}}
E["subzone:golden-plains"] = {t="subzone",n="Golden Plains",z="mulgore",s="The Golden Plains form a vast, sun-drenched basin stretching southeast of Thunder Bluff in central Mulgore. Cherished by the tauren, these sweeping grasslands serve as prime hunting territory and a vital grazing habitat for wandering herds of kodo.",h="The Golden Plains form a vast, sun-drenched basin stretching southeast of Thunder Bluff in central Mulgore.",sec={{t="Heart of the Hunt",b="Spreading out beneath the towering mesas of Thunder Bluff, the Golden Plains represent the untamed heart of Mulgore. The shifting sea of golden and green grass offers an abundant sanctuary for local wildlife, making it one of the most treasured traditional hunting grounds of the nomadic tauren. Broad kodo herds roam across the rolling basin, while hunters share stories of legendary quarry, most notably the great kodo Arra'chea, who wanders quietly through the tall stalks.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are the Golden Plains?",a="The Golden Plains are a broad grassland basin located southeast of Thunder Bluff in Mulgore. They are a beloved cultural hunting ground for the tauren, defined by wide vistas and sprawling wildlife.",al={"golden plains mulgore","where are golden plains"}},{q="What animals roam the Golden Plains?",a="The basin is famous for its abundant herds of wild kodo, alongside other native creatures of Mulgore. The most famous beast said to wander these grasslands is the elusive and revered kodo named Arra'chea.",al={"golden plains beasts","kodos in golden plains"}},{q="Why is this area important to the tauren?",a="The plains serve as a vital source of game and hides, providing sustenance for the newly founded settlements across Mulgore and celebrating the tauren tradition of tracking and honoring their quarry.",al={"tauren golden plains","why tauren hunt here"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, the Golden Plains are an enduring symbol of their homeland's bounty, offering sacred hunting grounds beneath the shadow of Thunder Bluff.",["class:Hunter"]="The vast open expanse and roaming kodo herds make these plains a prime testing ground for trackers seeking legendary beasts like Arra'chea."},kw={"arra'chea","golden plains","hunting grounds","kodo","mulgore","thunder bluff"},rel={"zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren"},src={"Golden Plains"}}
E["subzone:great-gate"] = {t="subzone",n="Great Gate",z="mulgore",s="The Great Gate marks the fortified pass leading from the arid Barrens into the verdant tauren homeland of Mulgore. Anchored against sheer mountain ridges, this wooden bulwark ensures the security of the tribes and screens travelers entering their sacred plains.",h="The Great Gate marks the fortified pass leading from the arid Barrens into the verdant tauren homeland of...",sec={{t="Threshold of Mulgore",b="Carved through the towering peaks encircling Mulgore, this pass serves as the primary overland artery connecting the high grasslands to the wider world of the Barrens. Guarded vigilantly by tauren braves, the massive palisade wall and gatehouses keep aggressive quilboar tribes, centaur raiders, and hostile beast packs from wandering unchecked into the tribal pastures.",sp=0},{t="A Shield for the Tribes",b="Since the tauren unified under Cairne Bloodhoof and ended their long nomadic wandering across Kalimdor, maintaining secure borders has been essential. The Great Gate represents more than just a checkpoint; it symbolizes the hard-won peace and safety of the tauren nation, standing firm against whatever dangers stalk the dusty savannah beyond.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where does this gate lead?",a="The gate spans the main pass connecting Mulgore to the Barrens. Passing through the archway takes travelers directly from the dusty central savannah into the lush, grassy heartland of the tauren tribes.",al={"where does great gate go","great gate path","entrance to mulgore"}},{q="Who guards the Great Gate?",a="Braves and sentries of the tauren tribes stand watch over the gate and its adjacent ramparts. They monitor all traffic moving through the canyon to safeguard Thunder Bluff and the outlying villages from external threats.",al={"great gate defenders","who protects the gate","mulgore gate guards"}},{q="Why is Mulgore fortified here?",a="The mountains naturally enclose Mulgore on nearly all sides, leaving only narrow gaps like this pass. Fortifying this chokepoint provides an effective shield against hostile quilboar, raiders, and wild predators that inhabit the Barrens.",al={"why is there a wall","mulgore border defense","why build the gate"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The gate marks the sacred threshold of your homeland, serving as the front line of defense protecting elders, youth, and grazing herds from the perils of Kalimdor.",["faction:Horde"]="For travelers coming from Orgrimmar or the crossroads of the Barrens, the Great Gate is the official welcoming point into the sovereign territory of the tauren allies."},kw={"barrens border","great gate","mulgore gate","mulgore pass","tauren wall","thunder bluff entrance"},rel={"zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","topic:horde","topic:quilboar","subzone:camp-taurajo"},src={"Great Gate"}}
E["subzone:kodo-rock"] = {t="subzone",n="Kodo Rock",z="mulgore",s="Kodo Rock is a natural stone monument located in the plains of Red Cloud Mesa, between Camp Narache and Brambleblade Ravine. Standing quietly amid the grasses of Mulgore, it serves as an early pilgrimage site for young tauren shamans learning to commune with the spirits of the earth. Because of its modest silhouette against the rolling landscape, travelers must keep a keen eye out to spot it.",h="Kodo Rock is a natural stone monument located in the plains of Red Cloud Mesa, between Camp Narache and...",sec={{t="A Waypoint on the Mesa",b="Situated roughly halfway along the path connecting Camp Narache to the hostile quillboar territory of Brambleblade Ravine, Kodo Rock is marked by a patch of worn earth at its base. While it lacks the sheer grandeur of the towering bluffs surrounding Red Cloud Mesa, its distinct profile resembles the revered kodo beasts that wander the grasslands. For wandering tribesfolk, it acts as a quiet natural beacon in the windswept plains of Mulgore.",sp=0},{t="Spiritual Significance",b="To the Shu'halo, natural formations often carry spiritual weight. Kodo Rock holds special reverence for young tauren shamans beginning their spiritual walk. Novices are directed here on early rites of passage, using the solitary stone monument as a sacred grounds to commune with elemental forces and honor the natural world before venturing into more perilous reaches of the continent.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is Kodo Rock located?",a="Kodo Rock is situated on Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. You can find it roughly halfway along a straight path between Camp Narache and Brambleblade Ravine, marked on the ground by a small clearing of bare dirt.",al={"kodo rock location","where is kodo rock","find kodo rock"}},{q="Why is Kodo Rock hard to find?",a="The monument blends naturally into the rolling hills and grasses of the mesa. It is only clearly visible from a relatively short distance, requiring travelers to pay close attention to the terrain and watch for the patch of dirt beneath it.",al={"cant find kodo rock","kodo rock hidden"}},{q="Why is Kodo Rock important to tauren shamans?",a="Tauren shamans visit the rock during their earliest trials out of Camp Narache. It serves as a peaceful, sacred landmark for novices learning to attune their spirits to the land and the elemental forces of Mulgore.",al={"shaman quest kodo rock","why go to kodo rock"}},{q="What dangers are near Kodo Rock?",a="While the immediate patch around Kodo Rock is generally peaceful, heading further northeast leads directly toward Brambleblade Ravine, a canyon densely populated by aggressive Bristleback quilboar.",al={"is kodo rock safe","enemies near kodo rock"}}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="For novice tauren shamans beginning their spiritual path at Camp Narache, Kodo Rock is a vital landmark used to attune with the elemental world.",["race:Tauren"]="The stone formation serves as a familiar landmark across the plains of Red Cloud Mesa, reflecting the sacred bond between the Shu'halo and the beasts of Mulgore."},kw={"brambleblade ravine","camp narache","kodo rock","monument","mulgore","red cloud mesa","shaman trial","tauren shaman"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Kodo Rock"}}
E["subzone:palemane-rock"] = {t="subzone",n="Palemane Rock",z="mulgore",s="Palemane Rock is a cavern and campsite nestled into the western mountains of Mulgore. It serves as a primary stronghold for the aggressive Palemane gnoll tribe, posing a continual nuisance and threat to the nearby tauren of Bloodhoof Village.",h="Palemane Rock is a cavern and campsite nestled into the western mountains of Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Gnoll Stronghold",b="Situated along the western rim of the grassy plains of Mulgore, Palemane Rock consists of a sprawling open-air encampment fronting a deep, shadowed cavern carved into the mountainside. The Palemane gnolls have claimed both the cave interior and the immediate foothills outside as their territory. Hostile and territorial, these gnolls aggressively defend their den, attacking hunters, travelers, and scouts who wander too close to their rocky haven.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Palemane Rock?",a="Palemane Rock is a natural cave formation and entrenched gnoll settlement set into the western cliffs of Mulgore.",al={"palemane rock info","palemane cave","where is palemane rock"}},{q="Who lives at Palemane Rock?",a="The cavern and surrounding camp are held by gnolls belonging to the Palemane tribe. They aggressively defend their territory against outsiders, including the native tauren.",al={"who is here","palemane mobs","gnolls at palemane rock"}},{q="Why does Palemane Rock matter to the tauren?",a="The proximity of Palemane Rock to central Mulgore and Bloodhoof Village makes it an active nest of hostile raiders that tauren hunters and braves must continually suppress.",al={"why do tauren care","bloodhoof village gnolls","threat to mulgore"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Palemane gnolls entrenched in these western caves are a persistent thorn in the side of your people, threatening game and unwary wanderers across Mulgore's pastures.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing Mulgore's borders against local pests like the Palemane gnolls is essential for keeping the tauren homeland safe and stable."},kw={"bloodhoof","cave","gnoll","mulgore","palemane","rock"},rel={"zone:mulgore","topic:gnoll"},src={"Palemane Rock"}}
E["subzone:ravaged-caravan"] = {t="subzone",n="Ravaged Caravan",z="mulgore",s="The Ravaged Caravan is a smoldering wreck located on the northeastern shore of Stonebull Lake in Mulgore. Once a supply convoy bound for Supervisor Fizsprocket's mining operations, it was ambushed and destroyed by local tauren determined to halt goblin exploitation of their homeland.",h="The Ravaged Caravan is a smoldering wreck located on the northeastern shore of Stonebull Lake in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Smoke Over Stonebull Lake",b="Perched on a rise above Stonebull Lake, the Ravaged Caravan marks an early clash between the tauren of Bloodhoof Village and the encroaching Venture Company. Sent to deliver mining supplies, blueprints, and equipment to Supervisor Fizsprocket's dig site, the caravan was cut off and wrecked by tauren defenders before it could reach its destination. Despite the devastation, stubborn Venture Co. survivors still cling to the ruined wagons and smoldering crates, jealously guarding what little cargo remains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What happened to this caravan?",a="The caravan was intercepted and wrecked by local tauren who spotted the Venture Company moving deep into Mulgore. Seeking to stop the goblins from stripping the land, the tauren destroyed the convoy before its cargo could reach the nearby mine.",al={"why is the caravan burned","what happened here","ravaged caravan backstory"}},{q="Who owns the wrecked wagons?",a="The caravan belongs to the Venture Company, an unscrupulous goblin mining consortium attempting to establish unpermitted operations in the hills of Mulgore.",al={"whose caravan is this","who owns the caravan","venture company caravan"}},{q="Where was the caravan heading?",a="It was hauling tools, machinery, and supplies north toward Supervisor Fizsprocket, who leads the Venture Company's strip-mining operation at the Venture Co. Mine.",al={"caravan destination","where was it going","fizsprocket supplies"}},{q="Why are there still Venture Co. workers lingering at the wreck?",a="The surviving Venture Co. hirelings and workers are fiercely protective of company property. Rather than abandoning the loss, they remain entrenched around the charred wagons, attempting to salvage what little remains of their equipment.",al={"why are goblins still here","venture co survivors","who is guarding the wreck"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The charred wagons represent your people's immediate and decisive refusal to let foreign industrial syndicates despoil the sacred pastures of Mulgore.",["class:Druid"]="The black smoke choking the shore of Stonebull Lake serves as a stark reminder of the reckless damage goblin machinery inflicts on the natural balance."},kw={"mulgore","ravaged caravan","stonebull lake","supervisor fizsprocket","tauren","venture co","venture company","wagon"},rel={"quest:749","quest:764","quest:765","subzone:stonebull-lake","zone:mulgore","topic:venture-company","topic:tauren","topic:horde"},src={"Ravaged Caravan"}}
E["subzone:red-rocks"] = {t="subzone",n="Red Rocks",z="mulgore",s="Red Rocks is a towering sandstone bluff in northeastern Mulgore that serves as a sacred ancestral burial ground for the tauren. Here, fallen champions are commended to the Earth Mother through cleansing flame, and young braves travel to its quiet heights to seek the wisdom of their forebears.",h="Red Rocks is a towering sandstone bluff in northeastern Mulgore that serves as a sacred ancestral burial...",sec={{t="Hallowed Stones and Ancestral Pyres",b="Rising high above the plains of northeastern Mulgore, the crimson formations of Red Rocks mark a sacred threshold between the living world and the next. Tauren tradition reserves this solemn ground for their most valiant heroes—chieftains, defenders, and champions who gave their lives founding and protecting Thunder Bluff. Their remains are surrendered to the sky and soil through ceremonial pyres, returning their spirits to the Earth Mother and Sky Father. It is equally a place of spiritual beginning, where young tauren complete their rites of passage by honoring the ancestral graves and communing with the wind.",sp=0},{t="The Quilboar Desecration",b="The enduring peace of Red Rocks has been shattered by the Bristleback quilboar. Creeping out from their dens, these aggressive scavengers have infested the sandstone ledges and desecrated the ancestral burial pyres with filth. This violation deeply affronts the spiritual traditions of Thunder Bluff. Shaman and lorekeepers now summon aid to cleanse the crags, driving out the Bristlebacks and reclaiming the sanctity of their resting dead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of Red Rocks?",a="Red Rocks serves as the premier ancestral burial ground and ceremonial rite site for the tauren of Mulgore. Fallen heroes and honored braves are cremated here to return their spirits to the Earth Mother, and young tauren visit its heights to honor the dead during their trials of adulthood.",al={"why is red rocks important","red rocks purpose","what happens at red rocks"}},{q="Why are there quilboar at Red Rocks?",a="The Bristleback quilboar pushed out from their bramble burrows and overrun the plateau, defiling the burial mounds. Their intrusion is seen by the tauren as an intolerable sacrilege against the resting spirits of their ancestors.",al={"quilboar at red rocks","who is invading red rocks","why are bristlebacks here"}},{q="Who is buried at Red Rocks?",a="Burial at Red Rocks is an honor reserved for the most valiant tauren, including great warriors who fought alongside Cairne Bloodhoof to secure Mulgore and build Thunder Bluff, as well as heroes who died serving their tribes.",al={"who is cremated here","red rocks dead","whose graves are these"}},{q="What ceremonies are performed here?",a="Beyond funerary pyres commended to the Earth Mother and Sky Father, the bluff is a focal point for the Rite of Wisdom, where tauren braves leave offerings and listen for the guidance of ancestral spirits on the wind.",al={"rites at red rocks","tauren rituals red rocks"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For your people, Red Rocks is sacred soil. The presence of foul quilboar rooting among your honored ancestors' pyres is an open outrage that demands cleansing.",["class:Shaman"]="The wind carries the sorrow of unsettled spirits disturbed by quilboar defilement, calling on shamanic rites to restore elemental balance and ancestral peace."},kw={"bristleback","burial ground","earth mother","mulgore","quilboar","red rocks","rite of wisdom","thunder bluff"},rel={"zone:mulgore","npc:lorekeeper-raintotem","quest:833","quest:773","zone:thunderbluff","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","topic:tauren","topic:earth-mother"},src={"Red Rocks"}}
E["subzone:rolling-plains"] = {t="subzone",n="Rolling Plains",z="mulgore",s="The Rolling Plains span the southern expanse of Mulgore, stretching between Bloodhoof Village and the border of the Barrens. These windswept grasslands are home to thriving herds of wildlife, but they are also a point of perpetual vigilance due to prowling centaur raiders and aggressive harpies.",h="The Rolling Plains span the southern expanse of Mulgore, stretching between Bloodhoof Village and the border...",sec={{t="Heart of Southern Mulgore",b="Spanning south from Bloodhoof Village toward the natural earthen gates leading into the Barrens, the Rolling Plains embody the vast open ranges of the tauren homeland. The area teems with wild game essential to the tribes, including prairie wolves, kodo herds, flatland cougars, and swift tallstriders. For young hunters proving their worth to the elders, these sweeping savannahs serve as an indispensable proving ground where tracking, patience, and reverence for nature are put to the test.",sp=0},{t="Encroaching Dangers",b="Despite Mulgore's naturally fortified ring of bluffs, the Rolling Plains remain uniquely exposed to outside threats. It is the only frontier where aggressive centaur outriders have managed to push past defensive perimeters to harass passing travelers and hunting parties. Adding to the peril, a nest of predatory Windfury harpies roosts in the eastern crags, swooping down upon stray game and vulnerable nomadic camps.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What animals can be found on the Rolling Plains?",a="The grasslands support a rich variety of native fauna, including roaming herds of wild kodo, nimble tallstriders, prowling prairie wolves, and stalking flatland cougars. These beasts provide vital meat, hides, and sinew for the tribes of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"rolling plains wildlife","mobs rolling plains","animals mulgore plains"}},{q="Why are centaur found wandering this part of Mulgore?",a="The Rolling Plains border the eastern passes into the Barrens, making them the only region in Mulgore where centaur raiders have managed to breach the perimeter. Marauding outriders constantly scout the plains, probing tauren defenses and threatening isolated hunters.",al={"centaur in mulgore","why centaur rolling plains","centaur outriders"}},{q="What other hostile creatures threaten the plains?",a="Aside from roaming centaur scouts, a dangerous enclave of Windfury harpies nests along the eastern crags of the region, preying upon unwary travelers and harassing local wildlife.",al={"harpies rolling plains","dangers rolling plains"}},{q="Where do the Rolling Plains begin and end?",a="The plains cover much of southern Mulgore, extending southward from the perimeter of Bloodhoof Village all the way to the Great Gate and the mountain border adjoining the Barrens.",al={"rolling plains location","where are rolling plains","boundaries rolling plains"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Rolling Plains provide vital hunting grounds for the newly unified tribes, but the intrusion of ancient centaur enemies so close to Bloodhoof Village makes guarding this southern corridor a matter of tribal survival.",["class:Hunter"]="With ample herds of kodo, tallstriders, wolves, and cougars, the Rolling Plains offer an ideal wilderness for honing tracking skills, gathering provisions, and taming first animal companions."},kw={"bloodhoof village","centaur","harpies","kodo","mulgore","rolling plains","tallstrider","windfury"},rel={"zone:mulgore","subzone:bloodhoof-village","topic:centaur","zone:barrens"},src={"Rolling Plains"}}
E["subzone:stonebull-lake"] = {t="subzone",n="Stonebull Lake",z="mulgore",s="Stonebull Lake is a serene, horseshoe-shaped body of water wrapping around Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore. Fed by the pristine natural runoff of the plains, it provides vital fresh water, abundant fish, and peaceful shores for the young tauren coming of age under Cairne Bloodhoof's guidance.",h="Stonebull Lake is a serene, horseshoe-shaped body of water wrapping around Bloodhoof Village in central...",sec={{t="Waters of the Plain",b="Curving around Bloodhoof Village on three sides, Stonebull Lake defines the geography of central Mulgore. A wooden bridge crosses the lake's narrowest stretch, carrying the main dirt road between the settlement and the great mesa of Thunder Bluff to the north. Its calm waters are filled with native fish, making its banks a favored spot for resting hunters, weary travelers, and apprentice fishers learning their trade under the sweeping open sky.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is Stonebull Lake located?",a="Stonebull Lake is located in central Mulgore. It forms a horseshoe around Bloodhoof Village, flanking the settlement on three sides.",al={"stonebull lake location","where is stonebull lake","mulgore lake"}},{q="What is the bridge across the lake used for?",a="The bridge crosses the narrowest part of Stonebull Lake to carry the main road connecting Bloodhoof Village directly to Thunder Bluff in the north.",al={"stonebull bridge","bridge near bloodhoof","road over stonebull lake"}},{q="Why is Stonebull Lake important to the tauren?",a="The lake is an essential freshwater source and a vital fishing spot for the inhabitants of Bloodhoof Village. It also offers a quiet place of contemplation and gathering for young tauren braves training in central Mulgore.",al={"why does stonebull lake matter","purpose of stonebull lake"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren establishing their homes across Mulgore, Stonebull Lake provides gentle sustenance and a reminder of the Earth Mother's bounty near their capital."},kw={"bloodhoof village","fishing","lake","mulgore","stonebull lake","thunder bluff"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Stonebull Lake"}}
E["subzone:thunder-bluff"] = {t="subzone",n="Thunder Bluff",z="mulgore",s="Thunder Bluff is the high-perched capital city of the tauren, rising hundreds of feet above the rolling plains of Mulgore. Founded by Cairne Bloodhoof following the Third War, it serves as the united sanctuary and cultural heart for a people who roamed as nomads for generations.",h="Thunder Bluff is the high-perched capital city of the tauren, rising hundreds of feet above the rolling...",sec={{t="A Refuge Upon the Mesas",b="For countless generations, the tauren lived as nomadic wanderers across central Kalimdor, constantly beset by brutal centaur raiding parties. That era of flight ended during the Third War, when Cairne Bloodhoof forged a bond of honor with Warchief Thrall and the New Horde. Together, they drove back the centaur menace and claimed the fertile grasslands of Mulgore. Cairne gathered the scattered tribes and established Thunder Bluff atop a series of sweeping, natural stone mesas. Today, the city is connected by dizzying rope bridges and accessed by ingenious wooden elevators, offering both absolute defense and a bustling center of trade and spiritual assembly.",sp=0},{t="Unity and Gathering Mists",b="While Thunder Bluff stands as a beacon of harmony, its towering heights house diverse factions. Most tribes revere the wisdom of Cairne Bloodhoof, yet dissent quietly simmers among traditionalists such as Magatha Grimtotem and her kin on the Elder Rise. Deep beneath the city's surface lie the subterranean Pools of Vision, where spirit walkers and shaman peer into the ancestors' will. Despite underlying tribal rivalries, Thunder Bluff remains the true spiritual pillar of Kalimdor's Horde, welcoming hunters returning from the Barrens, traveling artisans, and druids communing with the Earth Mother.",sp=0}},faq={{q="How do travelers get up to Thunder Bluff?",a="Travelers reach the city via massive wooden rope-and-pulley elevators located on the southwestern and northeastern bluffs, or by taking flight directly to the city's wind rider roost atop the central mesa.",al={"thunder bluff elevator","how to get up to tb","entering thunder bluff"}},{q="Why did the tauren build their city on cliffs?",a="After enduring centuries of relentless centaur raids on open ground, Cairne Bloodhoof chose the towering stone mesas to grant his nomadic people an impregnable sanctuary overlooking the sacred grazing grounds of Mulgore.",al={"why mesas","why build on high bluffs","thunder bluff defense"}},{q="Who rules Thunder Bluff?",a="High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof leads Thunder Bluff. He united the scattered tribes and secured the peace of Mulgore, ruling with the counsel of tribal leaders and elders across the rises.",al={"leader of thunder bluff","who is the chief","cairne thunder bluff"}},{q="Are all tauren tribes loyal to Cairne?",a="While the vast majority revere Cairne's wisdom, some groups—most notably the Grimtotem tribe led by the elder crone Magatha—harbor doubts about the Horde alliance and seek their own course for the tauren people.",al={"grimtotem thunder bluff","tribes of thunder bluff","tauren political rivals"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, Thunder Bluff represents their first true permanent home after generations of wandering, holding their sacred totems, spirit lodges, and greatest chieftains.",["faction:Horde"]="Thunder Bluff provides the Horde with an essential bastion on southern Kalimdor, resting as a steady anchor of wisdom and resources alongside orcish Orgrimmar."},kw={"cairne bloodhoof","earth mother","grimtotem","mesas","mulgore","pools of vision","tauren","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:cairne-bloodhoof","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","subzone:pools-of-vision","npc:magatha-grimtotem","npc:baine-bloodhoof","topic:horde","zone:barrens"},src={"Thunder Bluff"}}
E["subzone:thunderhorn-water-well"] = {t="subzone",n="Thunderhorn Water Well",z="mulgore",s="Thunderhorn Water Well is a vital water source situated in the rolling plains of central Mulgore along the path between Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff. Like all the wells across the valley, it provides essential nourishment to the tribes and the herds roaming the grasslands. Keeping this water clean and defended is crucial to maintaining life in the tauren homeland.",h="Thunderhorn Water Well is a vital water source situated in the rolling plains of central Mulgore along the...",sec={{t="A Life-Giving Spring",b="Situated amid the sprawling grasslands of central Mulgore, the Thunderhorn Water Well taps directly into the pure aquifers feeding the valley. For the nomadic tauren and their newly settled villages, these communal wells represent life, sustenance, and the Earth Mother's bounty. Because water is precious across the sun-drenched plains, controlling the wells means controlling the land itself. Intruders such as the Venture Company have repeatedly eyed these sources, attempting to harness them for industrial exploitation and disrupt the fragile balance the tribes fight to preserve.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Thunderhorn Water Well located?",a="The well is located in central Mulgore, situated along the north-south road running between Bloodhoof Village and the bluffs of Thunder Bluff.",al={"thunderhorn well location","where is thunderhorn well","thunderhorn water well map"}},{q="Why is this water well important to the tauren?",a="The water wells of Mulgore tap into the pristine subterranean waters of the valley, sustaining the herds, pilgrims, and settlements. To the tauren, water is a sacred gift from the Earth Mother that must be safeguarded from outside corruption and greed.",al={"why is thunderhorn well important","tauren water wells meaning","why protect the wells"}},{q="Who has threatened the well?",a="Goblins of the Venture Company attempted to seize control of the well to monopolize the valley's water supply for their nearby mining and logging ventures, but local tauren fought back to reclaim and purify it.",al={"who attacked the well","venture co well","enemies at thunderhorn well"},sp=1}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, wells like Thunderhorn are vital lifelines gifted by the Earth Mother, requiring constant guardianship against foreign polluters and greedy trespassers.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman deeply revere the pure water springs of Mulgore, viewing the protection of water spirits and subterranean currents as a sacred duty."},kw={"bloodhoof","mulgore","thunder bluff","thunderhorn","venture company","water","well"},rel={"zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","subzone:bloodhoof-village","topic:tauren","topic:venture-company"},src={"Thunderhorn Water Well"}}
E["subzone:venture-co-mine"] = {t="subzone",n="Venture Co. Mine",z="mulgore",s="Tucked into the eastern cliffs of Mulgore, the Venture Co. Mine is an unauthorized industrial excavation run by greedy goblin opportunists. Despite being denied rights by the tauren, the Venture Company dug into the hills regardless, threatening the natural balance of the plains.",h="Tucked into the eastern cliffs of Mulgore, the Venture Co.",sec={{t="Unwanted Industry",b="When the Venture Company sought rights to strip Mulgore of its minerals, the tauren flatly refused. The corporate cartel ignored them, tunneling deep into the eastern mountain wall anyway under the direction of Supervisor Fizsprocket. Their sprawling complex features three entrances—a ground tunnel guarded by hired muscle, a sheer cliffside overlook, and an upper passage leading out to a small lumber camp. The encroaching machines, smoke, and slag heaps represent a blatant defilement of sacred land.",sp=0},{t="Expanding Ambitions",b="Mining was only the opening bid for the cartel. Internal documents show that Mogul Razdunk and the Venture Company board encouraged Fizsprocket to expand further into full-scale timber harvesting, promising advanced goblin shredders to clear the rolling hills. Although tauren Outrunners attacked and burned a corporate supply caravan near Stonebull Lake to stall their expansion, salvagers were immediately dispatched to recover the cargo. To halt the destruction, the tribes have turned toward direct retaliation against the mine's supervisors.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why are goblins mining in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company sought permission to extract minerals from Mulgore, but the tauren denied their request. Unwilling to respect native territorial claims or environmental balance, the cartel sneaked workers and machinery into the eastern cliffs to plunder the hills without authorization.",al={"why are there goblins here","venture co mulgore reason","who runs the mine"}},{q="Who is in charge of this mining site?",a="Supervisor Fizsprocket oversees all Venture Company operations in Mulgore from his post inside the main entrance. He takes orders directly from the company's executive board and Mogul Razdunk.",al={"mine boss","who leads the mine","supervisor fizsprocket"}},{q="What happened to the supply caravan by the lake?",a="Tauren Outrunners ambushed and set fire to a Venture Company supply caravan near the northern shore of Stonebull Lake to stop machinery from reaching the mine. The goblins swiftly dispatched salvage teams to sift through the charred wreckage and recover their goods.",al={"ravaged caravan story","stonebull lake caravan","who burned the caravan"}},{q="What else do the goblins plan to build here?",a="Beyond hollowing out the cliffs for ore, internal communiques reveal plans to construct a lumber mill and deploy shredders to clear-cut Mulgore's trees for additional corporate profit.",al={"venture co plans","goblin shredders mulgore","lumber camp plans"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The mine is a profound insult to the Earth Mother, bringing soot, noise, and reckless scarring to the ancestral valleys the tribes have sworn to safeguard.",["profession:Engineering"]="Past the mine tunnels and through the higher lumber clearing lurks Twizwick Sprocketgrind, a tinkerer willing to share mechanical trade secrets away from prying corporate eyes."},kw={"fizsprocket","goblins","mulgore","ravaged caravan","razdunk","shredders","stonebull lake","venture co mine"},rel={"topic:venture-company","quest:765","quest:764","subzone:ravaged-caravan","zone:mulgore","subzone:stonebull-lake","topic:tauren","topic:goblin"},src={"Venture Co. Mine"}}
E["subzone:wildmane-water-well"] = {t="subzone",n="Wildmane Water Well",z="mulgore",s="The Wildmane Water Well is the northernmost of the sacred water wells scattered across Mulgore, resting just north of the mesas of Thunder Bluff. Vital to the plains and their herds, it has become a contested flashpoint targeted by goblin opportunists seeking to choke the tauren's lifeblood.",h="The Wildmane Water Well is the northernmost of the sacred water wells scattered across Mulgore, resting just...",sec={{t="A Lifeblood Under Siege",b="For generations, the nomadic tauren have relied on sacred water wells dug across the golden plains of Mulgore to sustain their people and their wandering kodo herds. The Wildmane Well guards the northern expanse just beyond the shadow of Thunder Bluff. Its vital importance makes it an irresistible target for enemies of the tribes. The goblins of the Venture Company have fouled it with their poisons in a bid to control the regional water supply, and only a cleansing ritual with a totem crafted by the tauren can make its waters pure again.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Wildmane Water Well located?",a="The Wildmane Water Well is situated on the plains of Mulgore, directly north of the towering mesas of Thunder Bluff. It is the northernmost of the sacred wells that sustain the region.",al={"wildmane well location","where is wildmane well","wildmane water well map"}},{q="Why is the well important to the tauren?",a="Fresh water is life on the plains. The tauren hold the land's wells sacred as vital lifelines for their settlements, traveling hunters, and the great kodo herds roaming the plains of Mulgore.",al={"why do tauren care about the well","wildmane well sacred"}},{q="Who has tried to sabotage or control the well?",a="The goblins of the Venture Company have poisoned the well in an effort to control Mulgore's water supply, and the tauren must cleanse it with a sacred totem.",al={"who attacked the well","wildmane well enemies","who poisoned wildmane well"}},{q="Why are the Grimtotem tainting the water?",a="They are not. The poison fouling the Wildmane Water Well is the work of the goblins of the Venture Company, not the Grimtotem, who have not been exiled from Thunder Bluff.",al={"why grimtotem poison water","grimtotem well revenge"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The sacred wells of Mulgore are a gift from the Earth Mother. Defending the Wildmane Well from defilement and poison is a sacred duty for every young brave.",["class:Druid"]="The goblins' attempts to corrupt Mulgore's fresh water threaten the delicate balance of the plains, demanding druidic vigilance to cleanse and heal the land."},kw={"grimtotem","mulgore","sacred well","thunder bluff","venture company","water well","wildmane"},rel={"zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren","topic:grimtotem-tribe","topic:venture-company"},src={"Wildmane Water Well"}}
E["subzone:windfury-ridge"] = {t="subzone",n="Windfury Ridge",z="mulgore",s="Windfury Ridge is a rugged highland area flanking the pastures of Mulgore. Notorious for harboring vicious harpies alongside predatory wolves and cougars, it poses a constant hazard to travelers while offering prized feathers to tauren artisans.",h="Windfury Ridge is a rugged highland area flanking the pastures of Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Perilous Perch",b="Windfury Ridge rises sharply above Mulgore's serene plains, serving as the hunting grounds of the Windfury harpies. These vicious winged creatures nest high along the crags, readily swooping down upon any unwary wanderers who skirt the hills. The rocky terrain is further populated by prowling prairie wolves and wild mountain cougars, making the ridge one of the more treacherous borders of the tauren heartland. Despite the danger, the harpies are frequently hunted by brave tauren warriors; their vibrant, iridescent plumage is highly sought after across Thunder Bluff to adorn ceremonial garments for lavish feasts and wedding celebrations.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What makes Windfury Ridge dangerous?",a="The ridge is teeming with predatory cougars and prairie wolves, but its most persistent menace is the flock of aggressive harpies nesting along the heights. They actively dive from the crags to capture or butcher intruders who wander too close to their nesting sites.",al={"why is windfury ridge dangerous","hazards of windfury ridge","windfury ridge monsters"}},{q="Why do the tauren hunt the harpies on Windfury Ridge?",a="Beyond securing the borders from their predations, tauren hunt the Windfury harpies for their brilliant feathers. These colorful plumes are traditionally harvested and woven into ceremonial headdresses and decorations used during feasts and weddings.",al={"why kill windfury harpies","harpy feathers mulgore","hunting harpies"}},{q="Where is Windfury Ridge located?",a="Windfury Ridge lies along the elevated bluffs in the pastoral region of Mulgore, in the far north of Mulgore, beyond Thunder Bluff.",al={"windfury ridge location","where is windfury ridge","windfury ridge map"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The ridge is both a recurring nuisance to young hunters and a source of vital ceremonial ornaments used to honor marriages and seasonal gatherings.",["class:Hunter"]="The steep inclines and cunning predators provide an excellent early testing ground for tracking, beast taming, and range management."},kw={"cougars","feathers","harpies","mulgore","thunder bluff","windfury","windfury ridge"},rel={"zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren"},src={"Windfury Ridge"}}
E["subzone:winterhoof-water-well"] = {t="subzone",n="Winterhoof Water Well",z="mulgore",s="The Winterhoof Water Well is a vital fresh water source situated on the edge of the Rolling Plains in southern Mulgore. Crucial to the local wildlife and nomadic tauren tribes, the well has recently fallen under threat from the exploitative Venture Company, who seek to poison and control the region's water supply.",h="The Winterhoof Water Well is a vital fresh water source situated on the edge of the Rolling Plains in...",sec={{t="A Threatened Lifeline",b="In the arid stretches of central Kalimdor, water is life, and Mulgore's subterranean wells sustain both the herds of the plains and the encampments of the tauren. The Winterhoof Water Well, named for one of the respected tribes of the plains, taps into the deep aquifers beneath the Rolling Plains. Unfortunately, its strategic importance has drawn the avarice of the Venture Company. Rather than sharing the land or negotiating, the ruthless goblin cartel has attempted to poison the water supply to exert leverage over the surrounding territory.",sp=0},{t="Purification and Defense",b="To counter the industrial sabotage of the Venture Company, tauren elders and shamans have mobilized young braves across Mulgore. Mull Thunderhorn has crafted sacred totems designed to counter the industrial toxins and restore the spiritual and physical balance of the well. Cleansing the well is not merely an act of survival for the tauren, but a spiritual duty to ensure that the earth and its waters remain uncorrupted by foreign greed.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where is the Winterhoof Water Well located?",a="The well is located in southern Mulgore, right on the fringes of the Rolling Plains south of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"winterhoof well location","where is winterhoof well","find winterhoof water well"}},{q="Who poisoned the Winterhoof Water Well?",a="The Venture Company poisoned the well. The unscrupulous goblin trading organization attempted to foul the water supply in a bid to control the local resources and weaken the native tauren.",al={"who poisoned winterhoof well","why is the well poisoned","who ruined the water"}},{q="How can the poisoned well be cleansed?",a="The well is cleansed through a ritual utilizing an enchanted totem crafted by Mull Thunderhorn, which uses primal magic to purge the Venture Company's foul toxins from the water.",al={"cleanse winterhoof well","fix the well","winterhoof cleansing"}},{q="Why are water wells so sacred to the tauren?",a="Beyond providing necessary hydration for tribes and roaming herds, water sources in Mulgore are viewed as gifts from the Earth Mother. Corrupting them is considered both a direct physical threat and a severe spiritual affront.",al={"why do tauren care about wells","tauren well lore"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Defending and purifying the ancestral water wells is one of the first trials young braves face to safeguard the land from outside exploitation.",["class:Shaman"]="Cleansing the well requires elemental harmony, serving as a practical demonstration of using totems to heal wounded water spirits."},kw={"cleansing","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","rolling plains","totem","venture company","water well","winterhoof"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","quest:748","quest:754","quest:756","subzone:rolling-plains","zone:mulgore","topic:venture-company","topic:tauren"},src={"Winterhoof Water Well"}}
E["zone:mulgore"] = {t="zone",n="Mulgore",z="mulgore",s="Mulgore is the ancestral homeland of the tauren, a lush, sheltered valley of emerald grasslands, towering plateaus, and rolling plains nestled beneath the Stonetalon Mountains. Surrounded by high natural mountain walls, the realm was secured after Cairne Bloodhoof allied with Thrall's Horde to drive out the marauding centaur. Today, it serves as the spiritual and territorial heartland of the united tauren tribes, overseen from the great mesa city of Thunder Bluff.",h="Mulgore is the ancestral homeland of the tauren, a lush, sheltered valley of emerald grasslands, towering...",sec={{t="A Land Reclaimed",b="For generations, the tauren lived as nomads, perpetually hunted and displaced across the central plains by aggressive centaur warbands. Their fortune shifted when High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof encountered Thrall and his displaced orcs. Recognizing shared ideals of honor, shamanism, and strength, the tauren forged an alliance with the nascent Horde. With orcish assistance, Cairne broke the centaur's grip on the region and led his people into Mulgore. Here, they built permanent settlements for the first time in centuries, crowning the highest northern mesas with their capital, Thunder Bluff.",sp=0},{t="The Trials of the Earthmother",b="Reaching maturity in Mulgore requires answering the call of the Great Hunt and honoring the Earthmother. Young tauren begin their training on the southern plateau of Red Cloud Mesa at Camp Narache under Chief Hawkwind. As they descend into the Golden Plains and Bloodhoof Village, they undertake the Rites of the Earthmother, learning to commune with ancestral spirits, forage native herbs, and respect the herds of kodos and tallstriders that sustain their civilization.",sp=0},{t="Encroaching Shadows",b="Despite its tranquil appearance, Mulgore faces growing threats from all quarters. Displaced quilboar have entrenched themselves within Brambleblade Ravine and defiled the sacred burial grounds of Red Rocks. In the mountains, Windfury harpies prey upon wild game, while Palemane gnolls strike from hidden caves. More troubling still are external exploitations: the commercial goblins of the Venture Company have begun defiling the sacred water wells and quarrying the eastern hills, while dwarven explorers from Bael Modan have established the Bael'dun Digsite to unearth ancient titan artifacts against tauren protests.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why did the tauren settle permanently in Mulgore?",a="The tauren were historically a nomadic people who traveled Kalimdor while pursued by centaur marauders. After joining forces with Thrall's Horde, Cairne Bloodhoof gained the military strength needed to drive off the centaur and reclaim the ancestral valley of Mulgore. They constructed permanent settlements, including Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff, to establish a unified homeland where all tauren tribes could thrive in peace.",al={"why do tauren live here","tauren homeland history","why settle thunder bluff"}},{q="How do you leave Mulgore on foot?",a="Mulgore is completely ringed by impassable mountain walls except for a single southeastern pass. Following the main dirt highway east past Bloodhoof Village and Morin Cloudstalker's post leads through this mountain corridor directly into the arid plains of the Barrens.",al={"mulgore exit","how to get to barrens from mulgore","mulgore road out"}},{q="Who are the Venture Company and why are they here?",a="The Venture Company is an unscrupulous goblin-run commercial cartel seeking Kalimdor's natural riches. In Mulgore, they run a mining operation in the eastern mountains and have occupied the vital water wells—such as Thunderhorn, Wildmane, and Winterhoof—desecrating the land and threatening the regional water supply.",al={"venture co in mulgore","why are there goblins here","venture co mine"}},{q="Why are dwarves digging in tauren territory?",a="Dwarves from the Bael Modan expedition have established the Bael'dun Digsite in Mulgore's northern foothills. Driven by the Explorers' League to unearth relics of their Titan creators, they excavate tauren land without permission, ignoring tauren protests that such operations scar the Earthmother.",al={"baeldun digsite","why are dwarves in mulgore","bael dun"}},{q="What are the Rites of the Earthmother?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother are a sacred series of trials that every young tauren undertakes upon reaching adulthood. These tasks test hunting, tracking, vision communion, and knowledge of the natural balance, proving the young initiate's devotion to the land and the ancestral traditions of their people.",al={"rites of earthmother","tauren maturity quest","rite of the earthmother"}},{q="Who rules Mulgore?",a="High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof governs all tauren lands from the capital city of Thunder Bluff atop the northern mesas. His son, Baine Bloodhoof, governs Bloodhoof Village and manages the daily concerns of the lower plains under Cairne's wisdom.",al={"mulgore leader","who is the chief of mulgore","cairne bloodhoof leader"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="This valley is your ancestral home, reclaimed through sacrifice and unity. Preserving its wells and cleansing its sacred grounds are fundamental duties to the Earthmother.",["faction:Horde"]="Mulgore represents the heart of Horde spirituality and agriculture in central Kalimdor, bound to Thrall's people through a blood oath of mutual defense.",["class:Druid"]="The rolling plains and untouched groves serve as one of Kalimdor's purest natural sanctuaries, where tauren druids study balance and the gifts of nature.",["class:Hunter"]="The tradition of the Great Hunt thrives across these plains, where cougars, wolves, and wild kodo test the skill and patience of every tracker."},kw={"bael'dun","baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","cairne bloodhoof","camp narache","earthmother","kodo","mulgore","red cloud mesa","red rocks","thunder bluff","venture co"},rel={"zone:thunderbluff","zone:barrens","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","npc:baine-bloodhoof","subzone:bloodhoof-village","subzone:camp-narache","quest:763","subzone:bael-dun-digsite"},m={levels="1-10"},src={"Mulgore (Classic)","Mulgore"}}
